window.COURSE = {"lessons": [{"id": "00-how-sql-thinks", "title": "How SQL thinks", "minutes": 25, "summary": "One idea explains most early SQL errors: you write a query in one order, and the database runs it in another.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## A query describes a result\n\nIn R you write *steps*: take this, filter it, then summarise. In SQL you write *one statement* describing the result you want, and the database decides how to compute it. That's what \"declarative\" means, and it has practical consequences.\n\n```sql\nSELECT product, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY product\nORDER BY n_loans DESC\n```\n\nEvery query is built from the same clauses, always written in this order:\n\n```sqlstatic\nSELECT    -- which columns to return (and calculations)\nFROM      -- which table(s)\nWHERE     -- which rows to keep\nGROUP BY  -- how to collapse rows into groups\nHAVING    -- which groups to keep\nORDER BY  -- how to sort the result\nLIMIT     -- how many rows to return\n```\n\nOnly `SELECT` and `FROM` are required. The rest are optional, but when present they **must** appear in this order.\n\n## The order it's written is not the order it runs\n\nThe database evaluates the clauses in a different order:\n\n| Step | Clause | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `FROM` / `JOIN` | assemble the rows |\n| 2 | `WHERE` | drop rows that fail the condition |\n| 3 | `GROUP BY` | collapse rows into groups |\n| 4 | `HAVING` | drop groups that fail the condition |\n| 5 | `SELECT` | compute output columns and name them |\n| 6 | `ORDER BY` | sort |\n| 7 | `LIMIT` | keep the first *n* |\n\nRead a query starting from `FROM`, not from `SELECT`. That habit alone will make other people's SQL easier to follow."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "Your dplyr pipeline is already written in run order. The query above is:\n\n```r\nloans |>\n  filter(originated_date >= as.Date(\"2025-01-01\")) |>\n  group_by(product) |>\n  summarise(n_loans = n()) |>\n  arrange(desc(n_loans))\n```\n\nSQL puts the `summarise()` part (`SELECT`) at the top and everything else below it. When you translate, write the `FROM` and `WHERE` first, then go back up and fill in `SELECT`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "The order explains rules that otherwise seem arbitrary:\n\n- **A column alias made in `SELECT` doesn't exist yet in `WHERE`**, because `WHERE` runs first.\n- **It does exist in `ORDER BY`**, which runs after `SELECT`.\n- **You can't filter on `COUNT(*)` in `WHERE`.** Groups don't exist yet; that's what `HAVING` is for."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l0-alias", "sql": "SELECT loan_id, principal * apr AS yearly_interest\nFROM loans\nWHERE yearly_interest > 1000", "explain": "`WHERE` runs before `SELECT`, so the alias isn't defined yet: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server and BigQuery all reject it. A few engines are lenient: DuckDB (this course's engine) and Snowflake accept it, so running it here works. **Don't rely on that in an interview.** Repeat the expression instead:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE principal * apr > 1000\n```\nOr compute it in a subquery or CTE first (lesson 5).", "options": [{"text": "It returns loans with more than 1,000 of yearly interest", "correct": false}, {"text": "It fails: `yearly_interest` doesn't exist yet when `WHERE` runs", "correct": true}, {"text": "It returns every loan, because the alias is ignored", "correct": false}, {"text": "It returns no rows", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "In PostgreSQL, MySQL or SQL Server, what happens when you run this?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Tables have no row order\n\nA data frame has row 1, row 2, row 3. A SQL table doesn't. Without `ORDER BY`, the database may return rows in any order, and the order can change between runs. If order matters, say so. If you use `LIMIT`, you almost always need `ORDER BY` too, or \"the top 10\" is just \"some 10\".\n\n## Grain: what is one row?\n\nThe most useful question in SQL, and the one interviewers are really testing, is **what does one row represent?**\n\n- `loans`: one row per loan.\n- `installments`: one row per *scheduled payment*. A 12-month loan has 12 rows.\n- `payments`: one row per payment *attempt*, including failures and (as you'll discover) some duplicates.\n\nThe result of a query has a grain too. `GROUP BY product` produces one row per product. A join between loans and installments produces one row per installment, not per loan. Most wrong answers in SQL interviews are grain mistakes: counting installments when you meant loans, or summing a loan's principal once per payment."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "Say the grain out loud before you write anything: \"So I want one row per merchant, with the number of loans and the total principal.\" It catches mistakes early and shows the interviewer how you think."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l0-grain", "sql": "SELECT borrower_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY borrower_id", "explain": "`FROM loans` means only borrowers who appear in `loans` can show up. Borrowers with no loans are invisible, because they aren't in the table being grouped. To include them you'd start from `borrowers` and join (lesson 3).", "options": [{"text": "One row per loan", "correct": false}, {"text": "One row per borrower in the `borrowers` table", "correct": false}, {"text": "One row per borrower who has at least one loan", "correct": true}, {"text": "One row per person (de-duplicating people with two accounts)", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What is the grain of this query's result?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Your first queries\n\nThe exercises below check your answer against a reference result. Click **Check answer** (or press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>). Use **Run** freely while you work things out."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l0-first", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "List the merchants onboarded on or after 1 January 2025. Return `merchant_name`, `category` and `onboarded_date`.\n\nWrite date literals as `DATE '2025-01-01'`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT merchant_name, category, onboarded_date\nFROM merchants\nWHERE onboarded_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start with `FROM merchants`, then a `WHERE` on `onboarded_date`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l0-translate", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Translate this dplyr pipeline into SQL:\n\n```r\nloans |>\n  filter(product == \"monthly\") |>\n  select(loan_id, principal, term_months) |>\n  arrange(desc(principal)) |>\n  head(5)\n```\n\nStrings in SQL use **single quotes**: `'monthly'`. Double quotes mean an identifier (a column or table name) in standard SQL.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT loan_id, principal, term_months\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly'\nORDER BY principal DESC\nLIMIT 5", "alts": [], "hints": ["`filter()` → `WHERE`, `select()` → `SELECT`, `arrange(desc())` → `ORDER BY ... DESC`, `head(5)` → `LIMIT 5`.", "Equality in SQL is a single `=`, not `==`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "", "text": "`LIMIT 5` works in PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, BigQuery, DuckDB and SQLite. SQL Server uses `SELECT TOP 5 ...`. The standard (and Oracle 12c+) form is `FETCH FIRST 5 ROWS ONLY` after `ORDER BY`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- SQL is written `SELECT → FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → ORDER BY → LIMIT` but runs `FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY → LIMIT`.\n- Read queries from `FROM` down.\n- Tables have no row order; use `ORDER BY` whenever order matters.\n- Before writing a query, state its grain: what one row of the result represents."}]}, {"id": "01-filtering-and-null", "title": "Filtering, CASE and NULL", "minutes": 35, "summary": "A quick refresher on SELECT and WHERE, then the part that trips everyone up — how NULL behaves.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## SELECT and WHERE, briskly\n\nYou remember this part, so here's the whole toolkit in one place:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  principal * apr AS yearly_interest,   -- calculated column with an alias\n  UPPER(product) AS product_label\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly'\n  AND principal BETWEEN 1000 AND 2000   -- inclusive at both ends\n  AND status IN ('late', 'charged_off')\nORDER BY principal DESC\n```\n\n| Operator | Means | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `=`, `<>` | equal, not equal | `!=` also works almost everywhere |\n| `IN ('a', 'b')` | any of these | |\n| `BETWEEN a AND b` | `>= a AND <= b` | **inclusive**, a trap with timestamps (lesson 8) |\n| `LIKE 'Blue%'` | pattern: `%` any text, `_` one character | case-sensitive in PostgreSQL, Snowflake and DuckDB; usually not in MySQL or SQL Server |\n| `IS NULL`, `IS NOT NULL` | missing / present | the *only* correct way to test for NULL |\n\n`SELECT DISTINCT` removes duplicate **rows** (all selected columns together), not duplicates in the first column."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l1-distinct", "sql": "SELECT DISTINCT product, term_months\nFROM loans", "explain": "`DISTINCT` works on the combination of columns. For the purposes of `DISTINCT` and `GROUP BY`, all NULLs count as the same value, so Pay in 4 contributes a single `(pay_in_4, NULL)` row.", "options": [{"text": "2: one per product", "correct": false}, {"text": "4: one per term", "correct": false}, {"text": "5: four monthly terms plus one Pay in 4 row with NULL", "correct": true}, {"text": "6", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "How many rows does this return? (Recall `term_months` is NULL for Pay in 4 loans; monthly loans have terms 3, 6, 12 or 24.)"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## AND before OR\n\n`AND` binds tighter than `OR`, just as `*` binds tighter than `+`. This is one of the most common silent bugs:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE product = 'monthly' AND status = 'late' OR status = 'charged_off'\n-- means:\nWHERE (product = 'monthly' AND status = 'late') OR status = 'charged_off'\n```\n\nWhen you mix `AND` and `OR`, always use parentheses, or replace the `OR` with `IN`."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l1-precedence", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "We want **monthly** loans that are either late or charged off. This query runs, but returns the wrong rows. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT loan_id, product, status\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly' AND status = 'late' OR status = 'charged_off'", "solution": "SELECT loan_id, product, status\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly'\n  AND status IN ('late', 'charged_off')", "alts": ["SELECT loan_id, product, status\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly' AND (status = 'late' OR status = 'charged_off')"], "hints": ["Run the starter and look at the `product` column. Do you see any `pay_in_4` rows?", "Group the two status conditions with parentheses, or use `status IN (...)`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## CASE: if/else inside a query\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  CASE\n    WHEN apr = 0 THEN 'interest-free'\n    WHEN apr < 0.20 THEN 'low APR'\n    ELSE 'high APR'\n  END AS apr_band\nFROM loans\nLIMIT 10\n```\n\nConditions are tested top to bottom and the first true one wins. If nothing matches and there's no `ELSE`, the result is NULL."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`CASE WHEN` is `case_when()`, and it behaves the same way: first match wins, and unmatched rows get `NA`/NULL. The SQL spelling: each branch is `WHEN condition THEN value`, there's one optional `ELSE` instead of `.default = ` or `TRUE ~`, and the whole thing ends with `END`. Forgetting `END` is the most common syntax error."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l1-case", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated on 31 December 2025, return `loan_id`, `principal`, and a column `size_band` that is `'small'` below 250, `'medium'` from 250 up to (not including) 1000, and `'large'` from 1000.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  CASE\n    WHEN principal < 250 THEN 'small'\n    WHEN principal < 1000 THEN 'medium'\n    ELSE 'large'\n  END AS size_band\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Because the first matching `WHEN` wins, the second branch only needs `principal < 1000`: anything below 250 was already caught."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## NULL: the value that isn't one\n\nNULL means *unknown* or *missing*. SQL follows a simple rule that has surprising consequences:\n\n> **Any comparison with NULL is NULL**, not true or false. And `WHERE` keeps a row only when the condition is **TRUE**.\n\nSo SQL has three truth values: TRUE, FALSE and NULL (unknown).\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  NULL = NULL   AS null_equals_null,\n  NULL <> 1     AS null_not_one,\n  NULL IS NULL  AS null_is_null,\n  1 + NULL      AS arithmetic_with_null\n```\n\nConsequences:\n\n- `WHERE annual_income = NULL` returns **nothing**, ever. Use `IS NULL`.\n- `WHERE payment_method <> 'ach'` silently drops rows where `payment_method` is NULL.\n- `WHERE NOT (condition)` also drops rows where the condition is NULL. `NOT NULL` is still NULL."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l1-not-equal", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM payments\nWHERE payment_method <> 'ach'", "explain": "For the NULL rows, `NULL <> 'ach'` is NULL, not TRUE, so `WHERE` drops them. If \"not ACH\" should include unknown methods, say so explicitly:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE payment_method <> 'ach' OR payment_method IS NULL\n```", "options": [{"text": "16,935", "correct": true}, {"text": "18,387", "correct": false}, {"text": "48,388", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "`payments` has 30,001 `ach` rows, 16,935 `debit_card` rows and 1,452 rows where `payment_method` is NULL. What does this return?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "Here, R and SQL actually agree: `NA != \"ach\"` is `NA`, and `filter()` drops rows where the condition is `NA`. Your instincts are right so far. The differences show up in aggregation (lesson 2) and joins (lesson 3)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### COALESCE: fill in a default\n\n`COALESCE(a, b, c)` returns the first non-NULL argument. It's how you replace NULLs in output:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') AS method,\n  amount\nFROM payments\nLIMIT 5\n```"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`COALESCE(x, 'unknown')` is `coalesce(x, \"unknown\")` or `replace_na(x, \"unknown\")`. Same function, same name."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l1-missing-income", "kind": "fill", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "List borrower accounts in California (`state = 'CA'`) that have **no** reported income. Return `borrower_id` and `email`.", "starter": "SELECT borrower_id, email\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE state = 'CA'\n  AND annual_income ____", "solution": "SELECT borrower_id, email\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE state = 'CA'\n  AND annual_income IS NULL", "alts": [], "hints": ["`= NULL` never matches anything."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l1-no-score", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Some applications were approved even though no credit file was found (`credit_score` is NULL). List those applications made on or after 1 December 2025: `application_id`, `applied_at` and `requested_amount`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT application_id, applied_at, requested_amount\nFROM applications\nWHERE decision = 'approved'\n  AND credit_score IS NULL\n  AND applied_at >= DATE '2025-12-01'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Three conditions joined by `AND`: the decision, the missing score, and the date."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Sorting and NULLs\n\n`ORDER BY col DESC` sorts descending. Add more columns to break ties: `ORDER BY principal DESC, loan_id`. Where NULLs land depends on the database:"}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "", "text": "NULLs sort **last** in ascending order in PostgreSQL, DuckDB, Snowflake and Oracle, and **first** in MySQL, SQL Server, SQLite and BigQuery. Write `ORDER BY x NULLS LAST` where it's supported (not MySQL or SQL Server) when it matters, or sort on `x IS NULL` first."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "When a question says \"top 10\", ask what should happen with ties, and add a tie-breaker (usually the ID) so the result is deterministic. Interviewers notice."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- `AND` binds before `OR`: parenthesize or use `IN`.\n- `CASE WHEN ... THEN ... ELSE ... END`: first match wins, no match gives NULL.\n- Comparisons with NULL are NULL, and `WHERE` keeps only TRUE. Use `IS NULL` / `IS NOT NULL`.\n- `<>` and `NOT` silently drop NULL rows.\n- `COALESCE` replaces NULL with a default."}]}, {"id": "02-aggregation-and-grain", "title": "Aggregation and grain", "minutes": 45, "summary": "GROUP BY, the three kinds of COUNT, rates done right, and how NULLs quietly change your averages.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Collapsing rows\n\nAn aggregate function turns many rows into one value. Without `GROUP BY`, the whole table collapses into a single row:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*)        AS n_loans,\n  SUM(principal)  AS total_principal,\n  AVG(principal)  AS avg_principal,\n  MIN(originated_date) AS first_loan,\n  MAX(originated_date) AS last_loan\nFROM loans\n```\n\nWith `GROUP BY`, you get one row per group:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*)                 AS n_loans,\n  ROUND(AVG(principal), 2) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product\n```\n\n**The rule:** every column in `SELECT` must either appear in `GROUP BY` or be inside an aggregate function. `SELECT product, status, COUNT(*) ... GROUP BY product` is an error, because there's no single `status` for the whole `pay_in_4` group."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`GROUP BY` + aggregates is `group_by() |> summarise()`. Three differences:\n\n- SQL has no `.by =` shortcut, and **you list the grouping columns twice**: once in `SELECT` (to see them) and once in `GROUP BY` (to group by them).\n- A grouped `summarise()` drops the grouping one level at a time. SQL has no leftover grouping: the result is a plain table.\n- `group_by() |> mutate()` (keep every row, add a group statistic) is **not** `GROUP BY`. That's a window function (lesson 6)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Three kinds of COUNT\n\n| Expression | Counts |\n|---|---|\n| `COUNT(*)` | rows |\n| `COUNT(col)` | rows where `col` is **not NULL** |\n| `COUNT(DISTINCT col)` | distinct non-NULL values of `col` |"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l2-counts", "sql": "SELECT\n  COUNT(*)                    AS a,\n  COUNT(term_months)          AS b,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT term_months) AS c\nFROM loans", "explain": "`COUNT(term_months)` skips NULLs, and so does `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)`. Pay in 4 loans don't contribute a \"NULL term\" to the distinct count. Compare with `SELECT DISTINCT term_months`, which *does* return a NULL row.", "options": [{"text": "8,823 · 8,823 · 5", "correct": false}, {"text": "8,823 · 5,425 · 4", "correct": true}, {"text": "8,823 · 5,425 · 5", "correct": false}, {"text": "5,425 · 5,425 · 4", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 8,823 loans, 3,398 of them Pay in 4, where `term_months` is NULL. What does this return?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "- `COUNT(*)` is `n()`.\n- `COUNT(x)` is `sum(!is.na(x))`.\n- `COUNT(DISTINCT x)` is `n_distinct(x, na.rm = TRUE)`. Careful: plain `n_distinct(x)` **counts NA as a value**, so it would give 5 here."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Aggregates skip NULLs\n\n`SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN` and `MAX` ignore NULLs. That's convenient and dangerous, because it happens silently.\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  AVG(annual_income)              AS avg_of_reported,\n  AVG(COALESCE(annual_income, 0)) AS avg_treating_missing_as_zero,\n  COUNT(*) - COUNT(annual_income) AS n_missing\nFROM borrowers\n```\n\nNeither is \"the\" right answer. The first is the average of people who reported an income; the second pretends non-reporters earn nothing. Pick deliberately, and say which in an interview."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "This is a real difference. In R, `mean(x)` returns `NA` if anything is missing, and you opt in to skipping with `na.rm = TRUE`. **In SQL, `na.rm = TRUE` is always on and can't be turned off.** If NULLs matter, you have to count them yourself (`COUNT(*) - COUNT(x)`)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l2-avg-income", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 0, "prompt": "We want the **average reported income** by state, ignoring borrowers who didn't report one. The query runs, but the averages are too low. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT\n  state,\n  AVG(COALESCE(annual_income, 0)) AS avg_income\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "solution": "SELECT\n  state,\n  AVG(annual_income) AS avg_income\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "alts": [], "hints": ["What does `COALESCE(annual_income, 0)` do to someone with no reported income, and what does `AVG` do with a NULL?"], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## WHERE vs HAVING\n\n- `WHERE` filters **rows**, before grouping.\n- `HAVING` filters **groups**, after grouping, so it can use aggregates.\n\n```sql\nSELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE created_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'   -- rows: accounts opened in 2025\nGROUP BY state\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 100                  -- groups: states with 100+ of them\nORDER BY n_accounts DESC\n```"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`WHERE` is a `filter()` *before* `summarise()`; `HAVING` is a `filter()` *after* it."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l2-having", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Find states with more than 300 borrower accounts. Return `state` and `n_accounts`. This one doesn't even run. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE COUNT(*) > 300\nGROUP BY state", "solution": "SELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 300", "alts": [], "hints": ["Aggregates can't appear in `WHERE`, because groups don't exist yet when `WHERE` runs."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Conditional aggregation\n\nThis is the most useful pattern in analytics SQL: put a `CASE` *inside* an aggregate to count or sum only some rows.\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_charged_off,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS charge_off_rate\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product\n```\n\n- `SUM(CASE WHEN cond THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)` counts rows meeting a condition.\n- `AVG(CASE WHEN cond THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)` is the **share** of rows meeting it, because the mean of 0/1 values is a proportion.\n- `SUM(CASE WHEN cond THEN amount END)` sums only matching rows. The missing `ELSE` gives NULL, which `SUM` skips."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "In R you'd write `sum(status == \"charged_off\")` and `mean(status == \"charged_off\")`, because logicals coerce to 0/1. Some databases let you sum a boolean, but not all, so `CASE` is the portable spelling. Learn it until it's automatic."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l2-pivot", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each product, count loans in each status. Return `product`, `n_current`, `n_late`, `n_paid_off`, `n_charged_off` (one row per product).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  product,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'current' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)     AS n_current,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'late' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)        AS n_late,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'paid_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)    AS n_paid_off,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_charged_off\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": ["One `SUM(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)` per status column, and `GROUP BY product`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "This \"pivot with conditional aggregation\" works in every dialect. Some databases have a `PIVOT` keyword, but the syntax varies too much to rely on.\n\n## Rates: the integer division trap\n\nIn PostgreSQL and SQL Server, dividing two integers **truncates**: `7 / 2` is `3`, and `COUNT(*) / COUNT(*)`-style rates come out as 0 or 1. DuckDB (this course's engine), MySQL, Snowflake and BigQuery return a decimal, so the bug won't show up here, but it will in a Postgres interview.\n\nThe habits that make rates safe everywhere:\n\n```sqlstatic\n1.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) / COUNT(*)  -- force decimals\nAVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)                 -- or average a 0/1 flag\nx / NULLIF(y, 0)                                                         -- avoid divide-by-zero\n```\n\n`NULLIF(y, 0)` returns NULL when `y` is 0, so the division gives NULL instead of an error."}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "Integer division", "text": "If a rate comes back as exactly 0 or 1 for every group, you've almost certainly divided integers. Multiply by `1.0` first."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l2-scored", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Do applicants without a credit file get approved less often? For applications, return `score_status` (`'scored'` when `credit_score` is present, `'no score'` when it's NULL), `n_applications`, and `approval_rate` (approved ÷ all, as a decimal like 0.734).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score' ELSE 'scored' END AS score_status,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY CASE WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score' ELSE 'scored' END", "alts": [], "hints": ["You can `GROUP BY` an expression. Repeat the whole `CASE` in `GROUP BY`. (Many databases also accept `GROUP BY 1`, meaning \"the first selected column\", which is handy in interviews.)", "The rate is the average of a 0/1 flag: `AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`."], "explain": "Repeating the expression is the most portable form. `GROUP BY 1` works in PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, BigQuery and DuckDB, but not in SQL Server. Grouping by the alias (`GROUP BY score_status`) works in some engines but not all, so avoid relying on it."}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "", "text": "PostgreSQL and DuckDB also support `COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE status = 'late')`. It's nice, but not portable: `CASE` works everywhere."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- Every selected column is either grouped or aggregated.\n- `COUNT(*)` counts rows, `COUNT(x)` non-NULL values, `COUNT(DISTINCT x)` distinct non-NULL values.\n- `SUM`/`AVG`/`MIN`/`MAX` silently skip NULLs. Decide whether that's what you want.\n- `WHERE` filters rows before grouping; `HAVING` filters groups after.\n- Conditional aggregation: `SUM(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)` to count, `AVG(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)` for rates.\n- Protect rates from integer division (`1.0 *`) and zero denominators (`NULLIF`)."}]}, {"id": "03-joins", "title": "Joins: syntax and types", "minutes": 55, "summary": "How to combine tables: INNER and LEFT joins, keys, the ON-versus-WHERE trap, and why NULL keys never match.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Why joins exist\n\nEach table holds one kind of thing: loans, merchants, borrowers. Instead of copying the merchant's name onto every loan, `loans` stores a `merchant_id`, a **foreign key** that points to the merchant's row. A join follows that pointer.\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  l.loan_id,\n  l.principal,\n  m.merchant_name,\n  m.category\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE l.originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'\n```\n\nAnatomy:\n\n- `FROM loans l` gives the table a short **alias**, `l`. Aliases make queries shorter, and they're required when the same column name exists in both tables.\n- `JOIN merchants m ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id` means: for each loan, find the merchant rows where this condition is true.\n- `l.loan_id`: prefix columns with the alias. Unprefixed `merchant_id` here would be an \"ambiguous column\" error, because it exists in both tables."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "Prefix **every** column with its table alias once a query has a join, even when it isn't ambiguous. It reads better, it survives someone adding a column later, and interviewers read it as care."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## How to think about a join\n\nVenn diagrams are a popular way to explain joins, and they mislead: they suggest joins work on *sets of values*. A better mental model:\n\n> **For each row on the left, find every row on the right where the `ON` condition is true, and output one combined row per match.**\n\nThat model predicts everything:\n\n- A loan matches exactly one merchant → one output row per loan.\n- A loan matches 12 installments → **12 output rows** for that loan.\n- A row that matches nothing → dropped (INNER JOIN) or kept with NULLs (LEFT JOIN).\n\n## INNER JOIN and LEFT JOIN\n\n`JOIN` (short for `INNER JOIN`) keeps only rows that found at least one match. `LEFT JOIN` keeps **every** row from the left table; where there's no match, the right table's columns are NULL.\n\n```sql\n-- every borrower, with their loans if any\nSELECT\n  b.borrower_id,\n  b.created_at,\n  l.loan_id,\n  l.principal\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nORDER BY b.borrower_id DESC\nLIMIT 10\n```\n\nThe newest borrowers mostly haven't taken a loan yet, so you'll see NULLs in `loan_id` and `principal`."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l3-left-count", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_rows\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id", "explain": "Each borrower with loans appears once per loan (8,823 rows in total), and each of the 1,412 borrowers without loans appears once with NULLs: 8,823 + 1,412 = 10,235. A LEFT JOIN never has *fewer* rows than its left table, but it can have many more.", "options": [{"text": "6,120: one per borrower", "correct": false}, {"text": "8,823: one per loan", "correct": false}, {"text": "10,235", "correct": true}, {"text": "53,996,760: every borrower paired with every loan", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 6,120 borrowers and 8,823 loans. 1,412 borrowers have no loans. How many rows does this return?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "The joins map one to one:\n\n| dplyr | SQL |\n|---|---|\n| `inner_join(x, y, by = join_by(id))` | `FROM x JOIN y ON y.id = x.id` |\n| `left_join(x, y, by = ...)` | `FROM x LEFT JOIN y ON ...` |\n| `right_join()` | `RIGHT JOIN`: rare, just swap the tables and use LEFT |\n| `full_join()` | `FULL OUTER JOIN` |\n| `semi_join()` / `anti_join()` | `WHERE EXISTS` / `WHERE NOT EXISTS` (lesson 4) |\n\nTwo differences matter:\n\n- dplyr merges the key into one column and suffixes clashing names with `.x`/`.y`. SQL keeps both tables' columns side by side, and you pick them with the alias.\n- dplyr (≥ 1.1) **warns** about unexpected many-to-many matches. SQL never warns; it just multiplies rows."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l3-state-loans", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many loans were made to borrowers in each state? Return `state` and `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  b.state,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "alts": [], "hints": ["`state` lives on `borrowers`; loans live on `loans`. Join them on `borrower_id`, then group.", "Start from `loans`: the thing you're counting is the left table."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Counting with a LEFT JOIN\n\nA classic bug: after a LEFT JOIN, `COUNT(*)` counts the borrower's own row even when there's no loan, so borrowers with no loans show 1 instead of 0."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l3-count-zero", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For every borrower account in Nevada (`state = 'NV'`), return `borrower_id` and `n_loans`, the number of loans they have, **including 0** for borrowers with none. The query below gives some borrowers the wrong count. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT\n  b.borrower_id,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE b.state = 'NV'\nGROUP BY b.borrower_id", "solution": "SELECT\n  b.borrower_id,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE b.state = 'NV'\nGROUP BY b.borrower_id", "alts": [], "hints": ["Run the starter and find a borrower with `n_loans = 1`. Do they really have a loan?", "Count a column from the *right* table. It's NULL when there was no match, and `COUNT(column)` skips NULLs."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## ON vs WHERE in a LEFT JOIN\n\nWith an INNER JOIN, a condition behaves the same in `ON` or `WHERE`. With a LEFT JOIN it doesn't:\n\n- A condition in `ON` decides **which right-hand rows match**. Left rows are kept no matter what.\n- A condition in `WHERE` runs **after** the join and filters the combined rows. A `WHERE` on a right-table column throws away the NULL rows the LEFT JOIN just kept, **silently turning it into an INNER JOIN**.\n\n```sql\n-- every merchant, with its December 2025 loans (if any)\nSELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  m.merchant_name,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS dec_2025_loans\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-12-01'   -- right-table filter goes in ON\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nORDER BY dec_2025_loans\nLIMIT 5\n```\n\nMove that date condition into a `WHERE` and the merchants with zero December loans vanish."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l3-on-where", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For **every** merchant, return `merchant_id` and `n_charged_off`, its number of charged-off loans, **including merchants with 0**. Fix the query.", "starter": "SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_charged_off\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nWHERE l.status = 'charged_off'\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id", "solution": "SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_charged_off\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n AND l.status = 'charged_off'\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id", "alts": ["SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_charged_off\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id"], "hints": ["The answer should have 150 rows, one per merchant. How many does the starter return?", "Move the status condition from `WHERE` into the `ON` clause."], "explain": "Both versions keep every merchant. The first restricts which loans *match*; the second matches all loans and counts only the charged-off ones with conditional aggregation. Either is a good answer. Being able to explain why the `WHERE` version was wrong is the part interviewers listen for."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## NULL keys never match\n\nThe `ON` condition is a comparison, and `NULL = NULL` is NULL, not TRUE. So **rows with a NULL key never match anything**, not even another NULL."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l3-null-keys", "sql": "WITH a AS (\n  SELECT 1 AS k UNION ALL SELECT NULL\n),\nb AS (\n  SELECT 1 AS k UNION ALL SELECT NULL\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS n_rows\nFROM a\nJOIN b ON b.k = a.k", "explain": "Only `1 = 1` is TRUE. `NULL = NULL` is NULL, so the NULL rows don't pair up. With a LEFT JOIN, the NULL row from `a` would be kept with no match.", "options": [{"text": "1", "correct": true}, {"text": "2", "correct": false}, {"text": "4", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "`WITH` just defines two tiny inline tables here (lesson 5 covers it). How many rows does the join return?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "This is a real trap for R users: **dplyr joins match `NA` to `NA` by default** (`na_matches = \"na\"`). Join two data frames on a column with missing values and dplyr pairs the NAs up; SQL never does. If you really want NULLs to match, the condition is `ON a.k = b.k OR (a.k IS NULL AND b.k IS NULL)`, and that's rarely what you want."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Joining more than two tables\n\nEach `JOIN` adds one table to the rows built so far. Chain them:"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l3-three-tables", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans to borrowers in California (`state = 'CA'`), return each merchant `category` (exactly as stored), with `n_loans` and `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  m.category,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE b.state = 'CA'\nGROUP BY m.category", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start `FROM loans l`, join `borrowers b` for the state and `merchants m` for the category."], "explain": "You'll notice `Fashion` and `Fashion ` (trailing space), plus a NULL category. The data is messy on purpose. Cleaning it (`TRIM`, `UPPER`/`LOWER`, `COALESCE`) is a problem-bank exercise."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Keys with more than one column\n\n`installments` is identified by **two** columns together: (`loan_id`, `installment_number`). Payments point to an installment the same way, so the join needs both:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  i.loan_id,\n  i.installment_number,\n  i.due_date,\n  i.amount_due,\n  p.paid_at,\n  p.amount,\n  p.status\nFROM installments i\nLEFT JOIN payments p\n  ON p.loan_id = i.loan_id\n AND p.installment_number = i.installment_number\nWHERE i.loan_id = 900194\nORDER BY i.installment_number, p.paid_at\n```\n\nForget the second condition and every payment matches *every* installment of the loan. That's the subject of the next lesson."}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "", "text": "`JOIN ... USING (loan_id)` is shorthand for equal-named keys in PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, BigQuery and DuckDB, but not SQL Server. `ON` works everywhere; prefer it under pressure."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- A join pairs each left row with **every** right row that satisfies `ON`.\n- `INNER JOIN` drops unmatched rows; `LEFT JOIN` keeps them with NULLs.\n- After a LEFT JOIN, count a right-table column, not `*`.\n- Right-table filters in a LEFT JOIN belong in `ON`. In `WHERE` they turn it into an inner join.\n- NULL keys never match, unlike dplyr's default.\n- Composite keys need every column in the `ON`."}]}, {"id": "04-fan-out-and-anti-joins", "title": "Joins: fan-out, semi and anti joins", "minutes": 50, "summary": "How joins multiply rows (and money), the aggregate-then-join fix, and how to ask \"has any\" or \"has none\" without a join.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Cardinality: how many matches per row?\n\nBefore you join two tables, ask: **for one row on the left, how many rows on the right can match?**\n\n| Relationship | Example | Rows after joining |\n|---|---|---|\n| many → one | loans → merchants | same as `loans` |\n| one → many | loans → installments | one per installment |\n| many → many | payments → installments on `loan_id` only | can explode |\n\nJoining *to the one side* (looking up a merchant name) is safe: the grain doesn't change. Joining *to the many side* changes the grain, and every column from the \"one\" side gets **repeated** once per match."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l4-installments", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_rows\nFROM loans l\nJOIN installments i\n  ON i.loan_id = l.loan_id", "explain": "Every installment belongs to exactly one loan, so the join produces one row per installment. The result's grain is now *installment*, even though you started `FROM loans`.", "options": [{"text": "8,823", "correct": false}, {"text": "65,990", "correct": true}, {"text": "8,823 × 65,990", "correct": false}, {"text": "Somewhere between them, it depends on the data", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "`loans` has 8,823 rows. Each loan has 3 to 24 installments; `installments` has 65,990 rows. What does this return?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Fan-out: when joins inflate totals\n\nHere is the bug that sinks more SQL screens than any other. What's the total principal we've lent?\n\n```sql\nSELECT SUM(principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans\n```\n\nNow the same total, but someone joined payments first, perhaps because they also wanted to sum collections:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal,\n  SUM(p.amount)    AS total_paid\nFROM loans l\nJOIN payments p\n  ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id\n```\n\nPrincipal jumped from about \\$6.5M to \\$44M. Each loan's principal was added once **per payment row**. Nothing errored and no warning appeared. This is **fan-out**.\n\n**Diagnose it** by comparing row counts with distinct keys:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*)                  AS n_rows,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM loans l\nJOIN payments p\n  ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id\n```\n\nIf `n_rows` > `n_loans`, any loan-level column you `SUM` or `COUNT(*)` is inflated.\n\n### The fix: aggregate first, then join\n\nBring the many side **down to the grain you need** before joining. Here that means one row per loan:\n\n```sql\nWITH paid AS (           -- step 1: payments → one row per loan\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    SUM(amount) AS total_paid\n  FROM payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n)\nSELECT                   -- step 2: now it's one-to-one\n  SUM(l.principal)  AS total_principal,\n  SUM(pd.total_paid) AS total_paid\nFROM loans l\nLEFT JOIN paid pd\n  ON pd.loan_id = l.loan_id\n```\n\n`WITH name AS (...)` defines a named intermediate result, a **CTE**, that the rest of the query can use like a table. Lesson 5 covers it properly; for now, read it as \"first compute this, then...\"."}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "SUM(DISTINCT) is not a fix", "text": "`SUM(DISTINCT l.principal)` \"fixes\" the inflated total only by accident. It adds up distinct *values*, so two different loans that both happen to be \\$500.00 count once. `COUNT(DISTINCT loan_id)` is safe for counting; `SUM(DISTINCT ...)` almost never is. Aggregate first, then join."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "dplyr ≥ 1.1 would warn you here (\"Each row in `x` is expected to match at most 1 row in `y`\") if you declared `relationship = \"many-to-one\"`, and it warns automatically for many-to-many. SQL has no such guardrail. **You are the guardrail:** check `COUNT(*)` against `COUNT(DISTINCT key)` whenever a join touches a one-to-many relationship."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Duplicates are fan-out's cousin\n\n`payments` has some rows loaded twice: same `payment_id`, same everything. Summing it double-counts those payments. `SELECT DISTINCT *` removes rows that are identical in every column:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM payments)                          AS raw_rows,\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) d) AS distinct_rows\n```\n\nA subquery in `FROM` (here `(SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) d`) works like a temporary table and needs an alias. Lesson 6 shows the more general dedup tool, `ROW_NUMBER()`."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l4-fanout", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "By product, we want `n_loans`, `total_principal` and `total_collected`: the sum of **successful** payments, counting each payment once, with loans that have no payments still counted. The starter has three separate problems. Find and fix all of them.", "starter": "SELECT\n  l.product,\n  COUNT(*)         AS n_loans,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal,\n  SUM(p.amount)    AS total_collected\nFROM loans l\nJOIN payments p\n  ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id\nWHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\nGROUP BY l.product", "solution": "WITH paid AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  l.product,\n  COUNT(*)          AS n_loans,\n  SUM(l.principal)  AS total_principal,\n  SUM(pd.collected) AS total_collected\nFROM loans l\nLEFT JOIN paid pd\n  ON pd.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY l.product", "alts": [], "hints": ["Problem 1: `COUNT(*)` and `SUM(l.principal)` are computed after joining to payments. What's the grain at that point?", "Problem 2: some payment rows are exact duplicates. Problem 3: an inner join drops loans that have no successful payments yet.", "Build a CTE that turns de-duplicated, successful payments into one row per loan, then `LEFT JOIN` it to `loans`."], "explain": "The three fixes: **aggregate before joining** (fan-out), **de-duplicate** (`SELECT DISTINCT *`), and **LEFT JOIN** so loans without payments still count. Each one changes the answer. In an interview, naming all three, even if you only had time to fix two, is a strong signal."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Composite keys and many-to-many\n\nA join on part of a composite key creates a many-to-many match. If you join payments to installments on `loan_id` alone, every payment matches every installment of its loan."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l4-composite", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loan `900194`, return each installment's `installment_number`, `amount_due` and `amount_paid` (sum of successful payments, each counted once; 0 if none). The amounts come out far too big. Fix the join.", "starter": "SELECT\n  i.installment_number,\n  i.amount_due,\n  COALESCE(SUM(p.amount), 0) AS amount_paid\nFROM installments i\nLEFT JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  ON p.loan_id = i.loan_id\n AND p.status = 'succeeded'\nWHERE i.loan_id = 900194\nGROUP BY i.installment_number, i.amount_due", "solution": "SELECT\n  i.installment_number,\n  i.amount_due,\n  COALESCE(SUM(p.amount), 0) AS amount_paid\nFROM installments i\nLEFT JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  ON p.loan_id = i.loan_id\n AND p.installment_number = i.installment_number\n AND p.status = 'succeeded'\nWHERE i.loan_id = 900194\nGROUP BY i.installment_number, i.amount_due", "alts": [], "hints": ["What identifies an installment? One column isn't enough."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Semi joins: \"has at least one\"\n\nOften you don't want columns from the other table; you just want to know whether a match **exists**. A join would duplicate rows for every match. Use `EXISTS`:\n\n```sql\n-- merchants that have at least one loan over $5,000\nSELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM loans l\n  WHERE l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n    AND l.principal > 5000\n)\n```\n\nThe subquery refers to `m` from the outer query: it's **correlated**. Read it as \"for each merchant, does at least one such loan exist?\" `SELECT 1` is a convention, since `EXISTS` only checks whether any row comes back.\n\n`IN` with a subquery does the same job:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE m.merchant_id IN (SELECT l.merchant_id FROM loans l WHERE l.principal > 5000)\n```"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l4-semi", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "List the merchants that have at least one **charged-off** loan: `merchant_id` and `merchant_name`, one row per merchant.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM loans l\n  WHERE l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n    AND l.status = 'charged_off'\n)", "alts": ["SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nWHERE m.merchant_id IN (\n  SELECT merchant_id FROM loans WHERE status = 'charged_off'\n)", "SELECT DISTINCT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nJOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nWHERE l.status = 'charged_off'"], "hints": ["A plain join gives one row per charged-off loan. Either de-duplicate, or use `EXISTS`/`IN`, which never duplicates."], "explain": "All three work. `EXISTS` states the intent most directly and never fans out. `JOIN` + `DISTINCT` works but does extra work and hides the intent. If you reach for `DISTINCT` to fix a join, ask yourself whether you wanted a semi join."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Anti joins: \"has none\"\n\n\"Customers who never...\", \"loans with no payment...\", \"merchants without...\" are anti joins, and interviews love them. Two reliable spellings:\n\n```sqlstatic\n-- 1. NOT EXISTS (preferred)\nSELECT b.borrower_id\nFROM borrowers b\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\n)\n\n-- 2. LEFT JOIN, keep the rows that found no match\nSELECT b.borrower_id\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE l.loan_id IS NULL\n```\n\nIn version 2, test a column that can't be NULL in a real match: the key itself is ideal."}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "NOT IN and NULL", "text": "There is a third spelling, `WHERE x NOT IN (SELECT y FROM ...)`, and it has a trap. If the subquery returns **even one NULL**, `NOT IN` returns **no rows at all**. `x NOT IN (1, NULL)` means `x <> 1 AND x <> NULL`, and `x <> NULL` is never TRUE. Use `NOT EXISTS`."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l4-not-in", "sql": "WITH t AS (SELECT 1 AS x UNION ALL SELECT 2 UNION ALL SELECT 3),\n     s AS (SELECT 1 AS y UNION ALL SELECT NULL)\nSELECT x\nFROM t\nWHERE x NOT IN (SELECT y FROM s)", "explain": "For `x = 2`: `2 <> 1` is TRUE, but `2 <> NULL` is NULL, and `TRUE AND NULL` is NULL, so the row is dropped. Every row fails the same way. `NOT EXISTS` returns 2 and 3 as you'd expect.", "options": [{"text": "2: the values 2 and 3", "correct": false}, {"text": "3", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": true}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "How many rows come back?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l4-anti", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts applied at least once but **never** got a loan? Return a single number, `n_borrowers`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM borrowers b\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM applications a WHERE a.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\n)\nAND NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\n)", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT a.borrower_id) AS n_borrowers\nFROM applications a\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = a.borrower_id\nWHERE l.loan_id IS NULL"], "hints": ["Two conditions on each borrower: an application exists, and no loan exists."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`semi_join(x, y)` is `WHERE EXISTS`, and `anti_join(x, y)` is `WHERE NOT EXISTS`. Same idea, same guarantee: one output row per row of `x`, never duplicated."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- Before joining, know the cardinality. Joining to the many side changes the grain.\n- **Fan-out** inflates sums and counts of the one side. Check `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(DISTINCT key)`.\n- Fix fan-out by **aggregating the many side first**, then joining.\n- Join on **every** column of a composite key.\n- \"Has any\" → `EXISTS`; \"has none\" → `NOT EXISTS` (or `LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL`). Avoid `NOT IN` with subqueries."}]}, {"id": "05-ctes-and-subqueries", "title": "Building queries in steps", "minutes": 45, "summary": "CTEs and subqueries let you break a hard question into small, checkable steps, which is how to approach every interview problem.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## One question, several steps\n\nInterview questions are rarely one `SELECT`. \"What share of borrowers took a second loan within 90 days of their first?\" needs: find each borrower's loans in order → find the first and second → compare dates → compute a share.\n\nSQL's tool for this is the **common table expression (CTE)**: a named query defined at the top with `WITH`, then used like a table.\n\n```sql\nWITH loans_per_borrower AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n)\nSELECT\n  n_loans,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM loans_per_borrower\nGROUP BY n_loans\nORDER BY n_loans\n```\n\nSeveral CTEs are separated by commas, and each can use the ones before it:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWITH step_one AS (\n  ...\n),\nstep_two AS (\n  SELECT ... FROM step_one ...\n)\nSELECT ... FROM step_two\n```"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "A CTE chain is what you'd write in R as intermediate objects:\n\n```r\nloans_per_borrower <- loans |> count(borrower_id, name = \"n_loans\")\nloans_per_borrower |> count(n_loans, name = \"n_borrowers\")\n```\n\nEach CTE is one of those assignments, scoped to the single query. The habit transfers directly: one CTE per idea, a descriptive name for each."}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "**Build CTE by CTE and run each one as you go.** Write the first CTE, finish with `SELECT * FROM step_one LIMIT 10`, look at the rows, then add the next. Out loud: \"First I'll get one row per borrower with their loan count, and check that looks right.\" This catches mistakes when they're cheap, and shows the interviewer a method rather than a guess."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l5-buckets", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Bucket borrower accounts by how many loans they have: `'1'`, `'2'`, or `'3+'`. Return `loan_bucket` and `n_borrowers`. Only borrowers with at least one loan count.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH per_borrower AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n)\nSELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN n_loans = 1 THEN '1'\n    WHEN n_loans = 2 THEN '2'\n    ELSE '3+'\n  END AS loan_bucket,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM per_borrower\nGROUP BY CASE\n    WHEN n_loans = 1 THEN '1'\n    WHEN n_loans = 2 THEN '2'\n    ELSE '3+'\n  END", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1 (CTE): one row per borrower with `n_loans`. Step 2: `CASE` on `n_loans`, then group by that `CASE`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Subqueries: the same idea, inline\n\nA subquery is a query in parentheses inside another. Where you put it determines what it must return:\n\n| Where | Must return | Example use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `FROM (...) alias` | a table | aggregate first, then use it |\n| `WHERE x IN (...)` | one column | membership test |\n| `WHERE EXISTS (...)` | anything | semi/anti join (lesson 4) |\n| `SELECT (...)` or `WHERE x > (...)` | **one value** (a *scalar* subquery) | compare to an overall total |\n\nA scalar subquery is perfect for \"compared to the overall average\":\n\n```sql\nSELECT loan_id, principal\nFROM loans\nWHERE principal > 5 * (SELECT AVG(principal) FROM loans)\nORDER BY principal DESC\n```\n\nCTEs and `FROM` subqueries are interchangeable. Prefer CTEs whenever there's more than one step: they read top to bottom, like a pipeline."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l5-share", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "What share of total principal does each merchant `category` (exactly as stored) account for? Return `category` and `share_of_principal` as a decimal (e.g. 0.183).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  m.category,\n  SUM(l.principal) / (SELECT SUM(principal) FROM loans) AS share_of_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nGROUP BY m.category", "alts": [], "hints": ["The numerator is a grouped `SUM`; the denominator is one number for the whole table: a scalar subquery."], "explain": "Lesson 6 shows a second way: `SUM(SUM(l.principal)) OVER ()`, a window over the grouped result. In PostgreSQL, if `principal` were an integer you'd need `1.0 *` to avoid integer division; here it's a decimal."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Comparing each row to its group\n\n\"Loans that are unusually large *for their product*\" compares each row to a group statistic. Compute the group statistic in a CTE and join it back:"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l5-above-group", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Find loans whose principal is more than **three times the average principal of their product**. Return `loan_id`, `product` and `principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH product_avg AS (\n  SELECT product, AVG(principal) AS avg_principal\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY product\n)\nSELECT l.loan_id, l.product, l.principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN product_avg pa\n  ON pa.product = l.product\nWHERE l.principal > 3 * pa.avg_principal", "alts": ["SELECT l.loan_id, l.product, l.principal\nFROM loans l\nWHERE l.principal > 3 * (\n  SELECT AVG(l2.principal)\n  FROM loans l2\n  WHERE l2.product = l.product\n)"], "hints": ["Step 1: average principal per product. Step 2: join it back to `loans` on `product` and filter."], "explain": "The second version uses a **correlated scalar subquery**: for each loan it computes the average of loans with the same product. It's correct and compact, but conceptually re-runs per row. The CTE version is easier to check step by step. Lesson 6's window functions give a third, often cleanest, option: `AVG(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY product)`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Aggregate-then-join, as a habit\n\nThe fix for fan-out from lesson 4 is the most common CTE pattern in analytics: **bring each table to the grain you need, then join**."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l5-collected", "kind": "fill", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For each loan `status`, what fraction of principal has been collected so far? Fill in the CTE so it returns **one row per loan** with `collected`: the sum of successful, de-duplicated payments.", "starter": "WITH collected AS (\n  -- one row per loan_id with the amount collected\n  ____\n)\nSELECT\n  l.status,\n  SUM(COALESCE(c.collected, 0)) / SUM(l.principal) AS collected_ratio\nFROM loans l\nLEFT JOIN collected c\n  ON c.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY l.status", "solution": "WITH collected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  l.status,\n  SUM(COALESCE(c.collected, 0)) / SUM(l.principal) AS collected_ratio\nFROM loans l\nLEFT JOIN collected c\n  ON c.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY l.status", "alts": [], "hints": ["Inside the CTE: start `FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p`, keep successful payments, group by `loan_id`."], "explain": "Paid-off loans come out above 1.0 because collections include interest. Charged-off loans show how much was recovered before default, typically well under half."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Stacking results: UNION ALL\n\n`UNION ALL` stacks the rows of two queries with the same columns. `UNION` does the same **and removes duplicate rows**, which is slower and usually not what you want.\n\n```sql\nSELECT 'application' AS event, applied_at AS event_time\nFROM applications WHERE borrower_id = 200364\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'loan', CAST(originated_date AS TIMESTAMP)\nFROM loans WHERE borrower_id = 200364\nORDER BY event_time\n```\n\nLook at the result: each loan sorts *before* the application it came from. Casting a date to a timestamp makes it midnight, earlier than the afternoon application. Mixing dates and timestamps is the subject of lesson 8."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l5-union", "sql": "SELECT product FROM loans\nUNION\nSELECT product FROM loans", "explain": "`UNION` de-duplicates the *combined* result, so only the two distinct products survive. `UNION ALL` would return 17,646 rows. Default to `UNION ALL`, and only use `UNION` when you mean \"and remove duplicates\".", "options": [{"text": "17,646", "correct": false}, {"text": "8,823", "correct": false}, {"text": "2", "correct": true}, {"text": "4", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "How many rows?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`UNION ALL` is `bind_rows()` (but columns are matched by **position**, not name). `UNION` is `bind_rows() |> distinct()`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- `WITH a AS (...), b AS (...) SELECT ...`: one CTE per step, named for what it holds.\n- Build and run CTEs one at a time; check each before adding the next.\n- Scalar subqueries give you a single number (an overall total or average) to compare against.\n- Group statistics: compute them in a CTE and join back (or use a window function, next lesson).\n- `UNION ALL` stacks; `UNION` also de-duplicates."}]}, {"id": "06-window-functions-ranking", "title": "Window functions: ranking and dedup", "minutes": 50, "summary": "Compute group statistics without collapsing rows, then use ROW_NUMBER for the three classic patterns: top-N per group, first/latest per group, and de-duplication.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Group statistics without collapsing\n\n`GROUP BY` turns many rows into one. Often you want to **keep every row** and attach something about its group, such as each loan's principal next to the average for its product:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  loan_id,\n  product,\n  principal,\n  AVG(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY product) AS product_avg,\n  principal / AVG(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY product) AS vs_product_avg\nFROM loans\nORDER BY loan_id\nLIMIT 10\n```\n\n`OVER (...)` turns an aggregate into a **window function**: compute it over a set of rows (the window), but return the result on every row instead of collapsing them.\n\n- `PARTITION BY product`: the window is \"all rows with the same product\". Leave it out and the window is the whole result.\n- `ORDER BY ...` inside `OVER`: orders rows within each partition, needed for ranking and running calculations."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "This is `group_by() |> mutate()`:\n\n```r\nloans |>\n  group_by(product) |>\n  mutate(product_avg = mean(principal),\n         vs_product_avg = principal / product_avg) |>\n  ungroup()\n```\n\n`PARTITION BY` is the `group_by()`, and the result keeps every row, exactly like `mutate()`. The difference: each window function carries its own partition, so one `SELECT` can mix windows partitioned different ways, plus a window with no partition at all."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## When windows are computed\n\nBack to the run order from lesson 0. Window functions are computed **in the `SELECT` step**, after `WHERE`, `GROUP BY` and `HAVING`. Two consequences:\n\n1. **The window only sees rows that survived `WHERE`.**\n2. **You can't filter on a window function in `WHERE`.** It doesn't exist yet. Compute it in a CTE, then filter outside."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l6-where-first", "sql": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  product,\n  principal,\n  AVG(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY product) AS product_avg\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "explain": "`WHERE` runs first, so the window sees only that day's loans. If you need an all-time average next to a filtered set of rows, compute it in a CTE over the full table first, then filter.", "options": [{"text": "About $333, the average over all Pay in 4 loans", "correct": false}, {"text": "About $347, the average over Pay in 4 loans originated that day", "correct": true}, {"text": "Each loan's own principal", "correct": false}, {"text": "An error: windows can't be combined with WHERE", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Across *all* loans, the average Pay in 4 principal is about \\$333. On 31 December 2025, it was about \\$347. What does `product_avg` show for the Pay in 4 rows here?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Ranking: ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK\n\nAll three number the rows of each partition in `ORDER BY` order. They differ only on ties:\n\n```sql\nWITH scores AS (\n  SELECT 'a' AS who, 100 AS score UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'b', 90 UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'c', 90 UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'd', 80\n)\nSELECT\n  who,\n  score,\n  ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS row_number,\n  RANK()       OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS rank,\n  DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS dense_rank\nFROM scores\n```\n\n| Function | Ties get | After a tie | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `ROW_NUMBER()` | different numbers (arbitrary order!) | continues 1, 2, 3, 4 | picking exactly one row per group |\n| `RANK()` | the same number | skips: 1, 2, 2, **4** | competition ranking |\n| `DENSE_RANK()` | the same number | no gap: 1, 2, 2, **3** | \"top 3 distinct values\" |"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l6-rank", "sql": "SELECT RANK() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) AS rnk\nFROM (SELECT 100 AS score UNION ALL SELECT 90 UNION ALL SELECT 90 UNION ALL SELECT 80) s", "explain": "Two rows tie at rank 2, so `RANK` skips 3 and the next row is 4. `DENSE_RANK` would give 3.", "options": [{"text": "3", "correct": false}, {"text": "4", "correct": true}, {"text": "2", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "With scores 100, 90, 90, 80, what is `RANK()` for the score of 80 (ordered high to low)?"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`row_number()`, `min_rank()` and `dense_rank()` in dplyr are the same three functions (`min_rank` is SQL's `RANK`). One difference: dplyr's `row_number()` breaks ties by current row order, and SQL has no row order. **Add a tie-breaker to `ORDER BY`** (usually an ID) so `ROW_NUMBER` is deterministic."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Pattern 1: first or latest row per group\n\nNumber the rows in each partition, then keep row 1. Because you can't filter a window in `WHERE`, this always takes a CTE:\n\n```sql\nWITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    l.*,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY borrower_id\n      ORDER BY originated_date DESC, loan_id DESC\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM loans l\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, loan_id, originated_date, principal\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1          -- each borrower's most recent loan\nORDER BY borrower_id\nLIMIT 10\n```"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l6-first-loan", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return each borrower's **first** loan: `borrower_id`, `loan_id`, `originated_date`. If a borrower took two loans on the same first day, pick the lower `loan_id`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    loan_id,\n    originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY borrower_id\n      ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, loan_id, originated_date\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["Same shape as the example above, but ascending order, so earliest first.", "The tie-breaker goes in the window's `ORDER BY`: `ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id`."], "explain": "Why not `MIN(originated_date)` with `GROUP BY borrower_id`? That gives the first *date*, but not which loan it was. Joining back on the date breaks when there are two loans that day. `ROW_NUMBER` returns the whole row, tie-break included."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l6-where-window", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return each borrower's **most recent** application: `borrower_id`, `application_id`, `applied_at`. This doesn't run. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT\n  borrower_id,\n  application_id,\n  applied_at,\n  ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at DESC) AS rn\nFROM applications\nWHERE rn = 1", "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    application_id,\n    applied_at,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at DESC, application_id DESC) AS rn\n  FROM applications\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, application_id, applied_at\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["`WHERE` runs before the window exists. Compute `rn` inside a CTE, then filter on it outside."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "", "text": "Snowflake, BigQuery, Databricks and DuckDB support `QUALIFY`, a `WHERE` for window functions, so no CTE is needed: `... QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...) = 1`. PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQL Server do **not**. Know it exists; default to the CTE version unless you're told the dialect."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Pattern 2: top N per group\n\nThe same pattern with `rn <= N`:"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l6-top3", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated in 2025, find the **three largest loans in each product**: `product`, `loan_id`, `principal`. Break ties on `principal` with the lower `loan_id`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    product,\n    loan_id,\n    principal,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product ORDER BY principal DESC, loan_id) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n)\nSELECT product, loan_id, principal\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn <= 3", "alts": [], "hints": ["Filter to 2025 inside the CTE (before the window), partition by product, order by principal descending."], "explain": "`ROW_NUMBER` guarantees exactly three per product. If the question were \"the top three principal *amounts*, including ties\", you'd use `DENSE_RANK` and might get more than three rows. Asking which one the interviewer wants is a good clarifying question."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Pattern 3: de-duplication\n\n`SELECT DISTINCT *` only removes rows that are identical in **every** column. Real duplicates are often messier: the same `payment_id` loaded twice with a different load timestamp, say. `ROW_NUMBER` handles both: partition by whatever *should* be unique, keep row 1.\n\n```sql\nWITH deduped AS (\n  SELECT\n    p.*,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY payment_id ORDER BY paid_at) AS rn\n  FROM payments p\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS n_payments, COUNT(DISTINCT payment_id) AS n_ids\nFROM deduped\nWHERE rn = 1\n```"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l6-dedup", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "What is the total amount of **successful** payments once duplicate `payment_id`s are removed? Return one number, `total_collected`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH deduped AS (\n  SELECT\n    p.*,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY payment_id ORDER BY paid_at) AS rn\n  FROM payments p\n)\nSELECT SUM(amount) AS total_collected\nFROM deduped\nWHERE rn = 1\n  AND status = 'succeeded'", "alts": ["SELECT SUM(amount) AS total_collected\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nWHERE status = 'succeeded'"], "hints": ["Number rows within each `payment_id`, keep `rn = 1`, then sum successful amounts."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "When you de-duplicate, say what you're treating as the unique key and why: \"payment_id should be unique, so I'll keep one row per payment_id.\" If you can, show the evidence: `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(DISTINCT payment_id)`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Windows over grouped results\n\nA window can wrap an aggregate. This runs after `GROUP BY`, so it sees one row per group:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  product,\n  SUM(principal) AS total_principal,\n  SUM(principal) / SUM(SUM(principal)) OVER () AS share_of_total\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product\n```\n\n`SUM(SUM(principal)) OVER ()` reads as: the sum, across all groups, of each group's sum. It looks strange the first time; it's the window equivalent of lesson 5's scalar subquery.\n\n## Recap\n\n- `fn() OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...)` computes per group **without collapsing rows**, like `group_by() |> mutate()`.\n- Windows run in the `SELECT` step: they see only rows that passed `WHERE`, and can't be filtered in `WHERE`, so use a CTE.\n- `ROW_NUMBER` (one per row), `RANK` (ties share, then skip), `DENSE_RANK` (ties share, no skip).\n- Always add a tie-breaker to the window's `ORDER BY`.\n- Three patterns: first/latest per group, top-N per group, de-duplication: all `ROW_NUMBER` + CTE + `WHERE rn ...`."}]}, {"id": "07-window-functions-sequences", "title": "Window functions: sequences and running totals", "minutes": 45, "summary": "LAG and LEAD for comparing a row to its neighbours, running totals, moving averages, and the window-frame default that catches people out.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## Looking at the previous row\n\n`LAG(x)` returns `x` from the previous row in the window's order, and `LEAD(x)` from the next. They're how you compute changes over time and gaps between events.\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  n_loans,\n  LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS prev_month,\n  n_loans - LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS change\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month\n```\n\nThe first row has no previous row, so `LAG` returns NULL, and so does the `change` calculated from it. `LAG(x, 2)` looks two rows back, and `LAG(x, 1, 0)` supplies a default instead of NULL."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`lag()` and `lead()` in dplyr are the same idea. The important difference: **dplyr uses the data frame's current row order** unless you pass `order_by =`; **SQL requires the order in `OVER (ORDER BY ...)`**, because tables have no row order. Add `PARTITION BY` where you'd `group_by()`, for example per borrower."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l7-mom", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For 2025, return each month's loan count and the change versus the previous month: `month` (1–12), `n_loans`, `prev_n_loans`, `pct_change` (as a decimal, so 0.05 for +5%). Sort by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  n_loans,\n  LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS prev_n_loans,\n  1.0 * (n_loans - LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month))\n      / LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS pct_change\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "hints": ["Aggregate to one row per month in a CTE, then apply `LAG` over that result.", "Percent change is `(this - previous) / previous`. Use `1.0 *` so it can't become integer division."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Gaps between events\n\nWith `PARTITION BY`, `LAG` compares each row to the previous row **for the same entity**:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  borrower_id,\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date,\n  LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS prev_loan_date\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id IN (200364, 200147)\nORDER BY borrower_id, originated_date\n```\n\nSubtracting two dates gives the number of days between them in PostgreSQL and DuckDB (other dialects use `DATEDIFF`; see lesson 8)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l7-gap", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 1, "prompt": "Across all borrowers, what is the average number of days between one loan and the borrower's **next** loan? Return a single number, `avg_days_between`. Only consecutive pairs of loans count; a borrower's first loan has no gap.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH gaps AS (\n  SELECT\n    originated_date\n      - LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id)\n      AS days_since_prev\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT AVG(days_since_prev) AS avg_days_between\nFROM gaps", "alts": [], "hints": ["In a CTE, compute `originated_date - LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY ...)`. First loans get NULL.", "`AVG` skips NULLs, which is exactly what you want for first loans."], "explain": "Here aggregates skipping NULLs works in your favour: no `WHERE days_since_prev IS NOT NULL` needed, though writing it does no harm and makes the intent explicit."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Running totals\n\nAn aggregate with `ORDER BY` inside `OVER` becomes **cumulative**: each row sees the rows from the start of its partition up to itself.\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month,\n    SUM(principal) AS principal\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  principal,\n  SUM(principal) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS running_principal,\n  SUM(principal) OVER ()               AS year_total\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month\n```"}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY t)` is `cumsum(x)` after `arrange(t)`. With `PARTITION BY` it's a grouped `cumsum`. `SUM(x) OVER ()` (empty window) is `sum(x)` repeated on every row."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l7-running", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For 2025, return `month` (1–12), `n_loans` that month, and `ytd_loans`: the cumulative number of loans so far in the year. Sort by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  n_loans,\n  SUM(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS ytd_loans\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "hints": ["Aggregate per month first, then `SUM(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month)`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Window frames, and the default that bites\n\nWhen a window has an `ORDER BY`, it also has a **frame**: which rows count as \"so far\". The default is\n\n```sqlstatic\nRANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n```\n\n`RANGE` means \"every row whose `ORDER BY` value is ≤ mine\", so **rows that tie on the order column are all included together**. A running total over data with repeated dates jumps by the whole day at once, and tied rows show the same total."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l7-ties", "sql": "WITH t AS (\n  SELECT 1 AS d, 10 AS x UNION ALL\n  SELECT 2, 5 UNION ALL\n  SELECT 2, 5 UNION ALL\n  SELECT 3, 1\n)\nSELECT d, x, SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY d) AS running\nFROM t", "explain": "With the default `RANGE` frame, both `d = 2` rows are peers and are included together: 10 + 5 + 5 = 20 on *both* rows. To accumulate strictly row by row, ask for `ROWS`: `SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY d ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)`. Even then, the order *among* the tied rows is arbitrary, so add a tie-breaker.", "options": [{"text": "15", "correct": false}, {"text": "20", "correct": true}, {"text": "10", "correct": false}, {"text": "21", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What is `running` on the first row with `d = 2`?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Moving averages with ROWS\n\nFrames also give you moving windows:\n\n```sqlstatic\nAVG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)\n```\n\nThat's a 3-row moving average: this row and the two before it. At the start of the data it simply averages fewer rows."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l7-moving", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For 2025, return `month`, `n_loans`, and `ma_3`: the average of this month's and the previous two months' loan counts (fewer at the start of the year). Sort by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  n_loans,\n  AVG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS ma_3\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "hints": ["`ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW` inside the `OVER`, after the `ORDER BY`."], "explain": "`ROWS` counts *rows*, not months. If a month had no loans at all, it would be missing from `monthly`, and \"2 preceding\" would reach back three months. With sparse data, build a complete month list first (lesson 8)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Other useful window functions\n\n| Function | Returns |\n|---|---|\n| `FIRST_VALUE(x) OVER (...)` | `x` from the first row of the window |\n| `LAST_VALUE(x) OVER (...)` | **careful**: with the default frame, the \"last\" row is the current row. Add `ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING` |\n| `NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY x)` | quartile number 1–4 |\n| `COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY g)` | group size on every row: handy for \"share of group\" |\n\n## Recap\n\n- `LAG`/`LEAD` compare a row to its neighbours; always give the window an `ORDER BY`, and partition per entity.\n- `SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY t)` is a running total; `SUM(x) OVER ()` is the grand total on every row.\n- The default frame with `ORDER BY` is `RANGE ... CURRENT ROW`: tied rows are included together.\n- Use `ROWS BETWEEN n PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW` for moving windows.\n- Aggregate to the right grain (often per month) in a CTE first, then apply the window."}]}, {"id": "08-dates-and-time", "title": "Dates and time", "minutes": 45, "summary": "Dates vs timestamps, safe date ranges, truncating to months, date arithmetic, and filling in missing months. This is where SQL dialects differ most.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "dialect", "title": "Read this first", "text": "Date functions are the least portable part of SQL. This lesson uses PostgreSQL-style syntax, which DuckDB shares. The concepts are identical everywhere; only the spelling changes. Once you know your interview dialect, keep the [dialect sheet](#/ref/03-dialects) open next to this lesson."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Dates and timestamps\n\n| Type | Example | In this data |\n|---|---|---|\n| `DATE` | `2025-03-14` | `originated_date`, `due_date`, `charged_off_date`, `onboarded_date` |\n| `TIMESTAMP` | `2025-03-14 18:22:05` | `applied_at`, `paid_at`, `created_at` |\n\nWrite literals as `DATE '2025-03-14'` and `TIMESTAMP '2025-03-14 18:22:05'`. Convert a timestamp to its date with `CAST(paid_at AS DATE)`.\n\nWhen you compare a timestamp with a date, the date is treated as **midnight at the start of that day**. That one fact causes the most common date bug in SQL.\n\n## Ranges: use half-open intervals\n\n\"Payments in January 2025.\" The tempting version:\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE paid_at BETWEEN DATE '2025-01-01' AND DATE '2025-01-31'\n```\n\n`DATE '2025-01-31'` means `2025-01-31 00:00:00`, so every payment made later on 31 January is excluded. The fix that works for both dates and timestamps, in every dialect: **from the start, up to but not including the next start**.\n\n```sqlstatic\nWHERE paid_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND paid_at <  DATE '2025-02-01'\n```"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l8-half-open", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Count the **successful** payment rows made in January 2025. Return `n_payments`. This undercounts. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_payments\nFROM payments\nWHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  AND paid_at BETWEEN DATE '2025-01-01' AND DATE '2025-01-31'", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_payments\nFROM payments\nWHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  AND paid_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND paid_at < DATE '2025-02-01'", "alts": [], "hints": ["What time of day does `DATE '2025-01-31'` mean when it's compared with a timestamp?"], "explain": "`CAST(paid_at AS DATE) BETWEEN ...` also gives the right count, but wrapping the column in a function can stop a real database using an index on it. The half-open range is the professional habit."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Pulling dates apart: EXTRACT\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  originated_date,\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date)  AS yr,\n  EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS mon,\n  EXTRACT(DOW FROM originated_date)   AS day_of_week   -- 0 = Sunday here\nFROM loans\nLIMIT 5\n```"}, {"type": "pitfall", "title": "Day-of-week numbering", "text": "PostgreSQL and DuckDB number Sunday 0. MySQL's `DAYOFWEEK()` and SQL Server's `DATEPART(weekday, ...)` number Sunday **1**. BigQuery's `EXTRACT(DAYOFWEEK ...)` is also 1 = Sunday. Check before you write `= 0`."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l8-month-merge", "sql": "SELECT EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)", "explain": "`EXTRACT(MONTH ...)` is just the month number, so January 2023, 2024 and 2025 all land in group `1`. That's fine *within* one year (lesson 7 filtered to 2025 first). For a time series across years, **truncate** instead.", "options": [{"text": "36: one per month of each year", "correct": false}, {"text": "12", "correct": true}, {"text": "3", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Loans span 2023–2025. How many rows does this return?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Truncating to a period: DATE_TRUNC\n\n`DATE_TRUNC('month', d)` rounds a date or timestamp **down** to the first instant of its month, keeping the year. It's the standard way to build monthly time series:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY month\n```\n\nOther units: `'year'`, `'quarter'`, `'week'` (Monday-start in PostgreSQL), `'day'`. In PostgreSQL `DATE_TRUNC` returns a timestamp even for a date, hence the `CAST(... AS DATE)`."}, {"type": "rlens", "title": "", "text": "`DATE_TRUNC('month', d)` is `lubridate::floor_date(d, \"month\")`, and `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)` is `year(d)`. The R habit of making a `month` column with `floor_date()` and then `count(month)` translates directly."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l8-monthly", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated in 2024, return one row per month: `month` (the first day of the month), `n_loans` and `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2024-01-01'\n  AND originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Filter to 2024 with a half-open range, then group by `DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date)`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Date arithmetic\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date,\n  originated_date + INTERVAL '30 days'           AS plus_30_days,\n  originated_date + INTERVAL '1 month'           AS plus_1_month,\n  DATE '2025-12-31' - originated_date            AS days_on_book\nFROM loans\nWHERE loan_id = 900194\n```\n\n- `date + INTERVAL '...'` adds time. Month arithmetic clamps to month end (Jan 31 + 1 month = Feb 28/29).\n- `date - date` gives a number of **days** in PostgreSQL and DuckDB. Elsewhere it's a `DATEDIFF` function, with arguments in a different order per dialect.\n- Timestamp minus timestamp gives an *interval*, not a number. Cast to dates first if you want days.\n\n### Months between two dates\n\n\"Months on book\" (how old a loan was when something happened) is a core lending measure. The portable way to count calendar months between two dates:\n\n```sqlstatic\n(EXTRACT(YEAR FROM later) - EXTRACT(YEAR FROM earlier)) * 12\n  + (EXTRACT(MONTH FROM later) - EXTRACT(MONTH FROM earlier))\n```\n\nThis counts month *boundaries* crossed: 31 Jan → 1 Feb is 1. Dialects' `DATEDIFF(month, ...)` functions mostly do the same."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l8-mob", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For charged-off loans, how many months after origination did the charge-off happen? Return `months_on_book` and `n_loans`, sorted by `months_on_book`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  (EXTRACT(YEAR FROM charged_off_date) - EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date)) * 12\n    + (EXTRACT(MONTH FROM charged_off_date) - EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)) AS months_on_book,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE status = 'charged_off'\nGROUP BY\n  (EXTRACT(YEAR FROM charged_off_date) - EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date)) * 12\n    + (EXTRACT(MONTH FROM charged_off_date) - EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date))\nORDER BY months_on_book", "alts": [], "hints": ["Use the months-between formula with `charged_off_date` as the later date. Repeat it in `GROUP BY` (or use `GROUP BY 1`)."], "explain": "Nothing charges off before about month 4, because charge-off happens 120 days after the first missed payment. A metric like \"charged off within 3 months\" would be 0 by construction. Understanding *why* a number is what it is matters as much as the SQL."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Joining on dates with an offset\n\nComparisons across tables work the same way. \"Payments made more than 30 days after the due date\":"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l8-days-late", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For **successful** payments made in Q4 2025 (October–December), what was the average number of days between the installment's due date and the payment date, by payment method? Return `payment_method` (show `'unknown'` for NULL) and `avg_days_late`. Count each payment once (remember the duplicates). Early payments count as negative.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(p.payment_method, 'unknown') AS payment_method,\n  AVG(CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) - i.due_date) AS avg_days_late\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nJOIN installments i\n  ON i.loan_id = p.loan_id\n AND i.installment_number = p.installment_number\nWHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n  AND p.paid_at >= DATE '2025-10-01'\n  AND p.paid_at < DATE '2026-01-01'\nGROUP BY COALESCE(p.payment_method, 'unknown')", "alts": [], "hints": ["Join payments to installments on **both** key columns to get each payment's `due_date`.", "Days late = `CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) - i.due_date`. Cast the timestamp to a date first so you get a number of days, not an interval."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Filling gaps: a calendar to join against\n\n`GROUP BY month` only returns months that have data. A merchant with no loans in March simply has no March row, which breaks charts, `LAG` and moving averages. The fix is to generate every month and `LEFT JOIN` the data onto it:\n\n```sql\nWITH months AS (\n  SELECT CAST(m AS DATE) AS month\n  FROM generate_series(DATE '2025-01-01', DATE '2025-12-01', INTERVAL '1 month') AS t(m)\n)\nSELECT\n  mo.month,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM months mo\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', l.originated_date) AS DATE) = mo.month\n AND l.merchant_id = 1054\nGROUP BY mo.month\nORDER BY mo.month\n```\n\nNote the merchant filter sits in the `ON`, for the same reason as in lesson 3."}, {"type": "dialect", "title": "Generating a series", "text": "`generate_series` exists in PostgreSQL and DuckDB. Snowflake uses `TABLE(GENERATOR(...))`, BigQuery `UNNEST(GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY(...))`, and SQL Server 2022 has `GENERATE_SERIES` for numbers only. A portable fallback: take `SELECT DISTINCT DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) FROM loans` as the calendar, since every month has *some* loan, or write a recursive CTE."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- `DATE` vs `TIMESTAMP`: comparing a timestamp to a date means \"midnight that day\".\n- Use half-open ranges, `>= start AND < next_start`, never `BETWEEN` with timestamps.\n- `EXTRACT(MONTH ...)` merges years; use `DATE_TRUNC('month', ...)` for time series.\n- `date - date` is days (PostgreSQL/DuckDB); months-between: the year×12 + month formula.\n- Generate a calendar and `LEFT JOIN` onto it to show periods with zero activity.\n- Date syntax is the most dialect-specific thing you'll write: check the dialect sheet."}]}, {"id": "09-lending-metrics", "title": "Lending metrics in SQL", "minutes": 60, "summary": "The handful of metrics a consumer-credit risk team lives on (funnel rates, days past due, charge-offs by vintage), and the SQL patterns behind each.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "Interviewers for a risk and analytics team want to know that you can write the SQL *and* that you understand what the number means. This lesson pairs each metric with the pattern that computes it and the trap that makes it wrong.\n\n## The funnel: applications → approvals → loans\n\n| Metric | Definition | Grain to watch |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Approval rate** | approved ÷ applications | applications, borrowers, or people? |\n| **Take-up (conversion) rate** | loans ÷ approved applications | an approval that isn't used isn't a loan |\n\n`loans.application_id` links each loan back to its application, one-to-zero-or-one, so a `LEFT JOIN` from applications to loans can't fan out."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l9-funnel", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "By year of application, return `app_year`, `n_applications`, `n_approved`, `n_loans`, `approval_rate` (approved ÷ applications) and `take_up_rate` (loans ÷ approved).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at) AS app_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN a.decision = 'approved' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_approved,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN a.decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate,\n  1.0 * COUNT(l.loan_id) / SUM(CASE WHEN a.decision = 'approved' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS take_up_rate\nFROM applications a\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.application_id = a.application_id\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start from `applications`, `LEFT JOIN loans` on `application_id`, and group by the year.", "`COUNT(l.loan_id)` counts only applications that became loans. Use conditional aggregation for approvals."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "interview", "title": "The grain question", "text": "\"Approval rate\" sounds simple until you notice declined applicants often **retry within minutes** for less money. At the application grain, one person declined twice then approved once counts as a 33% approval rate; at the borrower-day grain, 100%. Neither is wrong, but you should ask which the team uses, or state the one you chose. Duplicate accounts (one person, two `borrower_id`s) push \"unique customers\" the same way."}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Approval rates by score band\n\nCredit decisions are analysed by **score band**. NULL scores are their own band, not something to drop:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n    WHEN credit_score < 600 THEN '<600'\n    WHEN credit_score < 660 THEN '600-659'\n    WHEN credit_score < 720 THEN '660-719'\n    ELSE '720+'\n  END AS score_band,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN applied_at < DATE '2024-07-01' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS apps_before,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN applied_at < DATE '2024-07-01'\n           THEN CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END END) AS approval_before,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN applied_at >= DATE '2024-07-01' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS apps_after,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN applied_at >= DATE '2024-07-01'\n           THEN CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END END) AS approval_after\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY 1\nORDER BY 1\n```\n\nThe nested `CASE` produces 1/0 for rows in the period and NULL for rows outside it. `AVG` skips the NULLs, so each column is the approval rate *within* its period. Approval for the 600–659 band dropped sharply after the policy change on 2024-07-01.\n\n## Delinquency: days past due\n\nA loan is **delinquent** when a scheduled payment is overdue. **Days past due (DPD)** is measured from the **oldest unpaid installment**:\n\n> DPD as of date *D* = *D* − due date of the oldest installment that is due before *D* and not fully paid. 0 if there isn't one.\n\nLoans are then put in **buckets**: current, 1–29, 30–59, 60–89, 90+ DPD. \"30+ DPD rate\" (share of open balances or loans that are 30 or more days past due) is a standard early-warning metric.\n\nThe SQL needs three steps, and every lesson so far shows up:\n\n1. **Paid per installment**: de-duplicated, successful payments, summed per `(loan_id, installment_number)` (lessons 4 and 6).\n2. **Oldest unpaid installment per loan**: join to installments on both keys, keep those due before the snapshot and not fully paid, take `MIN(due_date)` (lessons 3 and 2).\n3. **DPD and bucket** for open loans: `LEFT JOIN` so loans with nothing overdue get DPD 0 (lessons 3 and 8)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l9-dpd", "kind": "fill", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "As of 2025-12-31, count **open** loans (status `current` or `late`) in each DPD bucket: `'current'` (0), `'1-29'`, `'30-59'`, `'60-89'`, `'90+'`. Return `dpd_bucket` and `n_loans`. Steps 1 and 3 are written; fill in step 2.", "starter": "WITH paid AS (            -- step 1: amount paid per installment\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, SUM(amount) AS paid\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n),\noldest_unpaid AS (        -- step 2: one row per loan with an overdue installment\n  ____\n),\ndpd AS (                  -- step 3\n  SELECT\n    l.loan_id,\n    COALESCE(DATE '2025-12-31' - ou.oldest_due, 0) AS dpd\n  FROM loans l\n  LEFT JOIN oldest_unpaid ou\n    ON ou.loan_id = l.loan_id\n  WHERE l.status IN ('current', 'late')\n)\nSELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN dpd = 0 THEN 'current'\n    WHEN dpd < 30 THEN '1-29'\n    WHEN dpd < 60 THEN '30-59'\n    WHEN dpd < 90 THEN '60-89'\n    ELSE '90+'\n  END AS dpd_bucket,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM dpd\nGROUP BY 1", "solution": "WITH paid AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, SUM(amount) AS paid\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n),\noldest_unpaid AS (\n  SELECT\n    i.loan_id,\n    MIN(i.due_date) AS oldest_due\n  FROM installments i\n  LEFT JOIN paid pd\n    ON pd.loan_id = i.loan_id\n   AND pd.installment_number = i.installment_number\n  WHERE i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'\n    AND COALESCE(pd.paid, 0) < i.amount_due\n  GROUP BY i.loan_id\n),\ndpd AS (\n  SELECT\n    l.loan_id,\n    COALESCE(DATE '2025-12-31' - ou.oldest_due, 0) AS dpd\n  FROM loans l\n  LEFT JOIN oldest_unpaid ou\n    ON ou.loan_id = l.loan_id\n  WHERE l.status IN ('current', 'late')\n)\nSELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN dpd = 0 THEN 'current'\n    WHEN dpd < 30 THEN '1-29'\n    WHEN dpd < 60 THEN '30-59'\n    WHEN dpd < 90 THEN '60-89'\n    ELSE '90+'\n  END AS dpd_bucket,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM dpd\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start `FROM installments i LEFT JOIN paid pd` on **both** key columns. Why LEFT? Installments with no payment at all must still count as unpaid.", "Keep installments due before the snapshot (`i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'`) where `COALESCE(pd.paid, 0) < i.amount_due`, then `MIN(i.due_date)` per loan."], "explain": "Three traps this avoids: **future installments** (due after the snapshot, so not overdue yet), **partial payments** (an installment with a payment isn't necessarily paid; compare the sum with `amount_due`), and **installments with no payments at all** (an inner join would hide exactly the loans you're looking for). As a check, the non-current buckets add up to the 213 loans with status `late`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Charge-offs and vintages\n\nA **charge-off** is the lender writing a loan off as a loss. Here that happens 120 days after the first missed payment. The headline risk metric is the **charge-off rate**: charged-off loans (or dollars) ÷ loans (or dollars) originated."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l9-young", "sql": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) AS orig_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS charge_off_rate\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY 1\nORDER BY 1", "explain": "Charge-off takes at least ~4 months from origination, and most 2025 loans were made in the second half of the year. They haven't had time to go bad. The policy change helps too, but you can't measure it from this table, because the years are at different ages. This is the single most important idea in credit analytics: **only compare loans at the same age.**", "options": [{"text": "Credit policy tightened in July 2024, so 2025 borrowers are much safer", "correct": false}, {"text": "Most 2025 loans are too young to have charged off yet", "correct": true}, {"text": "2025 has more Pay in 4 loans, which never charge off", "correct": false}, {"text": "Duplicate payments make 2025 look better", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Charge-off rate by origination year comes out 2023: 6.5%, 2024: 4.7%, 2025: 1.1%. What's the main reason 2025 looks so good?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "### Vintage analysis: compare like with like\n\nA **vintage** is a cohort of loans originated in the same period (here, a quarter). To compare vintages fairly, measure every vintage over the **same window**: say, \"charged off within 12 months of origination\". Then include only vintages that have **had** 12 months: originated on or before 2024-12-31 for a 2025-12-31 snapshot."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l9-vintage", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "By origination quarter, return `vintage` (first day of the quarter), `n_loans`, and `co_rate_12m`: the share of loans charged off **within 12 months of origination** (`charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '12 months'`). Include only vintages with a full 12 months of history.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE) AS vintage,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '12 months'\n           THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_rate_12m\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE)", "alts": [], "hints": ["`DATE_TRUNC('quarter', ...)` gives the vintage. A loan with `charged_off_date` NULL makes the comparison NULL, so the `CASE` falls to `ELSE 0`, which is what you want.", "Full history: originated before 2025-01-01 means 12 months had passed by the snapshot."], "explain": "Now the policy change shows up: vintages from 2023 through Q2 2024 sit around 5–7%, and from Q3 2024 roughly 2–4%. The **vintage curve** extends this: for each vintage, the cumulative charge-off rate at 1, 2, 3, … months on book, plotted as one line per vintage. Each point uses only vintages old enough to have reached that age."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Other metrics you should be able to define\n\n| Metric | Meaning | SQL shape |\n|---|---|---|\n| **First payment default (FPD)** | first installment still unpaid N days after it was due | installment 1, paid-per-installment CTE, date offset |\n| **Roll rate** | share of loans moving from one bucket to a worse one, month to month | DPD bucket at two snapshot dates, joined on `loan_id` |\n| **Loss rate ($)** | charged-off principal ÷ originated principal | conditional `SUM`, not `COUNT` |\n| **Collections rate** | collected ÷ scheduled, for installments already due | aggregate payments to installment grain, then compare |\n| **Repeat rate** | share of borrowers taking another loan within N days | `ROW_NUMBER`/`LAG` per borrower |"}, {"type": "interview", "title": "Count or dollars?", "text": "For any rate, ask: **by count or by dollars?** A 3% charge-off rate by loan count can be 5% by dollars if large loans default more. Risk teams usually care about dollars; product teams often count customers."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Recap\n\n- Funnel rates: watch the grain (applications vs borrowers vs people) and say which you used.\n- Score bands: NULL is its own band.\n- DPD: oldest unpaid, already-due installment, comparing paid amounts with what was due. Exclude future installments, handle partial payments, keep installments with no payments.\n- Never compare loans of different ages. Use vintages with a fixed outcome window, and only mature vintages.\n- Always ask: by count or by dollars?"}]}, {"id": "10-interview-craft", "title": "The interview itself", "minutes": 35, "summary": "A repeatable method for any SQL question (clarify, plan, build, verify, explain) and what to say while you do it.", "lesson": "", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## What's being assessed\n\nA SQL screen for an analyst role tests three things, roughly in this order of weight:\n\n1. **Correctness under messiness**: do you notice the duplicate, the NULL, the fan-out, the timestamp?\n2. **Structured thinking**: can you break a vague business question into steps?\n3. **Fluency**: can you write the syntax without fighting it?\n\nYou can recover from forgotten syntax (\"in Postgres I'd use `DATE_TRUNC`; let me check the exact name here\"). It's much harder to recover from a confident wrong number. The method below is built around that.\n\n## The loop\n\n### 1. Clarify (1–2 minutes)\n\nRestate the question in your own words, then ask about what's ambiguous. For this kind of data:\n\n- **As of when?** Snapshot date, and whether \"in 2025\" means originated, applied, or paid in 2025.\n- **Which rows count?** Successful payments only? Open loans only? Exclude charged-off?\n- **Count or dollars?** Loans, borrowers, or people (duplicate accounts)?\n- **Ties and ordering?** \"Top 3\" with ties: exactly 3, or all tied?\n- **NULLs?** No credit score: exclude, or its own band?\n- **Duplicates?** \"I noticed payment_id isn't unique. Should I de-duplicate?\"\n\nIf the interviewer says \"your call\", **state your assumption out loud** and carry on. That's a full-credit answer.\n\n### 2. Plan the grain (1 minute)\n\nSay what one row of the final answer is, then sketch the steps as comments before writing code:\n\n```sqlstatic\n-- final grain: one row per origination quarter\n-- 1. loans with their quarter and a 12-month charge-off flag\n-- 2. aggregate per quarter\n-- 3. keep quarters with 12 months of history\n```\n\nThe comments become your CTE names.\n\n### 3. Build step by step\n\nWrite the first CTE, run it with `SELECT * FROM step1 LIMIT 10`, look at it, then add the next. Narrate briefly: \"This gives me one row per loan with the amount collected. Let me check a loan I know.\"\n\n### 4. Verify\n\nBefore you announce a number, spend a minute trying to break it:\n\n| Check | How |\n|---|---|\n| Grain | `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(DISTINCT key)` at each step |\n| Fan-out | does a total match the same total from the base table? |\n| Lost rows | did an inner join or a `WHERE` on a LEFT-joined column drop anything? |\n| Spot-check | pick one ID and verify it by hand: `WHERE loan_id = ...` |\n| Smell test | is a rate between 0 and 1? Do categories add up to the total? |\n\n### 5. Explain\n\nGive the answer, then one sentence of meaning and one of caveats: \"2023 vintages charge off at about 6% within a year, versus 3% after the July 2024 policy change. Caveat: I counted loans, not dollars, and the latest vintages aren't mature yet.\""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "l10-smell", "sql": "SELECT m.category, SUM(l.principal) AS principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nJOIN payments p ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY m.category", "explain": "The question doesn't need `payments` at all. Someone added the join for a different column and left it in. Loans → merchants is many-to-one (safe); loans → payments is one-to-many (fan-out). Reconciling a total against a number you trust is the fastest way to catch it.", "options": [{"text": "Duplicate merchants", "correct": false}, {"text": "The join to payments fans out each loan once per payment", "correct": true}, {"text": "NULL categories are being double counted", "correct": false}, {"text": "Interest is included in principal", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "You've written a query for \"total principal originated by merchant category\" and the grand total across categories is \\$44M. You know from earlier that the loans table sums to about \\$6.5M. What's the most likely cause?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Talking while you type\n\nSilence makes interviewers nervous; constant narration makes it hard to think. A useful rhythm: **say the plan, type quietly, say what you're checking.**\n\n> \"So we want one row per borrower whose first loan was Pay in 4. I'll number each borrower's loans by date, take the first, and use `LEAD` to get the next loan date. Then the share whose next loan came within 90 days. Let me first make sure the numbering looks right… yes, borrower 200364 has four loans in date order.\"\n\n## When you're stuck\n\n- **Shrink the problem.** Solve it for one borrower or one month first.\n- **Write the version you can**, then improve it: \"This counts applications; if we want people I'd de-duplicate accounts by normalized email.\"\n- **Say what you'd do** if you can't remember syntax: \"I want the previous row's value per borrower, a `LAG` partitioned by borrower.\" The interviewer often fills in the syntax.\n- **Don't go silent for more than ~30 seconds.** Think out loud, even if it's messy.\n\n## Timed, unattended screens\n\nOn HackerRank-style tests with no interviewer:\n\n- Read **all** questions first; do the quickest ones first.\n- The checker usually compares exact output, so match the requested **column order, names, sort and rounding** precisely.\n- Test against edge cases yourself; you won't get partial credit for \"nearly\".\n\n## Capstone\n\nUse the full loop on this one. Clarify what you'd ask (the definitions below are the interviewer's answers), plan in comments, build CTE by CTE, verify, then check."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "l10-capstone", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "**Question:** Of borrowers whose **first** loan was Pay in 4, what share took **another loan within 90 days** of that first loan?\n\nDefinitions agreed with the interviewer:\n- Work at the `borrower_id` level (ignore duplicate accounts).\n- A borrower's first loan is the earliest `originated_date`, ties broken by lowest `loan_id`.\n- \"Another loan within 90 days\" means their next loan's `originated_date` is at most 90 days after the first loan's.\n- Only include borrowers whose first loan was originated on or before `2025-10-02`, so everyone had 90 days to repeat before the snapshot.\n\nReturn `n_borrowers`, `n_repeat_90d` and `repeat_rate`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ordered AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    loan_id,\n    product,\n    originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn,\n    LEAD(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS next_loan_date\n  FROM loans\n),\nfirst_loans AS (\n  SELECT *\n  FROM ordered\n  WHERE rn = 1\n    AND product = 'pay_in_4'\n    AND originated_date <= DATE '2025-10-02'\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN next_loan_date - originated_date <= 90 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_repeat_90d,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN next_loan_date - originated_date <= 90 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS repeat_rate\nFROM first_loans", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1: for every loan, its position in the borrower's sequence (`ROW_NUMBER`) and the next loan's date (`LEAD`), with the same window ordering.", "Step 2: keep `rn = 1`, Pay in 4, and the date cutoff. **Filter after the window**, not before. Otherwise \"first loan\" would mean \"first Pay in 4 loan\".", "Step 3: a borrower with no next loan has `next_loan_date` NULL, so the comparison is NULL and the `CASE` falls to 0."], "explain": "The trap is in step 2: if you put `WHERE product = 'pay_in_4'` in the same query as the window, the window only sees Pay in 4 loans, and you'd measure \"first Pay in 4 loan\" and \"next Pay in 4 loan\", a different question. Windows see only the rows that survive `WHERE` (lesson 6)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Before your screen\n\n- Re-read the [pitfalls checklist](#/ref/04-pitfalls).\n- If you know the dialect, read its column in the [dialect sheet](#/ref/03-dialects) and write five date expressions from memory.\n- Take a [mock screen](#/mock) the day before, under time, and talk out loud even though nobody's listening."}]}], "drills": [{"id": "00-select-and-functions", "title": "SELECT, expressions and functions", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Calculated columns, rounding and casting, string functions, custom sort orders, paging, DISTINCT.", "lesson": ["00-how-sql-thinks", "01-filtering-and-null"], "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills cover the everyday building blocks of `SELECT` and `ORDER BY`. Several functions here are spelled differently across dialects; the explanations say where.\n\n## Calculated columns"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-interest", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated on **31 December 2025**, return `loan_id`, `principal`, `apr`, and `yearly_interest`: principal × APR, **rounded to 2 decimals**.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  apr,\n  ROUND(principal * apr, 2) AS yearly_interest\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "s0-cast-round", "sql": "SELECT\n  CAST(3.7 AS INTEGER) AS cast_int,\n  FLOOR(3.7)           AS floored,\n  ROUND(3.7)           AS rounded", "explain": "In PostgreSQL, DuckDB, MySQL and Snowflake, casting a decimal to an integer **rounds**. In SQL Server it **truncates** (3). Don't rely on either: say what you mean with `ROUND`, `FLOOR` (down) or `CEILING` (up).", "options": [{"text": "3 · 3 · 4", "correct": false}, {"text": "4 · 3 · 4", "correct": true}, {"text": "4 · 4 · 4", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What does this return?"}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Strings"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-email-domain", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts use each email **domain** (the part after `@`)? Normalize the domain to lower case with no stray spaces. Return `domain` and `n_accounts`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  LOWER(TRIM(SUBSTRING(email, POSITION('@' IN email) + 1))) AS domain,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(SUBSTRING(email, POSITION('@' IN email) + 1)))", "alts": ["SELECT\n  LOWER(TRIM(SPLIT_PART(email, '@', 2))) AS domain,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(SPLIT_PART(email, '@', 2)))"], "hints": ["`POSITION('@' IN email)` gives the position of the `@`; `SUBSTRING(email, start)` takes everything from `start` onwards."], "explain": "`SPLIT_PART(email, '@', 2)` is neater, but only PostgreSQL, Snowflake and DuckDB have it. MySQL uses `SUBSTRING_INDEX(email, '@', -1)`; SQL Server uses `CHARINDEX('@', email)` in place of `POSITION`. You'll also spot one odd domain: real data always has a few."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "s0-concat-null", "sql": "SELECT merchant_name, 'Category: ' || category AS label\nFROM merchants\nWHERE category IS NULL", "explain": "Concatenating anything with NULL gives NULL, like arithmetic. Wrap the column: `'Category: ' || COALESCE(category, 'unknown')`. `CONCAT()` behaves differently by dialect: PostgreSQL's skips NULLs, MySQL's returns NULL. `||` isn't string concatenation in MySQL or SQL Server at all.", "options": [{"text": "`Category: `", "correct": false}, {"text": "NULL", "correct": true}, {"text": "`Category: NULL`", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Two merchants have a NULL `category`. For those two, what is `label`?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-label", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For merchants onboarded in **2025**, return `merchant_id` and `label` in the form `Summit Apparel (fashion)`: the name, then the cleaned category (lower case, trimmed) in brackets, with `unknown` for a missing category.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  merchant_id,\n  merchant_name || ' (' || COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(category)), 'unknown') || ')' AS label\nFROM merchants\nWHERE onboarded_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'", "alts": ["SELECT\n  merchant_id,\n  CONCAT(merchant_name, ' (', COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(category)), 'unknown'), ')') AS label\nFROM merchants\nWHERE onboarded_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'"], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-length", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants have names **longer than 20 characters**? Return `merchant_name` and `name_length`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT merchant_name, LENGTH(merchant_name) AS name_length\nFROM merchants\nWHERE LENGTH(merchant_name) > 20", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "SQL Server calls it `LEN()`, and it ignores trailing spaces; elsewhere it's `LENGTH()`."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Sorting and paging"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-order-case", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Count loans by status, listed in **lifecycle order**: `current`, `late`, `charged_off`, `paid_off`. Return `status` and `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY status\nORDER BY CASE status\n  WHEN 'current' THEN 1\n  WHEN 'late' THEN 2\n  WHEN 'charged_off' THEN 3\n  WHEN 'paid_off' THEN 4\nEND", "alts": [], "hints": ["You can `ORDER BY` an expression, including a `CASE` that maps each value to its position."], "explain": "`CASE status WHEN 'a' THEN ...` is the short form of `CASE WHEN status = 'a' THEN ...`. Handy when every branch tests the same column for equality."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s0-offset", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return the **11th to 15th largest** loans by principal (ties broken by lower `loan_id`): `loan_id`, `principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT loan_id, principal\nFROM loans\nORDER BY principal DESC, loan_id\nLIMIT 5 OFFSET 10", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "`OFFSET 10` skips the first ten rows. SQL Server and the standard spell it `OFFSET 10 ROWS FETCH NEXT 5 ROWS ONLY`. For \"the Nth largest *per group*\", use `ROW_NUMBER()` instead."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## DISTINCT and top-N"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-distinct", "sql": "SELECT DISTINCT payment_method\nFROM payments", "explain": "`ach`, `debit_card`, and NULL. `DISTINCT` (like `GROUP BY`) treats all NULLs as one value, unlike `=`, which never matches NULL. Yes, it's inconsistent; it's just how SQL works.", "options": [{"text": "2", "correct": false}, {"text": "3", "correct": true}, {"text": "48,388", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "How many rows?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-distinct-states", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which states have **at least one** account with income above 250,000? Return `state`, one row per state.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT DISTINCT state\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE annual_income > 250000", "alts": ["SELECT state\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE annual_income > 250000\nGROUP BY state"], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-top-scores", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return the **5 highest-scoring applications**: `application_id` and `credit_score`, highest first, ties broken by the lower `application_id`. Make it correct in any dialect.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT application_id, credit_score\nFROM applications\nWHERE credit_score IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY credit_score DESC, application_id\nLIMIT 5", "alts": [], "hints": ["Many applications share the top score. You need a tie-breaker.", "In PostgreSQL, `ORDER BY ... DESC` puts NULLs **first**, so without a filter your \"top 5\" would be five applications with no score."], "explain": "DuckDB happens to sort NULLs last in both directions, so you'd get the right answer here even without `IS NOT NULL`, but not in PostgreSQL or Oracle. Excluding NULLs explicitly makes the query mean the same thing everywhere."}]}, {"id": "01-filtering-and-null", "title": "WHERE, NULL and friends", "minutes": 30, "summary": "BETWEEN, IN, LIKE, AND/OR precedence, CASE, COALESCE, and how NULL behaves in all of them. Commit to each prediction before running anything.", "lesson": "01-filtering-and-null", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lesson 1. They're short on purpose: the goal is to make the rules automatic. For every **Predict**, commit to an answer before you look.\n\n## Ranges and lists"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-between", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return `loan_id` and `principal` for **monthly** loans with a **6-month** term and principal **from 500 to 1,000 inclusive**.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT loan_id, principal\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly'\n  AND term_months = 6\n  AND principal BETWEEN 500 AND 1000", "alts": [], "hints": ["`BETWEEN a AND b` includes both ends."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-between-order", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM loans\nWHERE principal BETWEEN 200 AND 100", "explain": "`x BETWEEN 200 AND 100` means `x >= 200 AND x <= 100`, which no number satisfies. The low value must come first. No error, no warning: just an empty result.", "options": [{"text": "The number of loans between 100 and 200", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": true}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What does this return?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-in", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return `borrower_id`, `state` and `annual_income` for accounts in **California, New York or Texas** with income **above 150,000**.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT borrower_id, state, annual_income\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE state IN ('CA', 'NY', 'TX')\n  AND annual_income > 150000", "alts": [], "hints": ["`IN ('CA', 'NY', 'TX')` instead of three `OR`s."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-precedence", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Accounts in California or New York earning **over 200,000**: `borrower_id`, `state`, `annual_income`. The query returns far too many rows. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT borrower_id, state, annual_income\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE state = 'CA' OR state = 'NY' AND annual_income > 200000", "solution": "SELECT borrower_id, state, annual_income\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE state IN ('CA', 'NY')\n  AND annual_income > 200000", "alts": ["SELECT borrower_id, state, annual_income\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE (state = 'CA' OR state = 'NY')\n  AND annual_income > 200000"], "hints": ["`AND` is evaluated before `OR`. Which accounts does `state = 'CA'` let through on its own?"], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Patterns"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-like-start", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Return `merchant_id` and `merchant_name` for merchants whose name **starts with** `Blue`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT merchant_id, merchant_name\nFROM merchants\nWHERE merchant_name LIKE 'Blue%'", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-like-case", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM merchants\nWHERE merchant_name LIKE 'blue%'", "explain": "`LIKE` is case-sensitive in PostgreSQL, DuckDB and Snowflake, so `'blue%'` doesn't match `'Blue ...'`. MySQL and SQL Server are usually case-insensitive and would return 10. Portable fix: `LOWER(merchant_name) LIKE 'blue%'`.", "options": [{"text": "10", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": true}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 10 merchants whose names start with `Blue`. What does this return here (DuckDB, like PostgreSQL)?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-like-contains", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many merchants have a `#` **anywhere** in their name? Return `n_merchants`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_merchants\nFROM merchants\nWHERE merchant_name LIKE '%#%'", "alts": [], "hints": ["`%` on both sides means \"anything before, anything after\"."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-clean-match", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Find every **Electronics** merchant, however the category was typed (any case, stray spaces). Return `merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `category`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT merchant_id, merchant_name, category\nFROM merchants\nWHERE LOWER(TRIM(category)) = 'electronics'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Normalize the column, then compare to a normalized constant: `LOWER(TRIM(category)) = 'electronics'`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## NULL in WHERE"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-or-null", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE annual_income > 100000\n   OR annual_income <= 100000", "explain": "For a NULL income both comparisons are NULL, and `NULL OR NULL` is NULL, so the row is dropped. A condition that looks like it covers \"everything\" still silently excludes NULLs.", "options": [{"text": "6,120: every number is either above or not above 100,000", "correct": false}, {"text": "5,559", "correct": true}, {"text": "561", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 6,120 borrower accounts; 561 have no income reported. How many rows does this count?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-not-null", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE NOT (annual_income > 100000)", "explain": "`NOT (NULL)` is still NULL, so the 561 unreported incomes are dropped too: 6,120 − 932 − 561 = 4,627. Negating a condition never brings NULL rows back.", "options": [{"text": "5,188 (6,120 − 932)", "correct": false}, {"text": "4,627", "correct": true}, {"text": "561", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "932 accounts have income above 100,000, and 561 have no income reported, out of 6,120. How many does this count?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-not-ach", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Count payment rows whose method is **anything other than ACH, including rows where the method is unknown**. Return `n_payments`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_payments\nFROM payments\nWHERE payment_method <> 'ach'\n   OR payment_method IS NULL", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_payments\nFROM payments\nWHERE COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') <> 'ach'"], "hints": ["`payment_method <> 'ach'` alone drops the NULL rows. Add them back explicitly."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-or-is-null", "kind": "fill", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Applications with a credit score of **at least 700, or with no score at all**. Return `application_id` and `credit_score`.", "starter": "SELECT application_id, credit_score\nFROM applications\nWHERE credit_score >= 700\n  ____", "solution": "SELECT application_id, credit_score\nFROM applications\nWHERE credit_score >= 700\n   OR credit_score IS NULL", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-neq-date", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM loans\nWHERE charged_off_date <> DATE '2025-01-01'", "explain": "Loans that were never charged off have NULL there, and `NULL <> date` is NULL. \"Not equal to X\" only matches rows that *have* a value. This is a classic way to lose most of a table.", "options": [{"text": "8,823", "correct": false}, {"text": "250", "correct": true}, {"text": "8,573", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "8,823 loans; 250 have a `charged_off_date` (none on 1 January 2025). What does this return?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-not-in-null", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM applications\nWHERE credit_score NOT IN (700, NULL)", "explain": "`x NOT IN (700, NULL)` means `x <> 700 AND x <> NULL`. The second part is never TRUE, so no row qualifies. The same happens with `NOT IN (subquery)` when the subquery returns a NULL. Use `NOT EXISTS`, or filter NULLs out of the list.", "options": [{"text": "13,670 (everything except the 80)", "correct": false}, {"text": "12,892 (scored applications except the 80)", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": true}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "80 applications have a score of exactly 700. What does this return?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-charged-june", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which loans were charged off in **June 2025**? Return `loan_id` and `charged_off_date`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT loan_id, charged_off_date\nFROM loans\nWHERE charged_off_date BETWEEN DATE '2025-06-01' AND DATE '2025-06-30'", "alts": ["SELECT loan_id, charged_off_date\nFROM loans\nWHERE charged_off_date >= DATE '2025-06-01'\n  AND charged_off_date < DATE '2025-07-01'"], "hints": [], "explain": "`BETWEEN` is safe here because `charged_off_date` is a **DATE**, with no time of day. On a timestamp column like `paid_at` it would miss most of 30 June (lesson 8). The half-open version works for both, which is why it's the safer habit."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## COALESCE"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d1-coalesce-same", "sql": "SELECT\n  SUM(CASE WHEN annual_income > 50000 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)              AS plain,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN COALESCE(annual_income, 0) > 50000 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS coalesced\nFROM borrowers", "explain": "NULL fails the first test, and 0 fails the second, so the result is identical. `COALESCE` only matters when the default you choose would *pass* the condition (`COALESCE(annual_income, 0) < 50000` *would* differ), or when you display or aggregate the value. Think about what your default does, rather than adding `COALESCE` by reflex.", "options": [{"text": "Yes", "correct": true}, {"text": "No, the COALESCE version counts more", "correct": false}, {"text": "No, the COALESCE version counts fewer", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Do `WHERE annual_income > 50000` and `WHERE COALESCE(annual_income, 0) > 50000` keep the same number of accounts?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-coalesce-types", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated on **31 December 2025**, return `loan_id`, `product`, and `term_label`: the term as text (e.g. `'12'`), or `'pay in 4'` when `term_months` is missing.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  product,\n  COALESCE(CAST(term_months AS VARCHAR), 'pay in 4') AS term_label\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "alts": ["SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  product,\n  CASE WHEN term_months IS NULL THEN 'pay in 4' ELSE CAST(term_months AS VARCHAR) END AS term_label\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'"], "hints": ["Try `COALESCE(term_months, 'pay in 4')` first and read the error.", "All arguments to `COALESCE` must be the same type. Turn the number into text with `CAST(term_months AS VARCHAR)`."], "explain": "`COALESCE(integer, text)` fails in most databases: the result can only have one type. `CAST(... AS VARCHAR)` works almost everywhere (MySQL wants `CAST(... AS CHAR)`)."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## CASE and NULL"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-income-bands", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Count borrower accounts by income band: `'not reported'`, `'under 50k'` (below 50,000), `'50k-100k'` (50,000 up to but not including 100,000) and `'100k+'`. Return `income_band` and `n_accounts`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN annual_income IS NULL THEN 'not reported'\n    WHEN annual_income < 50000 THEN 'under 50k'\n    WHEN annual_income < 100000 THEN '50k-100k'\n    ELSE '100k+'\n  END AS income_band,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["Put the `IS NULL` branch first. Otherwise NULLs fall through to the `ELSE`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-case-else", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "We want three groups: `'low'` (income below 40,000), `'not low'`, and `'unknown'`. Return `income_group` and `n_accounts`. The starter only shows two groups, and one of them is too big. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN annual_income < 40000 THEN 'low'\n    ELSE 'not low'\n  END AS income_group,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY 1", "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN annual_income IS NULL THEN 'unknown'\n    WHEN annual_income < 40000 THEN 'low'\n    ELSE 'not low'\n  END AS income_group,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["Where do the NULL incomes end up in the starter?"], "explain": ""}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Put it together"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d1-capstone", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Approved applications made **on or after 1 December 2025**, for a requested amount **from 1,000 to 2,000 inclusive**, where the credit score was **below 650 or missing**. Return `application_id`, `requested_amount`, `credit_score`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT application_id, requested_amount, credit_score\nFROM applications\nWHERE decision = 'approved'\n  AND applied_at >= DATE '2025-12-01'\n  AND requested_amount BETWEEN 1000 AND 2000\n  AND (credit_score < 650 OR credit_score IS NULL)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Four conditions joined with `AND`. One of them is itself an `OR`. What does it need?"], "explain": "Without the parentheses, `... AND credit_score < 650 OR credit_score IS NULL` would also return every unscored application ever made, declined ones included."}]}, {"id": "02-aggregation", "title": "Aggregation and NULLs", "minutes": 30, "summary": "COUNT variants, aggregates over NULLs and empty sets, HAVING, conditional sums, and safe division.", "lesson": "02-aggregation-and-grain", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lesson 2."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d2-counts", "sql": "SELECT\n  COUNT(*)                       AS a,\n  COUNT(payment_method)          AS b,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT payment_method) AS c\nFROM payments", "explain": "`COUNT(col)` skips NULLs, and so does `COUNT(DISTINCT col)`, even though `SELECT DISTINCT payment_method` would show a NULL row. Three tools, three different answers.", "options": [{"text": "48,388 · 48,388 · 3", "correct": false}, {"text": "48,388 · 46,936 · 2", "correct": true}, {"text": "48,388 · 46,936 · 3", "correct": false}, {"text": "46,936 · 46,936 · 2", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 48,388 payment rows; 1,452 have no `payment_method`. What does this return?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d2-sum-empty", "sql": "SELECT SUM(principal) AS total\nFROM loans\nWHERE principal < 0", "explain": "An aggregate with no `GROUP BY` always returns exactly one row. `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN` and `MAX` over zero rows give **NULL**; only `COUNT` gives 0. If a report should show 0, write `COALESCE(SUM(principal), 0)`.", "options": [{"text": "0", "correct": false}, {"text": "NULL", "correct": true}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}, {"text": "No rows at all", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "No loan has a negative principal. What does this return?"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d2-avg-term", "sql": "SELECT AVG(term_months) AS avg_term\nFROM loans", "explain": "`AVG` silently drops NULLs, so this is the average term of monthly loans, even though the query reads like \"all loans\". If the question were \"average term across all loans, with Pay in 4 as 1.5 months\", you'd have to say so: `AVG(COALESCE(term_months, 1.5))`.", "options": [{"text": "About 9.7: the average over monthly loans only", "correct": true}, {"text": "About 5.9: Pay in 4 counted as 0", "correct": false}, {"text": "NULL, because some values are NULL", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "`term_months` is 3, 6, 12 or 24 for monthly loans and NULL for the 3,398 Pay in 4 loans. Roughly what is this?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-state-income", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 0, "prompt": "For each state, return `n_accounts`, `n_reported` (accounts that reported an income) and `avg_income` (average of reported incomes).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  state,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  COUNT(annual_income) AS n_reported,\n  AVG(annual_income) AS avg_income\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "alts": [], "hints": ["`COUNT(annual_income)` counts non-NULL values; `AVG` already ignores NULLs."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-group-error", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each state: `n_accounts` and the highest income (`max_income`). The query doesn't run. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT state, annual_income, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "solution": "SELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts, MAX(annual_income) AS max_income\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "alts": [], "hints": ["Every selected column must be grouped or aggregated. Which aggregate gives \"highest\"?"], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-having", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants have **at least 50 loans** and **more than 100,000 in total principal**? Return `merchant_id`, `n_loans`, `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  merchant_id,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY merchant_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 50\n   AND SUM(principal) > 100000", "alts": [], "hints": ["Both conditions are about groups, so both go in `HAVING`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-success-fail", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each payment method (show NULL as `'unknown'`), count distinct successful and distinct failed payments. Return `payment_method`, `n_succeeded`, `n_failed`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') AS payment_method,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN status = 'succeeded' THEN payment_id END) AS n_succeeded,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN status = 'failed' THEN payment_id END) AS n_failed\nFROM payments\nGROUP BY COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown')", "alts": ["SELECT\n  COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') AS payment_method,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'succeeded' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_succeeded,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'failed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_failed\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nGROUP BY COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown')"], "hints": ["Either de-duplicate first and use `SUM(CASE ...)`, or use `COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN ... THEN payment_id END)`. The `CASE` with no `ELSE` gives NULL, which `COUNT` skips."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-zero-not-null", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For **every** borrower state, the total principal of **charged-off** loans: `state`, `charged_off_principal`. Show **0**, not NULL, for any state with none.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  b.state,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN l.principal ELSE 0 END) AS charged_off_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "alts": ["SELECT\n  b.state,\n  COALESCE(SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN l.principal END), 0) AS charged_off_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state"], "hints": ["Filtering `WHERE status = 'charged_off'` would drop states with none entirely. Use a conditional sum instead."], "explain": "`ELSE 0` makes the sum 0 when there's nothing to add. Without it, the sum over only NULLs is NULL."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-nullif", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For each merchant: `merchant_id`, `n_late`, `n_charged_off`, and `co_per_late`: charged-off loans divided by late loans. Return NULL for `co_per_late` when a merchant has no late loans, rather than an error.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  merchant_id,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'late' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_late,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_charged_off,\n  1.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)\n      / NULLIF(SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'late' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END), 0) AS co_per_late\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY merchant_id", "alts": [], "hints": ["`x / NULLIF(y, 0)`: when `y` is 0 the denominator becomes NULL, and so does the result."], "explain": "Without `NULLIF`, DuckDB quietly returns `inf` (or `NaN` for 0 ÷ 0), and PostgreSQL, SQL Server and Snowflake raise a division-by-zero error. Neither is what you want in a report. `NULLIF` makes the result NULL everywhere. The `1.0 *` guards against integer division."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-min-max", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each product: the first and last origination dates and the largest principal. Return `product`, `first_loan`, `last_loan`, `max_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  product,\n  MIN(originated_date) AS first_loan,\n  MAX(originated_date) AS last_loan,\n  MAX(principal) AS max_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-two-cols", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Loans and total principal by **product and status** together: `product`, `status`, `n_loans`, `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT product, status, COUNT(*) AS n_loans, SUM(principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product, status", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "One row per *combination* that exists. Combinations with no loans don't appear; for a full grid you'd need a cross join (joins drills)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-per-borrower", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each borrower state: `n_loans`, `n_borrowers` (distinct borrower accounts with a loan), and `loans_per_borrower`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  b.state,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT l.borrower_id) AS n_borrowers,\n  1.0 * COUNT(*) / COUNT(DISTINCT l.borrower_id) AS loans_per_borrower\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-having-distinct", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants have lent to **at least 100 different borrower accounts**? Return `merchant_id` and `n_borrowers`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT merchant_id, COUNT(DISTINCT borrower_id) AS n_borrowers\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY merchant_id\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT borrower_id) >= 100", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d2-median", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "What is the **median** principal for each product? Return `product` and `median_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  product,\n  PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY principal) AS median_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": ["SELECT product, MEDIAN(principal) AS median_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product"], "hints": ["SQL has no `median()` in the standard. The standard spelling is `PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY x)`."], "explain": "Medians are where dialects diverge most. `PERCENTILE_CONT(...) WITHIN GROUP` works in PostgreSQL, Snowflake, Oracle and DuckDB. Snowflake and DuckDB also have `MEDIAN()`. SQL Server only has it as a window function (`OVER (PARTITION BY ...)`), BigQuery uses `APPROX_QUANTILES`, and MySQL has none. If the dialect lacks it, say so and describe the `ROW_NUMBER`-and-count approach."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d2-where-vs-case", "sql": "SELECT\n  (SELECT AVG(principal) FROM loans WHERE status = 'charged_off')           AS a,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN principal END)                  AS b,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'charged_off' THEN principal ELSE 0 END)           AS c\nFROM loans", "explain": "Without `ELSE`, non-matching rows become NULL and `AVG` skips them, so (b) averages only charged-off loans, like the `WHERE` in (a). `ELSE 0` puts every other loan into the denominator as a zero, giving charged-off principal per loan overall. Both are legitimate numbers; just know which one you're computing.", "options": [{"text": "a and b", "correct": true}, {"text": "b and c", "correct": false}, {"text": "a and c", "correct": false}, {"text": "All three", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Which two of these return the same number?"}]}, {"id": "03-joins", "title": "Joins", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Join syntax until it's automatic, plus the traps — wrong keys, LEFT JOIN with WHERE, counting after a LEFT JOIN, fan-out in averages.", "lesson": ["03-joins", "04-fan-out-and-anti-joins"], "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lessons 3 and 4."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-three-way", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans with principal **above 5,000**, return `loan_id`, `principal`, the borrower's `state` and the `merchant_name`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT l.loan_id, l.principal, b.state, m.merchant_name\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE l.principal > 5000", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start from `loans l`, then one `JOIN ... ON` per lookup table."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d3-inner-count", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM loans l\nJOIN applications a\n  ON a.application_id = l.application_id", "explain": "Each loan matches exactly one application (many-to-one from the loan's side), so the row count is unchanged. Applications that never became loans have nothing to match and don't appear.", "options": [{"text": "8,823", "correct": true}, {"text": "13,750", "correct": false}, {"text": "8,823 × 13,750", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Every loan came from exactly one application; there are 8,823 loans and 13,750 applications. How many rows?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-wrong-key", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated on **31 December 2025**, return `loan_id` and the `credit_score` from the application the loan came from. Too many rows come back. Fix the join.", "starter": "SELECT l.loan_id, a.credit_score\nFROM loans l\nJOIN applications a\n  ON a.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nWHERE l.originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "solution": "SELECT l.loan_id, a.credit_score\nFROM loans l\nJOIN applications a\n  ON a.application_id = l.application_id\nWHERE l.originated_date = DATE '2025-12-31'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Joining on `borrower_id` matches **every** application that borrower ever made. Which column links a loan to *its* application?"], "explain": "Joining on a column that's shared but isn't the real relationship is a common and silent bug: the query runs and the numbers look plausible. Ask \"what does one row on the other side represent, and how many will match?\""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-not-taken", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Approved applications that **never became a loan**, by year applied: `app_year`, `n_not_taken`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at) AS app_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_not_taken\nFROM applications a\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.application_id = a.application_id\nWHERE a.decision = 'approved'\n  AND l.loan_id IS NULL\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at)", "alts": ["SELECT\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at) AS app_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_not_taken\nFROM applications a\nWHERE a.decision = 'approved'\n  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.application_id = a.application_id)\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.applied_at)"], "hints": ["An anti join: `LEFT JOIN loans` and keep rows where `l.loan_id IS NULL`, or use `NOT EXISTS`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-left-where", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For **every** borrower account in Oregon (`'OR'`), the number of loans they took **in 2025**, with **0** for those who took none. Return `borrower_id`, `n_loans_2025`. Fix the query: borrowers with no 2025 loans are missing.", "starter": "SELECT b.borrower_id, COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans_2025\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE b.state = 'OR'\n  AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY b.borrower_id", "solution": "SELECT b.borrower_id, COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans_2025\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\n AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nWHERE b.state = 'OR'\nGROUP BY b.borrower_id", "alts": [], "hints": ["One of the two `WHERE` conditions is about the left table and is fine there. The other is about the right table. Where does it belong?"], "explain": "`b.state = 'OR'` filters borrowers, the left table, so it stays in `WHERE`. The date condition filters loans, so it moves to `ON`: \"match only 2025 loans, but keep every borrower\"."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d3-count-star", "sql": "SELECT m.merchant_id, COUNT(*) AS n, COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id\nORDER BY n_loans, m.merchant_id\nLIMIT 5", "explain": "The LEFT JOIN keeps the merchant as one row with NULL loan columns. `COUNT(*)` counts that row (1); `COUNT(l.loan_id)` skips the NULL (0).", "options": [{"text": "0", "correct": false}, {"text": "1", "correct": true}, {"text": "NULL", "correct": false}, {"text": "It doesn't appear", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Merchant 1002 has **no loans**. What does `n` show for it?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-never", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants have **never** had a loan? Return `merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `onboarded_date`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name, m.onboarded_date\nFROM merchants m\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n)", "alts": ["SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name, m.onboarded_date\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\nWHERE l.loan_id IS NULL"], "hints": [], "explain": "Look at the onboarding dates: most of these merchants joined years ago, so they're **inactive**, not new. A quick check like that is how you tell the two apart, and explaining *why* a result looks the way it does is worth a sentence in an interview."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-semi", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Borrower accounts with income **above 100,000** that have **at least one loan currently late**: `borrower_id`, `annual_income`, one row each.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT b.borrower_id, b.annual_income\nFROM borrowers b\nWHERE b.annual_income > 100000\n  AND EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1 FROM loans l\n    WHERE l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\n      AND l.status = 'late'\n  )", "alts": ["SELECT DISTINCT b.borrower_id, b.annual_income\nFROM borrowers b\nJOIN loans l\n  ON l.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE b.annual_income > 100000\n  AND l.status = 'late'"], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-fanout-avg", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Average loan principal by borrower state: `state`, `avg_principal`. Someone joined payments \"in case we need them later\". The averages are now wrong. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT b.state, AVG(l.principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nJOIN payments p\n  ON p.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "solution": "SELECT b.state, AVG(l.principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "alts": [], "hints": ["After the payments join, each loan appears once per payment. Which loans get more weight in the average? Which loans disappear entirely?"], "explain": "Fan-out doesn't only inflate sums: it **reweights averages** (12-month loans have more payments than Pay in 4 loans, so they count more) and an inner join **drops** loans with no payments. Remove joins you don't need."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-self", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "List each pair of accounts that belong to the same person (same email after `LOWER(TRIM(...))`): `account_a`, `account_b`, with `account_a` the smaller `borrower_id`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT a.borrower_id AS account_a, b.borrower_id AS account_b\nFROM borrowers a\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON LOWER(TRIM(a.email)) = LOWER(TRIM(b.email))\n AND a.borrower_id < b.borrower_id", "alts": [], "hints": ["Join `borrowers` to itself with two aliases. The join condition can be an expression, not just a column."], "explain": "`a.borrower_id < b.borrower_id` does two jobs: it stops an account matching itself, and it keeps only one of (A, B) and (B, A)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-cross", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For merchant `1054`, show loans per **month of 2025 and product**, with **every combination** present (12 months × 2 products = 24 rows) and 0 where there were none. Return `month` (first of month), `product`, `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH months AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n),\nproducts AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT product FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  mo.month,\n  pr.product,\n  COUNT(l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM months mo\nCROSS JOIN products pr\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', l.originated_date) AS DATE) = mo.month\n AND l.product = pr.product\n AND l.merchant_id = 1054\nGROUP BY mo.month, pr.product", "alts": [], "hints": ["Build the grid first: every month × every product with `CROSS JOIN`. Then `LEFT JOIN` the merchant's loans onto it."], "explain": "`CROSS JOIN` pairs every row with every row, the one join that's *supposed* to multiply. It's the standard way to build a complete grid before filling in counts."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-full-outer", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For every merchant with loans in **2024 or 2025**, show `merchant_id`, `loans_2024` and `loans_2025`, using 0 for a year with none.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH y24 AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, COUNT(*) AS n\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2024-01-01' AND originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n),\ny25 AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, COUNT(*) AS n\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n)\nSELECT\n  COALESCE(y24.merchant_id, y25.merchant_id) AS merchant_id,\n  COALESCE(y24.n, 0) AS loans_2024,\n  COALESCE(y25.n, 0) AS loans_2025\nFROM y24\nFULL OUTER JOIN y25\n  ON y25.merchant_id = y24.merchant_id", "alts": ["SELECT\n  merchant_id,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN originated_date >= DATE '2024-01-01' AND originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS loans_2024,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS loans_2025\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2024-01-01'\nGROUP BY merchant_id"], "hints": ["Some merchants only appear in one of the years. A `FULL OUTER JOIN` keeps unmatched rows from both sides; `COALESCE` picks whichever ID exists."], "explain": "The second version, conditional aggregation over one table, is simpler and avoids the join entirely. When both inputs come from the same table, that's usually the better answer. `FULL OUTER JOIN` earns its place when the two sides come from different tables (e.g. reconciling two systems). MySQL doesn't support it."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d3-range", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many loans were taken by a borrower who had **another loan in the 30 days before** it (strictly earlier date, at most 30 days earlier)? Return `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans l\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM loans p\n  WHERE p.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\n    AND p.originated_date < l.originated_date\n    AND p.originated_date >= l.originated_date - 30\n)", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT l.loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM loans l\nJOIN loans p\n  ON p.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\n AND p.originated_date < l.originated_date\n AND p.originated_date >= l.originated_date - 30"], "hints": ["Join `loans` to itself on the borrower, with a **range** condition on the dates instead of equality."], "explain": "Join conditions don't have to be `=`. Ranges like this are called non-equi joins. The plain self-join version can match several earlier loans per loan, hence `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)`; `EXISTS` never duplicates. `date - 30` works in PostgreSQL and DuckDB; elsewhere use your dialect's `DATEADD`."}]}, {"id": "04-subqueries-and-sets", "title": "Subqueries, CTEs and set operations", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Scalar, IN, correlated and derived-table subqueries; multi-step CTEs; UNION ALL, UNION, INTERSECT and EXCEPT.", "lesson": "05-ctes-and-subqueries", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lesson 5.\n\n## Subqueries"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-scalar", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Each product's share of **all loans** by count: `product`, `n_loans`, `share`. Use a scalar subquery for the total.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  1.0 * COUNT(*) / (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM loans) AS share\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "s4-multi-row", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM loans\nWHERE principal > (SELECT principal FROM loans WHERE borrower_id = 200364)", "explain": "A subquery used as a single value must return one row. Decide what you mean: `> (SELECT MAX(principal) ...)` for \"larger than all of them\", or `> ANY (...)` / `> ALL (...)` where supported. (Older DuckDB versions silently picked an arbitrary row instead of failing, which is worse.)", "options": [{"text": "Compares against the first of the four loans", "correct": false}, {"text": "Compares against all four (principal larger than every one)", "correct": false}, {"text": "An error: the subquery must return exactly one value", "correct": true}], "errors": true, "prompt": "Borrower 200364 has four loans. What happens?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-in", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many **applications** came from borrower accounts that have **at least one charged-off loan**? Return `n_applications`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM applications\nWHERE borrower_id IN (\n  SELECT borrower_id FROM loans WHERE status = 'charged_off'\n)", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM applications a\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM loans l\n  WHERE l.borrower_id = a.borrower_id\n    AND l.status = 'charged_off'\n)"], "hints": [], "explain": "A plain join to charged-off loans would count an application twice for a borrower with two charged-off loans. `IN` and `EXISTS` never duplicate."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-correlated", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each merchant, its **largest loan(s)**: `merchant_id`, `loan_id`, `principal`. If two loans tie for the largest, return both.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT l.merchant_id, l.loan_id, l.principal\nFROM loans l\nWHERE l.principal = (\n  SELECT MAX(l2.principal)\n  FROM loans l2\n  WHERE l2.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\n)", "alts": ["WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, loan_id, principal,\n    RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY merchant_id ORDER BY principal DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT merchant_id, loan_id, principal\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rnk = 1"], "hints": ["A correlated subquery: for each loan, compute the max principal **of that loan's merchant** and keep the loan if it matches."], "explain": "\"Keep ties\" is `RANK()` (or the correlated `MAX`), not `ROW_NUMBER()`, which would pick one arbitrarily."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-derived", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Among borrower accounts with at least one loan, how many loans does a borrower have **on average**? Return `avg_loans`. Use a subquery in `FROM`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT AVG(n_loans) AS avg_loans\nFROM (\n  SELECT borrower_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n) per_borrower", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "Aggregating twice (count per borrower, then average the counts) always needs two levels: a subquery or CTE. `AVG(COUNT(*))` is not allowed."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-chain", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Three steps: total principal per borrower account → bucket each borrower as `'under 1k'`, `'1k-5k'` (1,000 up to but not including 5,000) or `'5k+'` → per bucket, `n_borrowers` and `total_principal`. Return `bucket`, `n_borrowers`, `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH per_borrower AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, SUM(principal) AS total\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n),\nbucketed AS (\n  SELECT\n    CASE\n      WHEN total < 1000 THEN 'under 1k'\n      WHEN total < 5000 THEN '1k-5k'\n      ELSE '5k+'\n    END AS bucket,\n    total\n  FROM per_borrower\n)\nSELECT bucket, COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers, SUM(total) AS total_principal\nFROM bucketed\nGROUP BY bucket", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "Naming the bucket in its own CTE means the `CASE` is written once, and the final `GROUP BY` can use the plain column."}, {"type": "md", "text": "## Set operations"}, {"type": "predict", "id": "s4-union", "sql": "SELECT borrower_id FROM loans\nUNION\nSELECT borrower_id FROM applications", "explain": "`UNION` removes duplicates across the combined result, *including duplicates within each side*. `UNION ALL` would return all 22,573 rows.", "options": [{"text": "22,573: every loan row plus every application row", "correct": false}, {"text": "6,038", "correct": true}, {"text": "4,708: borrowers with loans", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "6,038 distinct borrower accounts appear in `applications`, and every loan's borrower also has an application. How many rows?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-union-all", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Build one timeline for borrower `200147`: every application, every loan origination, and every **successful** payment (each payment once). Return `event_type` (`'application'`, `'loan'`, `'payment'`) and `event_date` (a DATE).", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT 'application' AS event_type, CAST(applied_at AS DATE) AS event_date\nFROM applications\nWHERE borrower_id = 200147\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'loan', originated_date\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id = 200147\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'payment', CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE)\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nJOIN loans l\n  ON l.loan_id = p.loan_id\nWHERE l.borrower_id = 200147\n  AND p.status = 'succeeded'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Three `SELECT`s with the same two columns, stacked with `UNION ALL`. Payments don't have a borrower ID, so join to loans."], "explain": "Columns are matched **by position**, and the names come from the first `SELECT`. Using `UNION` here would merge two payments made on the same day into one row: a real bug."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-intersect", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts have taken **both** a Pay in 4 loan **and** a monthly loan? Return `n_borrowers`. Try `INTERSECT`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM (\n  SELECT borrower_id FROM loans WHERE product = 'pay_in_4'\n  INTERSECT\n  SELECT borrower_id FROM loans WHERE product = 'monthly'\n) both_products", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM (\n  SELECT borrower_id\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n  HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product) = 2\n) both_products"], "hints": [], "explain": "The `HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product) = 2` version works in every dialect and extends naturally to \"at least 3 of 5 categories\". `INTERSECT` is supported in PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Snowflake, Oracle and MySQL 8.0.31+; BigQuery requires `INTERSECT DISTINCT`."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "s4-except", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts **applied but never got a loan**? Use `EXCEPT`. Return `n_borrowers`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM (\n  SELECT borrower_id FROM applications\n  EXCEPT\n  SELECT borrower_id FROM loans\n) never_borrowed", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "Same answer as the `NOT EXISTS` version in lesson 4. `EXCEPT` compares whole rows and removes duplicates; Oracle spells it `MINUS`, BigQuery `EXCEPT DISTINCT`."}]}, {"id": "05-window-functions", "title": "Window functions", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Ranking and ties, top-N, dedup, LAG/LEAD, running totals, NTILE, FIRST_VALUE and LAST_VALUE, shares of a group.", "lesson": ["06-window-functions-ranking", "07-window-functions-sequences"], "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lessons 6 and 7 (the first one leans on lesson 5)."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-big-borrowers", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which borrower accounts have total principal **more than five times** the average borrower's total principal? Return `borrower_id` and `total_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH totals AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, SUM(principal) AS total_principal\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, total_principal\nFROM totals\nWHERE total_principal > 5 * (SELECT AVG(total_principal) FROM totals)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1: one row per borrower with their total. Step 2: compare each total with the average *of those totals*, not the average loan."], "explain": "\"Average borrower's total\" and \"average loan\" are different denominators. This is the same grain question as always: the average is taken over the rows of `totals`, one per borrower."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-rank-states", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Rank states by number of borrower accounts, most first, with tied states sharing a rank and no gaps. Return `state`, `n_accounts`, `state_rank`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  state,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS state_rank\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "alts": ["WITH counts AS (\n  SELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\n  FROM borrowers\n  GROUP BY state\n)\nSELECT state, n_accounts, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY n_accounts DESC) AS state_rank\nFROM counts"], "hints": ["\"Ties share, no gaps\" is `DENSE_RANK`. You can rank on `COUNT(*)` directly, because windows run after `GROUP BY`."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-second", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each product, the **second-largest** loan by principal (ties broken by the lower `loan_id`). Return `product`, `loan_id`, `principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    product, loan_id, principal,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product ORDER BY principal DESC, loan_id) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT product, loan_id, principal\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 2", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d4-count-over", "sql": "SELECT loan_id, COUNT(*) OVER () AS n\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'pay_in_4'\nLIMIT 3", "explain": "`OVER ()` is a window over every row that survived `WHERE`, which is all 3,398 Pay in 4 loans, not the whole table. `LIMIT` runs last, after the window is computed, so it doesn't shrink the count either.", "options": [{"text": "1", "correct": false}, {"text": "3", "correct": false}, {"text": "3,398", "correct": true}, {"text": "8,823", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "There are 3,398 Pay in 4 loans. What is `n` on the first row?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-lag-partition", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For borrowers `200364` and `200147`, each loan's `days_since_prev`: days since **that borrower's** previous loan (NULL for their first). Return `borrower_id`, `loan_id`, `days_since_prev`. One value is wrong. Find it and fix the window.", "starter": "SELECT\n  borrower_id,\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date - LAG(originated_date) OVER (ORDER BY borrower_id, originated_date, loan_id) AS days_since_prev\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id IN (200364, 200147)", "solution": "SELECT\n  borrower_id,\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date - LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS days_since_prev\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id IN (200364, 200147)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Look at the first loan of the second borrower. What is it being compared with?"], "explain": "Without `PARTITION BY`, `LAG` walks straight from one borrower's last loan into the next borrower's first. Sorting by borrower *looks* right but doesn't reset the window."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-share", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For borrower `200364`, each loan's share of that borrower's total principal: `loan_id`, `principal`, `share`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  principal / SUM(principal) OVER () AS share\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id = 200364", "alts": ["SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  principal,\n  principal / SUM(principal) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id) AS share\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id = 200364"], "hints": [], "explain": "With one borrower, `OVER ()` and `OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id)` agree. Remove the `WHERE` and only the partitioned version still gives per-borrower shares."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-last-per-day", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Declined applicants often retry the same day. Keep only each borrower's **last application per calendar day**, and count what's left. Return `n_applications`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    application_id,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY borrower_id, CAST(applied_at AS DATE)\n      ORDER BY applied_at DESC, application_id DESC\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM applications\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM (\n  SELECT DISTINCT borrower_id, CAST(applied_at AS DATE) AS applied_day\n  FROM applications\n) d"], "hints": ["Partition by two things: the borrower and the day (`CAST(applied_at AS DATE)`)."], "explain": "For a count alone, `COUNT(DISTINCT borrower, day)` gives the same number. The `ROW_NUMBER` version also keeps *which* application survived, which you'd need to compute a per-borrower-day approval rate."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-signups", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For 2025, new borrower accounts per month and the running total for the year: `month` (first day of the month), `n_new`, `running_total`. Sort by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', created_at) AS DATE) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_new\n  FROM borrowers\n  WHERE created_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', created_at) AS DATE)\n)\nSELECT month, n_new, SUM(n_new) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS running_total\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-top-ties", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "The **top 3 merchants by number of loans in 2025, keeping ties** (if two merchants tie for third, return both). Return `merchant_id`, `n_loans`, `rnk`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH counts AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n),\nranked AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, n_loans, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY n_loans DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM counts\n)\nSELECT merchant_id, n_loans, rnk\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rnk <= 3", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "`RANK() <= 3` keeps ties and can return more than three rows; `ROW_NUMBER() <= 3` returns exactly three; `DENSE_RANK() <= 3` returns the top three *distinct counts*, which can be many more merchants. Which one is \"right\" is a clarifying question."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-ntile", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Split **monthly** loans into **quartiles** by principal (ties broken by `loan_id`). For each quartile: `quartile`, `n_loans`, `min_principal`, `max_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH q AS (\n  SELECT principal, NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY principal, loan_id) AS quartile\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE product = 'monthly'\n)\nSELECT quartile, COUNT(*) AS n_loans, MIN(principal) AS min_principal, MAX(principal) AS max_principal\nFROM q\nGROUP BY quartile", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "`NTILE(n)` deals rows into n buckets as evenly as possible. Without the `loan_id` tie-breaker, identical principals on a boundary can land in either bucket, and the answer changes from run to run."}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d4-last-value", "sql": "SELECT x, LAST_VALUE(x) OVER (ORDER BY x) AS lv\nFROM (SELECT 1 AS x UNION ALL SELECT 2 UNION ALL SELECT 3) t", "explain": "With an `ORDER BY`, the default frame ends at the **current row**, so the \"last value\" so far is the row itself. For the true last value write `LAST_VALUE(x) OVER (ORDER BY x ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING)`, or flip the order and use `FIRST_VALUE`.", "options": [{"text": "3", "correct": false}, {"text": "1", "correct": true}, {"text": "NULL", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What is `lv` on the row where `x = 1`?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-first-value", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For **repeat** loans (a borrower's second loan or later), count by the product of the borrower's **first** loan and the product of this loan. Return `first_product`, `product`, `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH seq AS (\n  SELECT\n    product,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn,\n    FIRST_VALUE(product) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS first_product\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT first_product, product, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM seq\nWHERE rn > 1\nGROUP BY first_product, product", "alts": [], "hints": ["`FIRST_VALUE(product) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY ...)` puts the first loan's product on every one of the borrower's rows."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-lead", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For borrower `200147`, each loan with the date of their **next** loan and the days until it: `loan_id`, `originated_date`, `next_loan_date`, `days_to_next`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date,\n  LEAD(originated_date) OVER (ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS next_loan_date,\n  LEAD(originated_date) OVER (ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) - originated_date AS days_to_next\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id = 200147", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "With a single borrower, no `PARTITION BY` is needed; drop the `WHERE` and you'd need `PARTITION BY borrower_id`. The last loan's `next_loan_date` is NULL."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-running-borrower", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For borrower `200364`, a running total of principal borrowed over time: `loan_id`, `originated_date`, `principal`, `cumulative_principal`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  loan_id,\n  originated_date,\n  principal,\n  SUM(principal) OVER (ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS cumulative_principal\nFROM loans\nWHERE borrower_id = 200364", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d4-share-of-group", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Each merchant's share of its **cleaned** category's loans (lower case, trimmed; skip NULL categories): `merchant_id`, `category`, `n_loans`, `share_of_category`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  LOWER(TRIM(m.category)) AS category,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  1.0 * COUNT(*) / SUM(COUNT(*)) OVER (PARTITION BY LOWER(TRIM(m.category))) AS share_of_category\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE m.category IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id, LOWER(TRIM(m.category))", "alts": [], "hints": ["Group to one row per merchant, then divide by a window sum of those counts partitioned by category: `SUM(COUNT(*)) OVER (PARTITION BY ...)`."], "explain": ""}]}, {"id": "06-dates", "title": "Dates and timestamps", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Timestamp ranges and equality, truncation (month, quarter, week), extraction, date arithmetic, month ends, weekends.", "lesson": "08-dates-and-time", "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These drills go with lesson 8."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-half-open", "kind": "fix", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts were created in **November 2025**? Return `n_accounts`. The count is too low. Fix it.", "starter": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE created_at BETWEEN DATE '2025-11-01' AND DATE '2025-11-30'", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nWHERE created_at >= DATE '2025-11-01'\n  AND created_at < DATE '2025-12-01'", "alts": [], "hints": ["`created_at` is a timestamp. What time of day does `DATE '2025-11-30'` stand for?"], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-hour", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Applications by **hour of day** (0–23) of `applied_at`: `hour`, `n_applications`, sorted by hour.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(HOUR FROM applied_at) AS hour,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(HOUR FROM applied_at)\nORDER BY hour", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "With real data you'd also ask which **time zone** the timestamps are in. If they're UTC, US evening activity lands on the next calendar day, which shifts daily and hourly counts."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-quarter", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Loans originated in 2025, by quarter: `quarter` (first day of the quarter) and `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE) AS quarter,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE)", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d5-month-end", "sql": "SELECT CAST(DATE '2025-01-31' + INTERVAL '1 month' AS DATE) AS d", "explain": "Month arithmetic clamps to the last day of the target month. (R's `as.Date(\"2025-01-31\") + months(1)` gives `NA`; `%m+%` clamps like SQL does.) Payment schedules hit this every month for loans originated on the 29th–31st.", "options": [{"text": "2025-03-03", "correct": false}, {"text": "2025-02-28", "correct": true}, {"text": "2025-02-31", "correct": false}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "What date does this produce?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-account-age", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 1, "prompt": "Average account age in **days** as of 2025-12-31, by state: `state`, `avg_age_days`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  state,\n  AVG(DATE '2025-12-31' - CAST(created_at AS DATE)) AS avg_age_days\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state", "alts": [], "hints": ["Cast the timestamp to a date first, so the subtraction gives a number of days rather than an interval."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-last-30", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many loans were originated in the **30 days ending on 2025-12-31** (2 December through 31 December)? Return `n_loans`. Use date arithmetic from the snapshot date rather than typing `2025-12-02`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date > DATE '2025-12-31' - INTERVAL '30 days'", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-12-02'"], "hints": ["`DATE '2025-12-31' - INTERVAL '30 days'` is 1 December. \"After 1 December\" starts on the 2nd."], "explain": "Off-by-one errors on windows like this are common. Say the boundary out loud (\"strictly after December 1st, so 30 days including the 31st\") and the interviewer can confirm."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-on-time", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For successful payments made in **2025**, what share were made **on or before** the installment's due date? Count each payment once. Return `on_time_share`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  AVG(CASE WHEN CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) <= i.due_date THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS on_time_share\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nJOIN installments i\n  ON i.loan_id = p.loan_id\n AND i.installment_number = p.installment_number\nWHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n  AND p.paid_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND p.paid_at < DATE '2026-01-01'", "alts": [], "hints": ["Join to installments on both key columns to get the due date, then compare the payment **date** with it."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "predict", "id": "d5-ts-equals", "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM applications\nWHERE applied_at = DATE '2025-12-31'", "explain": "`applied_at` is a timestamp; the date becomes `2025-12-31 00:00:00`, and only an application made at exactly midnight would match. Use a range (`>= '2025-12-31' AND < '2026-01-01'`) or compare dates: `CAST(applied_at AS DATE) = DATE '2025-12-31'`.", "options": [{"text": "All applications made that day", "correct": false}, {"text": "0", "correct": true}, {"text": "An error", "correct": false}], "errors": false, "prompt": "Plenty of applications were made on 31 December 2025. What does this return?"}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-week", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Loans per **week** in December 2025 (weeks starting Monday): `week_start` and `n_loans`, sorted by week.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('week', originated_date) AS DATE) AS week_start,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-12-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('week', originated_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY week_start", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "The first \"week\" starts on Monday 1 December, and the last (29–31 December) is partial, so it looks low for that reason alone. Partial periods at either end of a range are a classic source of fake trends. Week start days vary by dialect: Monday in PostgreSQL, DuckDB and BigQuery's `ISOWEEK`; Sunday in some others."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-weekend", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "What share of applications were made on a **weekend** (Saturday or Sunday)? Return `weekend_share`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  AVG(CASE WHEN EXTRACT(DOW FROM applied_at) IN (0, 6) THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS weekend_share\nFROM applications", "alts": [], "hints": ["With `EXTRACT(DOW ...)`, Sunday is 0 and Saturday is 6."], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-floor-zero", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For successful payments made in **December 2025** (each counted once), the average days late, treating **early payments as 0** rather than negative. Return `avg_days_late`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  AVG(GREATEST(CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) - i.due_date, 0)) AS avg_days_late\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nJOIN installments i\n  ON i.loan_id = p.loan_id\n AND i.installment_number = p.installment_number\nWHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n  AND p.paid_at >= DATE '2025-12-01'\n  AND p.paid_at < DATE '2026-01-01'", "alts": ["SELECT\n  AVG(CASE WHEN CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) > i.due_date THEN CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) - i.due_date ELSE 0 END) AS avg_days_late\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nJOIN installments i\n  ON i.loan_id = p.loan_id\n AND i.installment_number = p.installment_number\nWHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n  AND p.paid_at >= DATE '2025-12-01'\n  AND p.paid_at < DATE '2026-01-01'"], "hints": [], "explain": "`GREATEST(x, 0)` floors a value at zero. It exists in PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, BigQuery and DuckDB (SQL Server only since 2022); the `CASE` version works everywhere."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "d5-last-day", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many installments fall due on the **last day of a month**? Return `n_installments`.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_installments\nFROM installments\nWHERE due_date = CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', due_date) + INTERVAL '1 month' - INTERVAL '1 day' AS DATE)", "alts": ["SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_installments\nFROM installments\nWHERE due_date = LAST_DAY(due_date)"], "hints": ["Last day of the month = first day of the month, plus one month, minus one day."], "explain": "`LAST_DAY()` exists in MySQL, Snowflake, Oracle and DuckDB (SQL Server: `EOMONTH()`); the truncate-add-subtract version works in PostgreSQL too."}]}, {"id": "07-classic-patterns", "title": "Classic interview patterns", "minutes": 30, "summary": "The recurring shapes of analyst SQL questions — streaks, retention, A-then-B, Nth event, histograms, pivots, consecutive events, year-over-year, concentration.", "lesson": ["09-lending-metrics", "10-interview-craft"], "date": "", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "These combine techniques from several lessons. Each one is a *pattern* you'll recognise in other questions. Read the explanation after solving, even if you got it right: the point is to name the pattern."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-nth", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Each borrower account's **third** loan (by date, ties by `loan_id`): `borrower_id`, `loan_id`, `originated_date`. Borrowers with fewer than three loans don't appear.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH seq AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id, loan_id, originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, loan_id, originated_date\nFROM seq\nWHERE rn = 3", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "**Pattern: Nth event per entity.** `ROW_NUMBER` per entity, filter `rn = N`. \"First\", \"latest\" and \"second purchase\" are all this shape."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-a-then-b", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts took a **Pay in 4 loan as their first loan** and **later took a monthly loan**? Return `n_borrowers`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH seq AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id, product, originated_date, loan_id,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n),\nfirsts AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, originated_date, loan_id\n  FROM seq\n  WHERE rn = 1 AND product = 'pay_in_4'\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM firsts f\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM seq s\n  WHERE s.borrower_id = f.borrower_id\n    AND s.product = 'monthly'\n    AND s.rn > 1\n)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1: find each borrower's first loan, keep the Pay in 4 ones. Step 2: does any later loan of theirs have product `monthly`?"], "explain": "**Pattern: did A, then B.** Find the A event per entity, then check for a B event after it (`EXISTS` with an ordering condition). Funnels (\"viewed, then applied, then took a loan\") chain this."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-histogram", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "A histogram of loan sizes in **500-wide buckets**, with everything from 5,000 up in one top bucket. Return `bucket_start` (0, 500, 1000, …, 5000) and `n_loans`, sorted.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  LEAST(FLOOR(principal / 500) * 500, 5000) AS bucket_start,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY LEAST(FLOOR(principal / 500) * 500, 5000)\nORDER BY bucket_start", "alts": ["SELECT\n  CASE WHEN principal >= 5000 THEN 5000 ELSE FLOOR(principal / 500) * 500 END AS bucket_start,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY CASE WHEN principal >= 5000 THEN 5000 ELSE FLOOR(principal / 500) * 500 END\nORDER BY bucket_start"], "hints": [], "explain": "**Pattern: binning.** `FLOOR(x / width) * width` gives each value's bucket start. Cap the tail so a few huge values don't create dozens of near-empty buckets."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-pivot", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each month of 2025, loans by product **as columns**: `month` (first of month), `n_pay_in_4`, `n_monthly`. Sort by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN product = 'pay_in_4' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_pay_in_4,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN product = 'monthly' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_monthly\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "**Pattern: long to wide.** One conditional aggregate per output column. It's `pivot_wider()` written by hand, and it works in every dialect."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-yoy", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Year-over-year: for each month number (1–12), loans in 2024, loans in 2025, and `yoy_growth` (2025 ÷ 2024 − 1). Return `month_num`, `loans_2024`, `loans_2025`, `yoy_growth`, sorted by month.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month_num,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) = 2024 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS loans_2024,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) = 2025 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS loans_2025,\n  1.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) = 2025 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)\n      / SUM(CASE WHEN EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) = 2024 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) - 1 AS yoy_growth\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2024-01-01'\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date)\nORDER BY month_num", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": "**Pattern: same period, different year.** Here `EXTRACT(MONTH ...)` merging years is exactly what you want, with the year split out into columns. Compare with `LAG(n, 12)` over a monthly series, which breaks if any month is missing."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-retention", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For accounts created in 2025 **on or before 1 December** (so everyone had 30 days to act), by **quarter of account creation**: `quarter`, `n_accounts`, and `share_loan_30d`: the share that took their first loan **within 30 days** of creating the account.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH first_loan AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, MIN(originated_date) AS first_loan_date\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n)\nSELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', b.created_at) AS DATE) AS quarter,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN f.first_loan_date - CAST(b.created_at AS DATE) <= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS share_loan_30d\nFROM borrowers b\nLEFT JOIN first_loan f\n  ON f.borrower_id = b.borrower_id\nWHERE b.created_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND b.created_at < DATE '2025-12-02'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', b.created_at) AS DATE)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Start from **borrowers** (the cohort), LEFT JOIN each one's first loan date, and average a \"within 30 days\" flag. Accounts with no loan get NULL, which the `CASE` turns into 0."], "explain": "**Pattern: cohort retention/activation.** Define the cohort, attach the first qualifying event, measure within a fixed window, and **exclude members who haven't had the full window yet**. That last step is the one people forget; it's the same maturity idea as vintages."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-streak", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Some borrowers took loans in several **consecutive calendar months**. Find borrower accounts whose longest such run is **3 months or more**: `borrower_id`, `longest_streak`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH months AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT\n    borrower_id,\n    EXTRACT(YEAR FROM originated_date) * 12 + EXTRACT(MONTH FROM originated_date) AS month_num\n  FROM loans\n),\ngrouped AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    month_num,\n    month_num - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY month_num) AS grp\n  FROM months\n),\nstreaks AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, grp, COUNT(*) AS streak_length\n  FROM grouped\n  GROUP BY borrower_id, grp\n)\nSELECT borrower_id, MAX(streak_length) AS longest_streak\nFROM streaks\nGROUP BY borrower_id\nHAVING MAX(streak_length) >= 3", "alts": [], "hints": ["Turn each month into a number (`year * 12 + month`), one row per borrower-month.", "For consecutive months, `month_num - ROW_NUMBER()` is **constant**. Group by it to find each run."], "explain": "**Pattern: gaps and islands.** Within a run of consecutive values, value minus row number stays the same, so it labels the \"island\". It answers \"longest streak\", \"consecutive days active\" and \"periods of continuous delinquency\". Well worth memorising."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-consecutive", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many loans have **two installments in a row** that were each paid **after** their due date? Treat an installment's paid date as the date of its **last** successful payment (each payment counted once), and ignore installments with no successful payment. Return `n_loans`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH paid AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, MAX(CAST(paid_at AS DATE)) AS paid_date\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n),\nflags AS (\n  SELECT\n    i.loan_id,\n    i.installment_number,\n    CASE WHEN pd.paid_date > i.due_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS late\n  FROM installments i\n  JOIN paid pd\n    ON pd.loan_id = i.loan_id\n   AND pd.installment_number = i.installment_number\n),\nseq AS (\n  SELECT\n    loan_id,\n    late,\n    LAG(late) OVER (PARTITION BY loan_id ORDER BY installment_number) AS prev_late\n  FROM flags\n)\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT loan_id) AS n_loans\nFROM seq\nWHERE late = 1 AND prev_late = 1", "alts": [], "hints": ["Step 1: a paid date per installment. Step 2: a late flag per installment. Step 3: `LAG` the flag within each loan and look for 1 followed by 1."], "explain": "**Pattern: consecutive events.** Flag each event, `LAG` the flag, and look for the combination. For runs longer than two, switch to gaps and islands."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "p-pareto", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "What is the **smallest number of merchants**, taking the biggest first, that together account for **at least half** of all principal lent? Return `n_merchants`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH totals AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, SUM(principal) AS total\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n),\nrunning AS (\n  SELECT\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, merchant_id) AS rn,\n    SUM(total) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, merchant_id ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS cum_total,\n    SUM(total) OVER () AS grand_total\n  FROM totals\n)\nSELECT MIN(rn) AS n_merchants\nFROM running\nWHERE cum_total >= 0.5 * grand_total", "alts": [], "hints": ["Rank merchants by total, compute a running total down that ranking, and find the first rank where it reaches half the grand total."], "explain": "**Pattern: concentration (Pareto).** A running share over a sorted list. The same shape answers \"what share of losses comes from the top 10% of merchants?\""}]}], "daily": [{"id": "2026-10-08", "title": "Daily set 1", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Five drills. Check each one, then copy your results at the bottom.", "lesson": "", "date": "2026-10-08", "interview": false, "blocks": [{"type": "note", "title": "How daily sets work", "text": "Do the five drills (check each one as usual), then press **Copy results** at the bottom and send me the text, or a photo of it. I'll review your SQL, not just pass/fail, and the next set will repeat what you got right, re-test what you missed in a new form, and add something new.\n\nDon't reveal solutions unless you're truly stuck: the review is most useful when it sees your own attempt."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy1-score-bands", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Approval rates by credit-score band. Bands: `'no score'` (no credit file), `'below 600'`, `'600-699'`, `'700+'`.\n\nReturn `score_band`, `n_applications` and `approval_rate` (share of applications approved, as a decimal).", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH banded AS (\n  SELECT\n    CASE\n      WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n      WHEN credit_score < 600 THEN 'below 600'\n      WHEN credit_score < 700 THEN '600-699'\n      ELSE '700+'\n    END AS score_band,\n    decision\n  FROM applications\n)\nSELECT\n  score_band,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM banded\nGROUP BY score_band", "alts": [], "hints": ["Put the `NULL` check **first**. `credit_score < 600` is unknown for a NULL score, so a NULL would fall through every `WHEN` to the `ELSE`.", "Approval rate is the mean of a dummy: `AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`."], "explain": "`CASE` takes the **first** `WHEN` that's true, so order matters: the `NULL` branch goes first, then the bands from low to high, with `ELSE` catching the rest. Labelling in a CTE and grouping on the label keeps the `CASE` written once. Note the 'no score' approval rate (about 25%): a missing value is information, not something to drop."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy1-electronics-zero", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Every **Electronics** merchant with its number of loans originated in **2025**, including merchants with none. Category text is messy (case, trailing spaces).\n\nReturn `merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `n_loans_2025`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH electronics_merchants AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, merchant_name\n  FROM merchants\n  WHERE LOWER(TRIM(category)) = 'electronics'\n),\nloans_2025 AS (\n  SELECT merchant_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n    AND originated_date < DATE '2026-01-01'\n  GROUP BY merchant_id\n)\nSELECT\n  electronics_merchants.merchant_id,\n  electronics_merchants.merchant_name,\n  COALESCE(loans_2025.n_loans, 0) AS n_loans_2025\nFROM electronics_merchants\nLEFT JOIN loans_2025\n  ON loans_2025.merchant_id = electronics_merchants.merchant_id", "alts": ["SELECT\n  merchants.merchant_id,\n  merchants.merchant_name,\n  COUNT(loans.loan_id) AS n_loans_2025\nFROM merchants\nLEFT JOIN loans\n  ON loans.merchant_id = merchants.merchant_id\n AND loans.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n AND loans.originated_date < DATE '2026-01-01'\nWHERE LOWER(TRIM(merchants.category)) = 'electronics'\nGROUP BY merchants.merchant_id, merchants.merchant_name"], "hints": ["Start `FROM` the merchants (every one must appear), clean the category with `LOWER(TRIM(...))`, and count 2025 loans per merchant in their own CTE.", "After the `LEFT JOIN`, merchants with no 2025 loans have `NULL` counts. `COALESCE(..., 0)` turns them into zeros."], "explain": "Two of the 22 Electronics merchants had no 2025 loans; they're the test. Either pre-filter loans in a CTE and `LEFT JOIN` (then `COALESCE`), or put the date condition in the `ON` clause and count `loans.loan_id`, not `*`, so a merchant with no match counts 0. Putting the date filter in `WHERE` would silently drop those two merchants."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy1-never-paid", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many borrower accounts have **at least one loan** but have **never had a successful payment** on any of their loans?\n\nReturn `n_borrowers`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH paid_borrowers AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT loans.borrower_id\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN payments ON payments.loan_id = loans.loan_id\n  WHERE payments.status = 'succeeded'\n)\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT loans.borrower_id) AS n_borrowers\nFROM loans\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM paid_borrowers\n  WHERE paid_borrowers.borrower_id = loans.borrower_id\n)", "alts": [], "hints": ["Payments link to loans, and loans link to borrowers. First find the borrowers who **have** paid (a CTE), then keep the borrowers with loans who are **not** in it.", "`WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM paid_borrowers WHERE paid_borrowers.borrower_id = loans.borrower_id)`. Count `DISTINCT` borrowers, because a borrower can have several loans."], "explain": "Anti-join in two steps: define the \"has paid\" population, then `NOT EXISTS` against it. Only succeeded payments count as paying. `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)` matters because the outer query runs over loans, and one borrower can have several."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy1-behind", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Monthly loans originated in **Q1 2025**: how many are **behind** at the snapshot, meaning less has been collected (successful payments) than was due on installments with `due_date` on or before `2025-12-31`? And by how much in total?\n\nReturn `n_loans_behind` and `total_shortfall`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH q1_monthly AS (\n  SELECT loan_id\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE product = 'monthly'\n    AND originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n    AND originated_date < DATE '2025-04-01'\n),\ndue AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount_due) AS total_due\n  FROM installments\n  WHERE due_date <= DATE '2025-12-31'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nclean_payments AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments\n),\ncollected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS total_collected\n  FROM clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n),\nper_loan AS (\n  SELECT\n    q1_monthly.loan_id,\n    due.total_due,\n    COALESCE(collected.total_collected, 0) AS total_collected\n  FROM q1_monthly\n  JOIN due ON due.loan_id = q1_monthly.loan_id\n  LEFT JOIN collected ON collected.loan_id = q1_monthly.loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans_behind,\n  SUM(total_due - total_collected) AS total_shortfall\nFROM per_loan\nWHERE total_collected < total_due", "alts": [], "hints": ["Two \"many\" tables hang off each loan: installments and payments. Joining both to loans at once multiplies rows (every installment × every payment). Aggregate **each one to one row per loan in its own CTE**, then join.", "Payments have exact duplicate rows and failed attempts: `SELECT DISTINCT *` first, then keep `status = 'succeeded'`. A loan with no successful payments needs `COALESCE(..., 0)` after the `LEFT JOIN`."], "explain": "The answer is 30 loans. Joining loans → installments → payments in one go and summing gives **242**: each payment is counted once per installment row and each installment once per payment row, so the totals are nonsense. One CTE per \"many\" table, each collapsed to the loan grain, then join: that's the rule."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy1-gap-fix", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 1, "prompt": "For each loan that is **not** a borrower account's first loan, find the number of days since that account's previous loan. Then summarise by the product of the later loan.\n\nReturn `product`, `n_repeat_loans` and `avg_days_since_previous`.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH sequenced AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    product,\n    originated_date,\n    LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS previous_loan_date\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_repeat_loans,\n  AVG(originated_date - previous_loan_date) AS avg_days_since_previous\nFROM sequenced\nWHERE previous_loan_date IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": ["For each loan you need the same account's previous loan date: a window function that looks one row back within each borrower.", "`LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id)`. A first loan gets `NULL`, so filter those out. `date - date` gives days."], "explain": "`LAG` within each borrower, ordered by date, then filter out first loans (their `LAG` is `NULL`) and average the difference. The `ORDER BY` inside `OVER` is essential: without it \"previous\" is whatever order the engine reads the rows in, and the averages come out wildly wrong without any error. Add `loan_id` as a tie-breaker for two loans on the same day. In Postgres, as here, `date - date` is a whole number of days."}]}, {"id": "2026-10-09", "title": "Daily set 2", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Five questions, asked the way an interviewer would ask them. Check each one, then copy your results at the bottom.", "lesson": "", "date": "2026-10-09", "interview": true, "blocks": [{"type": "note", "title": "How this round works", "text": "No hints and no solutions: treat it like the live round. Each question comes with the clarifications an interviewer would give if you asked. If you make any other assumption, write it as a comment at the top of your query (`-- assuming ...`). I'll review the assumptions as well as the SQL."}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy2-1", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "\"Some applicants leave the income field blank. For applications submitted in 2025, how many came from borrowers who reported an income and how many from borrowers who didn't, and how do approval rates compare?\"\n\n**Columns:** `income_reported` (`'yes'` or `'no'`), `n_applications`, `approval_rate`\n\n**If you asked, the interviewer would clarify:**\n- \"Didn't report\" means `annual_income` is missing on the borrower's account.\n- 2025 means submitted any time during calendar 2025.\n- Approval rate is the share of applications approved, as a decimal.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH applications_2025 AS (\n  SELECT\n    applications.decision,\n    CASE WHEN borrowers.annual_income IS NULL THEN 'no' ELSE 'yes' END AS income_reported\n  FROM applications\n  JOIN borrowers ON borrowers.borrower_id = applications.borrower_id\n  WHERE applications.applied_at >= TIMESTAMP '2025-01-01'\n    AND applications.applied_at < TIMESTAMP '2026-01-01'\n)\nSELECT\n  income_reported,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM applications_2025\nGROUP BY income_reported", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy2-2", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "\"Collections wants a quick read on our newest Pay in 4 cohort. For Pay in 4 loans originated in November 2025, look at every installment that has come due as of the snapshot. How many are there, and what share have been paid in full?\"\n\n**Columns:** `n_installments_due`, `share_fully_paid`\n\n**If you asked, the interviewer would clarify:**\n- \"Come due\" means `due_date` on or before 2025-12-31.\n- \"Paid in full\" means the successful payments applied to that installment add up to at least its `amount_due`. Some installments were paid in more than one payment.\n- Exact duplicate rows in `payments` are a known pipeline error: count them once.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH november_pay_in_4 AS (\n  SELECT loan_id\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE product = 'pay_in_4'\n    AND originated_date >= DATE '2025-11-01'\n    AND originated_date < DATE '2025-12-01'\n),\ninstallments_due AS (\n  SELECT installments.loan_id, installments.installment_number, installments.amount_due\n  FROM installments\n  JOIN november_pay_in_4 ON november_pay_in_4.loan_id = installments.loan_id\n  WHERE installments.due_date <= DATE '2025-12-31'\n),\nclean_payments AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments\n),\npaid_per_installment AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, SUM(amount) AS amount_paid\n  FROM clean_payments\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_installments_due,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN COALESCE(paid_per_installment.amount_paid, 0) >= installments_due.amount_due THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS share_fully_paid\nFROM installments_due\nLEFT JOIN paid_per_installment\n  ON paid_per_installment.loan_id = installments_due.loan_id\n AND paid_per_installment.installment_number = installments_due.installment_number", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy2-3", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "\"We're comparing ticket sizes across merchant categories. What's the average loan size in each category?\"\n\n**Columns:** `category`, `n_loans`, `avg_principal`\n\n**If you asked, the interviewer would clarify:**\n- Average loan size means the average principal **per loan**.\n- Categories are typed inconsistently: report them in lowercase with no stray spaces (`'home & furniture'`).\n- Leave out merchants with no category.", "starter": "", "solution": "SELECT\n  LOWER(TRIM(merchants.category)) AS category,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(loans.principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans\nJOIN merchants ON merchants.merchant_id = loans.merchant_id\nWHERE merchants.category IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(merchants.category))", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy2-4", "kind": "write", "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "\"How often do customers come back quickly? Of all repeat loans, what share were taken within 30 days of the same account's previous loan? Split it by the product of the repeat loan.\"\n\n**Columns:** `product`, `n_repeat_loans`, `share_within_30_days`\n\n**If you asked, the interviewer would clarify:**\n- A repeat loan is any loan that isn't the account's first.\n- Work at the account level (`borrower_id`); don't try to merge duplicate people.\n- \"Within 30 days\" means 30 days or fewer after the previous loan's `originated_date`.\n- If an account took two loans on the same day, treat the one with the lower `loan_id` as earlier.\n- The share is per loan: every repeat loan counts once.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH sequenced AS (\n  SELECT\n    product,\n    originated_date,\n    LAG(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS previous_loan_date\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_repeat_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN originated_date - previous_loan_date <= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS share_within_30_days\nFROM sequenced\nWHERE previous_loan_date IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}, {"type": "exercise", "id": "dy2-5", "kind": "write", "ordered": true, "digits": 4, "prompt": "\"Which merchants have the worst charge-off rates? Only look at merchants with enough volume to take seriously: at least 50 loans. Give me the top five.\"\n\n**Columns:** `merchant_name`, `n_loans`, `charge_off_rate`, worst first\n\n**If you asked, the interviewer would clarify:**\n- Charge-off rate is the share of a merchant's loans with `status = 'charged_off'`, across all time.", "starter": "", "solution": "WITH merchant_loans AS (\n  SELECT\n    merchants.merchant_name,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n    AVG(CASE WHEN loans.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS charge_off_rate\n  FROM loans\n  JOIN merchants ON merchants.merchant_id = loans.merchant_id\n  GROUP BY merchants.merchant_id, merchants.merchant_name\n  HAVING COUNT(*) >= 50\n)\nSELECT merchant_name, n_loans, charge_off_rate\nFROM merchant_loans\nORDER BY charge_off_rate DESC\nLIMIT 5", "alts": [], "hints": [], "explain": ""}]}], "problems": [{"type": "problem", "id": "t1-01", "title": "Loans by status", "tier": 1, "minutes": 3, "topics": ["group by"], "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many loans are in each status? Sort from the most common status to the least.", "columns": "`status`, `n_loans`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY status\nORDER BY n_loans DESC", "alts": [], "explain": "Grain: one row per status. `ORDER BY` can use the `SELECT` alias because it runs after `SELECT`.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-02", "title": "Ten largest loans of 2025", "tier": 1, "minutes": 4, "topics": ["order by", "dates"], "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "List the 10 largest loans (by principal) originated in 2025, largest first. If two loans have the same principal, list the lower `loan_id` first.", "columns": "`loan_id`, `originated_date`, `principal`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["Filter with a half-open range: `>= DATE '2025-01-01' AND < DATE '2026-01-01'`.", "Sort by two columns, `principal DESC, loan_id`, then `LIMIT 10`."], "solution": "SELECT loan_id, originated_date, principal\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND originated_date < DATE '2026-01-01'\nORDER BY principal DESC, loan_id\nLIMIT 10", "alts": [], "explain": "The tie-breaker makes the result deterministic. Without it, two runs could return different rows at the boundary.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-03", "title": "How much income is missing?", "tier": 1, "minutes": 4, "topics": ["null", "aggregation"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "What share of borrower accounts didn't report an income? Return the total number of accounts, the number with no income, and the share as a decimal (e.g. 0.123).", "columns": "`n_accounts`, `n_missing_income`, `share_missing`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["`COUNT(*)` counts rows; `COUNT(annual_income)` counts non-NULL incomes.", "Watch out for integer division when computing the share."], "solution": "SELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  COUNT(*) - COUNT(annual_income) AS n_missing_income,\n  1.0 * (COUNT(*) - COUNT(annual_income)) / COUNT(*) AS share_missing\nFROM borrowers", "alts": ["SELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN annual_income IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_missing_income,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN annual_income IS NULL THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS share_missing\nFROM borrowers"], "explain": "`COUNT(*) - COUNT(col)` is a neat idiom for counting NULLs. In PostgreSQL the share needs `1.0 *`, because both counts are integers.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-04", "title": "Product mix", "tier": 1, "minutes": 3, "topics": ["group by"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each product, return the number of loans, total principal, and average principal (rounded to 2 decimals).", "columns": "`product`, `n_loans`, `total_principal`, `avg_principal`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(principal) AS total_principal,\n  ROUND(AVG(principal), 2) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-05", "title": "Successful payments by method", "tier": 1, "minutes": 5, "topics": ["null", "count distinct"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many distinct successful payments were made with each payment method? Show missing methods as `'unknown'`. Remember that some payment rows were loaded twice.", "columns": "`payment_method`, `n_payments`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["`COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown')` in both `SELECT` and `GROUP BY`.", "Count distinct `payment_id`s rather than rows."], "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') AS payment_method,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT payment_id) AS n_payments\nFROM payments\nWHERE status = 'succeeded'\nGROUP BY COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown')", "alts": [], "explain": "`COUNT(DISTINCT payment_id)` handles the duplicate rows without a separate de-duplication step. That's fine for counting; for summing amounts you'd de-duplicate first.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-06", "title": "Applications by score band", "tier": 1, "minutes": 5, "topics": ["case", "null"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Count applications by credit score band: `'<600'`, `'600-659'`, `'660-719'`, `'720+'`, and `'no score'` when the score is missing.", "columns": "`score_band`, `n_applications`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["Test for NULL first in the `CASE`. Otherwise a NULL score falls through to the `ELSE`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n    WHEN credit_score < 600 THEN '<600'\n    WHEN credit_score < 660 THEN '600-659'\n    WHEN credit_score < 720 THEN '660-719'\n    ELSE '720+'\n  END AS score_band,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "explain": "If the `CASE` ended in `ELSE '720+'` with no NULL branch, every unscored application would be labelled 720+. A silent and very wrong result.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-07", "title": "States with the most accounts", "tier": 1, "minutes": 3, "topics": ["having"], "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which states have more than 300 borrower accounts? Sort by number of accounts, highest first.", "columns": "`state`, `n_accounts`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT state, COUNT(*) AS n_accounts\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY state\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 300\nORDER BY n_accounts DESC", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t1-08", "title": "Interest-free share by term", "tier": 1, "minutes": 5, "topics": ["conditional aggregation"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For **monthly** loans, what share are interest-free (APR of 0) at each term length?", "columns": "`term_months`, `n_loans`, `zero_apr_share`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["The share is the average of a 0/1 flag: `AVG(CASE WHEN apr = 0 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  term_months,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN apr = 0 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS zero_apr_share\nFROM loans\nWHERE product = 'monthly'\nGROUP BY term_months", "alts": [], "explain": "24-month loans are never interest-free here: 0% promotions only apply up to 12 months.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-01", "title": "Principal by merchant category (cleaned)", "tier": 2, "minutes": 7, "topics": ["joins", "data cleaning"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Merchant categories are inconsistently typed: some are upper case, lower case, or have trailing spaces, and a couple are missing. Report total loans and principal by **cleaned** category: lower-case and trimmed, with missing categories shown as `'unknown'`.", "columns": "`category`, `n_loans`, `total_principal`", "clarify": "- *Should 'Home & Furniture' and 'HOME & FURNITURE' be one category?* Yes, that's the point of cleaning.\n- *What casing should the output use?* Lower case is fine.", "hints": ["Look at `SELECT DISTINCT category FROM merchants` first.", "`COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), 'unknown')`. The order of the functions doesn't matter much, but the `COALESCE` must be outermost."], "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), 'unknown') AS category,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nGROUP BY COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), 'unknown')", "alts": [], "explain": "In an interview, cleaning inline like this is fine. In production you'd fix it upstream or keep a mapping table, and it's worth saying so.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-02", "title": "Approval rate by score band", "tier": 2, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["case", "conditional aggregation"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "What's the approval rate in each credit score band? Use the bands `'<600'`, `'600-659'`, `'660-719'`, `'720+'` and `'no score'`.", "columns": "`score_band`, `n_applications`, `approval_rate`", "clarify": "- *Application-level or borrower-level?* Application-level: every application counts, including quick retries.", "hints": ["Same `CASE` as the score band warm-up, plus `AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n    WHEN credit_score < 600 THEN '<600'\n    WHEN credit_score < 660 THEN '600-659'\n    WHEN credit_score < 720 THEN '660-719'\n    ELSE '720+'\n  END AS score_band,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-03", "title": "Average loan size by state", "tier": 2, "minutes": 5, "topics": ["joins"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each borrower state, how many loans were made and what was the average principal?", "columns": "`state`, `n_loans`, `avg_principal`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT\n  b.state,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(l.principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN borrowers b\n  ON b.borrower_id = l.borrower_id\nGROUP BY b.state", "alts": [], "explain": "Loans → borrowers is many-to-one, so the join doesn't change the grain, and `COUNT(*)` still counts loans.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-04", "title": "Merchants with no 2025 loans", "tier": 2, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["anti join", "dates"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants that were onboarded **before 2025** had **no loans originated in 2025**?", "columns": "`merchant_id`, `merchant_name`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["That's an anti join with a date condition inside it.", "`WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM loans l WHERE l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01')`."], "solution": "SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nWHERE m.onboarded_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND NOT EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1\n    FROM loans l\n    WHERE l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n      AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  )", "alts": ["SELECT m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nFROM merchants m\nLEFT JOIN loans l\n  ON l.merchant_id = m.merchant_id\n AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nWHERE m.onboarded_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND l.loan_id IS NULL"], "explain": "In the LEFT JOIN version, the 2025 condition **must** be in `ON`. In `WHERE` it would contradict `l.loan_id IS NULL` and return nothing.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-05", "title": "Repeat borrowers", "tier": 2, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["cte", "conditional aggregation"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Among borrower accounts with at least one loan, what share have two or more loans?", "columns": "`n_borrowers`, `n_repeat`, `repeat_share`", "clarify": "- *Accounts or people?* Accounts (`borrower_id`). Ignore duplicate accounts for now.", "hints": ["First one row per borrower with their loan count (CTE), then aggregate that."], "solution": "WITH per_borrower AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN n_loans >= 2 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_repeat,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN n_loans >= 2 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS repeat_share\nFROM per_borrower", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-06", "title": "Lent vs collected by origination year", "tier": 2, "minutes": 10, "topics": ["fan-out", "cte", "duplicates"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For each origination year, return the total principal lent and the total amount collected so far from those loans: successful payments only, with each payment counted once.", "columns": "`orig_year`, `total_principal`, `total_collected`", "clarify": "- *Collected when?* Any time up to the snapshot. Group by when the **loan** was originated, not when the payment was made.", "hints": ["Joining `loans` to `payments` and summing `principal` will inflate it. Why?", "Aggregate de-duplicated, successful payments to one row per loan first, then join to `loans`."], "solution": "WITH collected AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, SUM(amount) AS collected\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM l.originated_date) AS orig_year,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal,\n  SUM(c.collected) AS total_collected\nFROM loans l\nLEFT JOIN collected c\n  ON c.loan_id = l.loan_id\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM l.originated_date)", "alts": [], "explain": "This is the classic fan-out question. Saying \"I'll aggregate payments to the loan grain first so principal isn't repeated\" is exactly what the interviewer wants to hear. 2023 and 2024 collected more than they lent (interest), while 2025 loans are still being repaid.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-07", "title": "People with more than one account", "tier": 2, "minutes": 7, "topics": ["data cleaning", "having"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Some people opened a second borrower account with the same email typed differently (case or stray spaces). Find them: for each normalized email with more than one account, return the email, the number of accounts, and the lowest `borrower_id`.", "columns": "`email_normalized`, `n_accounts`, `first_borrower_id`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["Normalize with `LOWER(TRIM(email))`, group by it, keep groups with `COUNT(*) > 1`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  LOWER(TRIM(email)) AS email_normalized,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_accounts,\n  MIN(borrower_id) AS first_borrower_id\nFROM borrowers\nGROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(email))\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1", "alts": [], "explain": "This is the input to any \"unique customers\" metric: map each `borrower_id` to the first account of its person, and count those.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-08", "title": "Merchants with above-average loan sizes", "tier": 2, "minutes": 8, "topics": ["subquery", "having"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Which merchants with at least 20 loans have an average loan principal **above the overall average** loan principal?", "columns": "`merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `n_loans`, `avg_principal`", "clarify": "- *Overall average of what?* Of all loans (not the average of merchant averages).", "hints": ["Group loans by merchant, keep groups with `COUNT(*) >= 20` and `AVG(principal) > (SELECT AVG(principal) FROM loans)`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  m.merchant_name,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(l.principal) AS avg_principal\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 20\n   AND AVG(l.principal) > (SELECT AVG(principal) FROM loans)", "alts": [], "explain": "\"Average of all loans\" and \"average of merchant averages\" differ, because big merchants weigh more in the first. That's a good clarifying question to ask out loud.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-09", "title": "Payment failure rate by method", "tier": 2, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["conditional aggregation", "duplicates"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "What share of payment attempts fail, by payment method? Count each `payment_id` once. Show missing methods as `'unknown'`.", "columns": "`payment_method`, `n_attempts`, `failure_rate`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown') AS payment_method,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_attempts,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN status = 'failed' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS failure_rate\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\nGROUP BY COALESCE(payment_method, 'unknown')", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t2-10", "title": "First installment never paid", "tier": 2, "minutes": 8, "topics": ["anti join", "composite key"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "How many **monthly** loans have a first installment that was due before 2025-12-31 but has **no successful payment at all**?", "columns": "`n_loans`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["Start from `installments` where `installment_number = 1`, join `loans` to filter the product.", "Anti join to `payments` on **both** `loan_id` and `installment_number`, with `status = 'succeeded'` inside the subquery."], "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_loans\nFROM installments i\nJOIN loans l\n  ON l.loan_id = i.loan_id\nWHERE l.product = 'monthly'\n  AND i.installment_number = 1\n  AND i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'\n  AND NOT EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1\n    FROM payments p\n    WHERE p.loan_id = i.loan_id\n      AND p.installment_number = i.installment_number\n      AND p.status = 'succeeded'\n  )", "alts": [], "explain": "The `status = 'succeeded'` condition must be inside the `NOT EXISTS`: \"no successful payment\" is not the same as \"no payment\" (many of these loans have failed attempts).", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-01", "title": "Most recent payment per loan", "tier": 3, "minutes": 8, "topics": ["row_number"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For loans originated in **November 2025**, return each loan's most recent **successful** payment: when it was made and how much. Loans with no successful payment can be left out.", "columns": "`loan_id`, `last_paid_at`, `amount`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["`ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY loan_id ORDER BY paid_at DESC)` in a CTE, then keep `rn = 1`.", "Filter to successful payments and November loans inside the CTE."], "solution": "WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    p.loan_id,\n    p.paid_at,\n    p.amount,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY p.loan_id ORDER BY p.paid_at DESC, p.payment_id DESC) AS rn\n  FROM payments p\n  JOIN loans l\n    ON l.loan_id = p.loan_id\n  WHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n    AND l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-11-01'\n    AND l.originated_date < DATE '2025-12-01'\n)\nSELECT loan_id, paid_at AS last_paid_at, amount\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1", "alts": [], "explain": "`MAX(paid_at)` gives the time but not the amount. Getting the whole row is what `ROW_NUMBER` is for. Duplicate payment rows don't matter here, because they're identical and you keep only one.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-02", "title": "Month-over-month origination growth", "tier": 3, "minutes": 10, "topics": ["lag", "date_trunc"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For every month from January 2024 to December 2025, return the number of loans originated and the percentage change from the previous month (as a decimal). Sort by month.", "columns": "`month` (first day of the month), `n_loans`, `pct_change`", "clarify": "- *What's January 2024's change?* Compared with December 2023, which is outside the requested range. Either NULL or using December 2023 is defensible; here, use December 2023 so January has a real value.", "hints": ["Compute monthly counts for **all** months in a CTE, apply `LAG`, and only then filter to 2024–2025, so January 2024 can see December 2023.", "Use `DATE_TRUNC('month', ...)`, not `EXTRACT(MONTH ...)`, which would merge years."], "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month,\n    COUNT(*) AS n_loans\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE)\n),\nwith_change AS (\n  SELECT\n    month,\n    n_loans,\n    1.0 * (n_loans - LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month))\n      / LAG(n_loans) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS pct_change\n  FROM monthly\n)\nSELECT month, n_loans, pct_change\nFROM with_change\nWHERE month >= DATE '2024-01-01'\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "explain": "The window runs in the `SELECT` step, after `WHERE`. If you filtered to 2024+ in the same query, `LAG` for January 2024 would be NULL. Computing the window first and filtering afterwards is a pattern worth remembering.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-03", "title": "Cumulative principal in 2025", "tier": 3, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["running total"], "ordered": true, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For 2025, return each month's principal originated and the cumulative principal for the year to date. Sort by month.", "columns": "`month` (first day of the month), `principal`, `cumulative_principal`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT\n    CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE) AS month,\n    SUM(principal) AS principal\n  FROM loans\n  WHERE originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  GROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', originated_date) AS DATE)\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  principal,\n  SUM(principal) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS cumulative_principal\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-04", "title": "Time to second loan", "tier": 3, "minutes": 8, "topics": ["row_number", "lead", "dates"], "ordered": false, "digits": 1, "prompt": "For borrowers with at least two loans, what is the average number of days between their **first** and **second** loan?", "columns": "`n_borrowers`, `avg_days_to_second`", "clarify": "- *Same-day loans?* Order by `originated_date`, then `loan_id`. A second loan on the same day counts as 0 days.", "hints": ["Number each borrower's loans and get the next loan's date with `LEAD`, both over the same window.", "Keep `rn = 1` rows where the next date isn't NULL."], "solution": "WITH ordered AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn,\n    LEAD(originated_date) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS next_date\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers,\n  AVG(next_date - originated_date) AS avg_days_to_second\nFROM ordered\nWHERE rn = 1\n  AND next_date IS NOT NULL", "alts": ["WITH ordered AS (\n  SELECT\n    borrower_id,\n    originated_date,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS rn\n  FROM loans\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers,\n  AVG(s.originated_date - f.originated_date) AS avg_days_to_second\nFROM ordered f\nJOIN ordered s\n  ON s.borrower_id = f.borrower_id\n AND s.rn = 2\nWHERE f.rn = 1"], "explain": "The self-join version (first loan joined to second loan) is a perfectly good answer, and some people find it easier to reason about.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-05", "title": "Top two merchants in each category", "tier": 3, "minutes": 10, "topics": ["row_number", "data cleaning"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "Within each **cleaned** merchant category (lower-case, trimmed), find the two merchants with the highest total principal. Exclude merchants whose category is missing. Break ties with the lower `merchant_id`.", "columns": "`category`, `merchant_id`, `total_principal`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["Step 1: total principal per merchant with its cleaned category. Step 2: `ROW_NUMBER()` partitioned by category. Step 3: `rn <= 2`."], "solution": "WITH merchant_totals AS (\n  SELECT\n    LOWER(TRIM(m.category)) AS category,\n    m.merchant_id,\n    SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal\n  FROM loans l\n  JOIN merchants m\n    ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\n  WHERE m.category IS NOT NULL\n  GROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), m.merchant_id\n),\nranked AS (\n  SELECT\n    *,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY total_principal DESC, merchant_id) AS rn\n  FROM merchant_totals\n)\nSELECT category, merchant_id, total_principal\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn <= 2", "alts": [], "explain": "Aggregate first (one row per merchant), then rank. Ranking loans directly would give you the top two *loans* per category.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-06", "title": "Applications by day of week", "tier": 3, "minutes": 6, "topics": ["extract", "dialects"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "By day of the week of the application, return the number of applications and the approval rate. Use `EXTRACT(DOW ...)` numbering (0 = Sunday) and sort by it.", "columns": "`day_of_week`, `n_applications`, `approval_rate`", "clarify": "", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT\n  EXTRACT(DOW FROM applied_at) AS day_of_week,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_applications,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS approval_rate\nFROM applications\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(DOW FROM applied_at)\nORDER BY day_of_week", "alts": [], "explain": "Synthetic data has no weekday effect, so the rates are flat. In an interview, say how you'd check the result is plausible. Also say the numbering out loud: in MySQL, `DAYOFWEEK()` makes Sunday 1.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-07", "title": "Category share of 2025 principal", "tier": 3, "minutes": 7, "topics": ["window over aggregate"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For loans originated in 2025, what share of total principal came from each **cleaned** merchant category (lower-case, trimmed, missing shown as `'unknown'`)? Use a window function rather than a subquery for the total.", "columns": "`category`, `principal`, `share`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["`SUM(SUM(l.principal)) OVER ()` is the grand total across all groups."], "solution": "SELECT\n  COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), 'unknown') AS category,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS principal,\n  SUM(l.principal) / SUM(SUM(l.principal)) OVER () AS share\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE l.originated_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY COALESCE(LOWER(TRIM(m.category)), 'unknown')", "alts": [], "explain": "", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-08", "title": "Time from sign-up to first loan", "tier": 3, "minutes": 8, "topics": ["dates", "case", "min"], "ordered": false, "digits": 2, "prompt": "For borrower accounts that have taken at least one loan, how long after the account was created did they take their first loan? Bucket the days as `'same day'` (0), `'1-30'`, `'31-90'`, and `'91+'`, and count borrowers in each.", "columns": "`days_bucket`, `n_borrowers`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["First loan date per borrower: `MIN(originated_date)` grouped by `borrower_id`. You don't need the loan itself, just the date.", "`created_at` is a timestamp: `CAST(b.created_at AS DATE)` before subtracting."], "solution": "WITH first_loan AS (\n  SELECT borrower_id, MIN(originated_date) AS first_loan_date\n  FROM loans\n  GROUP BY borrower_id\n),\ndays AS (\n  SELECT f.first_loan_date - CAST(b.created_at AS DATE) AS days_to_first\n  FROM first_loan f\n  JOIN borrowers b\n    ON b.borrower_id = f.borrower_id\n)\nSELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN days_to_first = 0 THEN 'same day'\n    WHEN days_to_first <= 30 THEN '1-30'\n    WHEN days_to_first <= 90 THEN '31-90'\n    ELSE '91+'\n  END AS days_bucket,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_borrowers\nFROM days\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "explain": "Here `MIN()` is enough because you only need the date. When you need other columns from the first row, use `ROW_NUMBER`.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t3-09", "title": "Retrying after a decline", "tier": 3, "minutes": 12, "topics": ["lead", "timestamps"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "When an application is **declined**, how often does the same borrower submit **another application within 24 hours**, and how often is that next application approved?\n\nReturn the number of declined applications, the share followed by another application within 24 hours, and, among those retries, the share approved.", "columns": "`n_declined`, `retry_rate_24h`, `retry_approval_rate`", "clarify": "- *\"Next application\"?* The borrower's very next application by `applied_at`, regardless of merchant.\n- *24 hours exactly?* `next_applied_at <= applied_at + INTERVAL '24 hours'`.", "hints": ["`LEAD(applied_at)` and `LEAD(decision)` over each borrower's applications, ordered by time.", "Then filter to declined applications and use conditional aggregation. The approval rate's denominator is retries, not all declines."], "solution": "WITH seq AS (\n  SELECT\n    application_id,\n    applied_at,\n    decision,\n    LEAD(applied_at) OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at, application_id) AS next_applied_at,\n    LEAD(decision)   OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY applied_at, application_id) AS next_decision\n  FROM applications\n),\ndeclines AS (\n  SELECT\n    *,\n    CASE WHEN next_applied_at <= applied_at + INTERVAL '24 hours' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS retried\n  FROM seq\n  WHERE decision = 'declined'\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS n_declined,\n  AVG(1.0 * retried) AS retry_rate_24h,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN retried = 1 THEN CASE WHEN next_decision = 'approved' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END END) AS retry_approval_rate\nFROM declines", "alts": [], "explain": "Two windows, then the filter. As in other problems, the decline filter must come **after** `LEAD`, or the \"next application\" would be the next *declined* one. Business takeaway: about one in six declines is followed by a quick retry (usually for less money), and many of those retries are approved. That is why application-level approval rates understate approvals per customer.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-01", "title": "30+ days past due by product", "tier": 4, "minutes": 18, "topics": ["dpd", "cte", "composite key"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "As of 2025-12-31, for each product, how many loans are open, and what share of them are **30 or more days past due**?\n\nAn open loan has status `current` or `late`. Days past due = days between the snapshot and the due date of the loan's **oldest installment that was due before the snapshot and isn't fully paid**. An installment is fully paid when its successful, de-duplicated payments add up to at least `amount_due`.", "columns": "`product`, `n_open`, `n_30_plus`, `rate_30_plus`", "clarify": "- *By count or balance?* Count of loans.\n- *Partial payments?* Count toward the installment; it's paid only when the total reaches `amount_due`.", "hints": ["CTE 1: paid amount per `(loan_id, installment_number)` from de-duplicated, successful payments.", "CTE 2: per loan, `MIN(due_date)` of installments due before the snapshot whose paid amount (`COALESCE(..., 0)`) is below `amount_due`. LEFT JOIN from installments!", "Final: open loans LEFT JOIN CTE 2, DPD = snapshot − oldest due (0 if none), conditional aggregation by product."], "solution": "WITH paid AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, SUM(amount) AS paid\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n),\noldest_unpaid AS (\n  SELECT i.loan_id, MIN(i.due_date) AS oldest_due\n  FROM installments i\n  LEFT JOIN paid pd\n    ON pd.loan_id = i.loan_id\n   AND pd.installment_number = i.installment_number\n  WHERE i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'\n    AND COALESCE(pd.paid, 0) < i.amount_due\n  GROUP BY i.loan_id\n),\nopen_loans AS (\n  SELECT\n    l.loan_id,\n    l.product,\n    COALESCE(DATE '2025-12-31' - ou.oldest_due, 0) AS dpd\n  FROM loans l\n  LEFT JOIN oldest_unpaid ou\n    ON ou.loan_id = l.loan_id\n  WHERE l.status IN ('current', 'late')\n)\nSELECT\n  product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_open,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS n_30_plus,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN dpd >= 30 THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS rate_30_plus\nFROM open_loans\nGROUP BY product", "alts": [], "explain": "Every piece of the course is in here: de-duplication, aggregate-then-join on a composite key, a LEFT JOIN that keeps installments with no payments, future installments excluded, `COALESCE` for loans with nothing overdue, and conditional aggregation. If time is short in an interview, write CTE 1 and 2, say what CTE 3 would do, and explain the traps.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-02", "title": "Vintage charge-off curve", "tier": 4, "minutes": 15, "topics": ["vintage", "conditional aggregation", "dates"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Build a small vintage table. For each quarterly origination vintage with at least 12 months of history as of 2025-12-31, return the number of loans and the cumulative share charged off within **6**, **9** and **12** months of origination. Sort by vintage.", "columns": "`vintage` (first day of the quarter), `n_loans`, `co_6m`, `co_9m`, `co_12m`", "clarify": "- *\"Within 6 months\"?* `charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '6 months'`.\n- *Which vintages are mature?* Originated before 2025-01-01, so every loan has 12 months of history.", "hints": ["One row per vintage: `DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date)`.", "Three `AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '...' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)` columns."], "solution": "SELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE) AS vintage,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '6 months' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_6m,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '9 months' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_9m,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '12 months' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_12m\nFROM loans\nWHERE originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', originated_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY vintage", "alts": [], "explain": "Read across a row to see a vintage's losses grow with age. Read down a column to compare vintages **at the same age**, the only fair comparison. The vintages after the July 2024 policy change sit visibly lower. To include younger vintages, you'd show each one only up to the age it has reached (NULL beyond it), which is how a vintage chart is built.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-03", "title": "First payment default", "tier": 4, "minutes": 15, "topics": ["fpd", "dates", "composite key"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "**First payment default (FPD30)**: a monthly loan's first installment is not fully paid within 30 days of its due date. Compute the FPD30 rate by origination quarter for monthly loans whose first installment was due **on or before 2025-12-01**, so all of them have had 30 days to pay. Sort by quarter.", "columns": "`vintage` (first day of the quarter), `n_loans`, `fpd30_rate`", "clarify": "- *Paid within 30 days?* Successful, de-duplicated payments on installment 1 with `CAST(paid_at AS DATE) <= due_date + 30` add up to at least `amount_due`.\n- *Partial payments?* They count toward the total.", "hints": ["Start from installment 1 of monthly loans with `due_date <= DATE '2025-12-01'`.", "Sum qualifying payments per loan in a CTE (joined on both keys, with the date condition), then LEFT JOIN and compare with `amount_due`."], "solution": "WITH first_inst AS (\n  SELECT i.loan_id, i.due_date, i.amount_due, l.originated_date\n  FROM installments i\n  JOIN loans l\n    ON l.loan_id = i.loan_id\n  WHERE l.product = 'monthly'\n    AND i.installment_number = 1\n    AND i.due_date <= DATE '2025-12-01'\n),\npaid_30 AS (\n  SELECT f.loan_id, SUM(p.amount) AS paid\n  FROM first_inst f\n  JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n    ON p.loan_id = f.loan_id\n   AND p.installment_number = 1\n  WHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n    AND CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) <= f.due_date + 30\n  GROUP BY f.loan_id\n)\nSELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', f.originated_date) AS DATE) AS vintage,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN COALESCE(pd.paid, 0) < f.amount_due THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS fpd30_rate\nFROM first_inst f\nLEFT JOIN paid_30 pd\n  ON pd.loan_id = f.loan_id\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('quarter', f.originated_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY vintage", "alts": [], "explain": "FPD is an early read on fraud and credit quality: you see it within weeks, where charge-offs take months. That's why it's used to monitor recent vintages. `due_date + 30` adds 30 days to a date in PostgreSQL and DuckDB; elsewhere use your dialect's `DATEADD`.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-04", "title": "Dollar loss rate by score band", "tier": 4, "minutes": 12, "topics": ["joins", "conditional aggregation"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For loans originated in 2023 and 2024, compute the **dollar** loss rate by credit score band at application: charged-off principal ÷ total principal. Bands: `'<600'`, `'600-659'`, `'660-719'`, `'720+'`, `'no score'`.", "columns": "`score_band`, `n_loans`, `total_principal`, `charged_off_principal`, `loss_rate`", "clarify": "", "hints": ["The score lives on `applications`; join loans to applications on `application_id`.", "`SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN l.principal ELSE 0 END)`."], "solution": "SELECT\n  CASE\n    WHEN a.credit_score IS NULL THEN 'no score'\n    WHEN a.credit_score < 600 THEN '<600'\n    WHEN a.credit_score < 660 THEN '600-659'\n    WHEN a.credit_score < 720 THEN '660-719'\n    ELSE '720+'\n  END AS score_band,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  SUM(l.principal) AS total_principal,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN l.principal ELSE 0 END) AS charged_off_principal,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN l.principal ELSE 0 END) / SUM(l.principal) AS loss_rate\nFROM loans l\nJOIN applications a\n  ON a.application_id = l.application_id\nWHERE l.originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY 1", "alts": [], "explain": "Using principal as the loss is a simplification (a real loss figure subtracts what was repaid before charge-off), and it's worth saying so. Restricting to 2023–2024 avoids comparing mature loans with very young ones.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-05", "title": "Scheduled vs collected", "tier": 4, "minutes": 15, "topics": ["fan-out", "composite key", "dates"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "For installments **due in 2025 before 2025-12-31**, by month due: how much was scheduled, how much of it has been collected (successful, de-duplicated payments applied to those installments, at any time up to the snapshot), and the collection rate. Sort by month.", "columns": "`due_month` (first day of the month), `scheduled`, `collected`, `collection_rate`", "clarify": "- *Can collected exceed scheduled for an installment?* Not in this data, but it's worth asking.", "hints": ["Aggregate payments to the installment grain **before** joining, or each installment's `amount_due` gets repeated once per payment.", "LEFT JOIN from installments so unpaid installments still count as scheduled."], "solution": "WITH paid AS (\n  SELECT loan_id, installment_number, SUM(amount) AS paid\n  FROM (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n  WHERE status = 'succeeded'\n  GROUP BY loan_id, installment_number\n)\nSELECT\n  CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', i.due_date) AS DATE) AS due_month,\n  SUM(i.amount_due) AS scheduled,\n  SUM(COALESCE(pd.paid, 0)) AS collected,\n  SUM(COALESCE(pd.paid, 0)) / SUM(i.amount_due) AS collection_rate\nFROM installments i\nLEFT JOIN paid pd\n  ON pd.loan_id = i.loan_id\n AND pd.installment_number = i.installment_number\nWHERE i.due_date >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'\nGROUP BY CAST(DATE_TRUNC('month', i.due_date) AS DATE)\nORDER BY due_month", "alts": [], "explain": "December collects visibly less, partly because late payers haven't paid *yet*: the same maturity effect as with charge-offs. A collection rate for December is not comparable with one for June.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-06", "title": "Do repeat loans perform better?", "tier": 4, "minutes": 15, "topics": ["row_number", "vintage"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Compare the 12-month charge-off rate of borrowers' **first** loans with their **repeat** loans (second and later), for loans originated before 2025-01-01. Charged off within 12 months means `charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '12 months'`.", "columns": "`loan_type` (`'first'` or `'repeat'`), `n_loans`, `co_rate_12m`", "clarify": "- *First loan ever, or first loan before 2025?* Ever: number each borrower's loans over their full history, **then** filter to pre-2025 originations.", "hints": ["`ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id)` over **all** loans in a CTE.", "Filter to `originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'` outside the CTE."], "solution": "WITH numbered AS (\n  SELECT\n    l.*,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY borrower_id ORDER BY originated_date, loan_id) AS loan_seq\n  FROM loans l\n)\nSELECT\n  CASE WHEN loan_seq = 1 THEN 'first' ELSE 'repeat' END AS loan_type,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN charged_off_date < originated_date + INTERVAL '12 months' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_rate_12m\nFROM numbered\nWHERE originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY CASE WHEN loan_seq = 1 THEN 'first' ELSE 'repeat' END", "alts": [], "explain": "Here the order doesn't actually change the answer: filtering to *before* 2025 keeps each borrower's earliest loans, so the numbering is the same either way. It **would** matter for a later window (say 2025 only). Thinking about it out loud is what counts. Duplicate accounts mean some \"first\" loans are really repeat customers. Mention it as a caveat.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-07", "title": "Riskiest merchants", "tier": 4, "minutes": 12, "topics": ["having", "ranking"], "ordered": true, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Among merchants with **at least 40 loans** originated in 2023–2024, which five have the highest charge-off rate (share of those loans with status `charged_off`)? Break ties with the lower `merchant_id`. Sort from highest rate.", "columns": "`merchant_id`, `merchant_name`, `n_loans`, `co_rate`", "clarify": "- *Why a minimum of 40?* A merchant with 3 loans and 1 charge-off has a 33% rate that means nothing. The threshold keeps small samples out.", "hints": [], "solution": "SELECT\n  m.merchant_id,\n  m.merchant_name,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_loans,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN l.status = 'charged_off' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS co_rate\nFROM loans l\nJOIN merchants m\n  ON m.merchant_id = l.merchant_id\nWHERE l.originated_date < DATE '2025-01-01'\nGROUP BY m.merchant_id, m.merchant_name\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 40\nORDER BY co_rate DESC, m.merchant_id\nLIMIT 5", "alts": [], "explain": "Ranking rates without a volume floor is a classic analytics mistake; mention it even if the question already gives a threshold. Credibility-weighted rates are a step further if the interviewer asks.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}, {"type": "problem", "id": "t4-08", "title": "Paid on time", "tier": 4, "minutes": 12, "topics": ["composite key", "dates", "fan-out"], "ordered": false, "digits": 3, "prompt": "Of installments due in **Q4 2025** (1 October to 30 December 2025), what share were paid in full **on or before their due date**? Show it by product.", "columns": "`product`, `n_installments`, `on_time_rate`", "clarify": "- *On or before the due date?* Successful, de-duplicated payments with `CAST(paid_at AS DATE) <= due_date` add up to at least `amount_due`. Later payments don't count toward \"on time\".", "hints": ["Aggregate qualifying payments per installment first: the date condition compares each payment with its installment's due date, so join installments in that CTE.", "Then LEFT JOIN that to the Q4 installments and compare with `amount_due`."], "solution": "WITH q4 AS (\n  SELECT i.loan_id, i.installment_number, i.due_date, i.amount_due, l.product\n  FROM installments i\n  JOIN loans l\n    ON l.loan_id = i.loan_id\n  WHERE i.due_date >= DATE '2025-10-01'\n    AND i.due_date < DATE '2025-12-31'\n),\npaid_on_time AS (\n  SELECT q.loan_id, q.installment_number, SUM(p.amount) AS paid\n  FROM q4 q\n  JOIN (SELECT DISTINCT * FROM payments) p\n    ON p.loan_id = q.loan_id\n   AND p.installment_number = q.installment_number\n  WHERE p.status = 'succeeded'\n    AND CAST(p.paid_at AS DATE) <= q.due_date\n  GROUP BY q.loan_id, q.installment_number\n)\nSELECT\n  q.product,\n  COUNT(*) AS n_installments,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN COALESCE(pt.paid, 0) >= q.amount_due THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) AS on_time_rate\nFROM q4 q\nLEFT JOIN paid_on_time pt\n  ON pt.loan_id = q.loan_id\n AND pt.installment_number = q.installment_number\nGROUP BY q.product", "alts": [], "explain": "Pay in 4's first installment is collected at checkout, so it's always on time. That flatters Pay in 4's rate. A careful analyst would mention it, or exclude installment 1 for a fairer comparison.", "starter": "", "kind": "write"}], "reference": [{"id": "00-start-here", "title": "Start here", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "## How this course works\n\nYou already think in data frames. This course teaches SQL the way SQL wants to be learned. It leans on dplyr only where that genuinely speeds you up, and it says plainly where your R instincts will mislead you.\n\nEverything runs in this browser tab. A real SQL engine (DuckDB) holds a synthetic consumer-lending database (merchants, borrowers, applications, loans, payment schedules and payments) with the messiness interviewers like to test: duplicates, NULLs, many-to-one relationships, timestamps.\n\nTry it: edit the query below and press **Run** (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>).\n\n```sql\nSELECT loan_id, originated_date, product, principal, status\nFROM loans\nORDER BY principal DESC\nLIMIT 5\n```\n\nThe **Tables** button (top right) opens the schema whenever you need it, as you'd have it on an interview screen.\n\n## The path\n\n| Block | What | Time |\n|---|---|---|\n| Foundations | Lessons 0–2: how SQL thinks, filtering and NULL, aggregation | ~2 h |\n| Joins | Lessons 3–4: syntax, cardinality, fan-out, anti-joins | ~2 h |\n| Structure | Lesson 5: CTEs and subqueries | ~45 min |\n| Windows | Lessons 6–7: ranking, dedup, LAG/LEAD, running totals | ~1.5 h |\n| Time | Lesson 8: dates and timestamps | ~45 min |\n| Domain | Lesson 9: lending metrics | ~1 h |\n| Craft | Lesson 10: the interview itself | ~30 min |\n| Practice | Problem bank (mixed tiers) and 2 timed mock screens | 3–4 h |\n\nThat's roughly 12 hours. If time gets short, keep the mocks and cut Tier 4 problems, not the other way round.\n\nA good rhythm per session: one or two lessons, then three or four problem-bank questions from earlier tiers. Mixing old material with new is what makes it stick.\n\n## Kinds of exercise\n\nEach lesson moves from watching to doing:\n\n- **Predict**: read a query and say what it returns *before* running it. This is how you build an accurate mental model, and it's exactly what happens when an interviewer asks \"what does this return?\"\n- **Fix the bug**: a query that runs but is wrong. Most real-world SQL errors look like this.\n- **Fill in**: a partly written query. The structure is given; you supply the key clause.\n- **Write**: a blank editor and a question. This is the interview.\n\n**Check answer** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>) runs your query and compares the result with the reference answer:\n\n- Row order is ignored unless the question asks for a sort.\n- Column names don't need to match, but column order does unless you used the expected names.\n- Numbers are compared to 2 decimal places unless stated otherwise.\n\nWhen you're wrong it tells you *how*: too many rows (often a fan-out), too few (often a join or NULL), or different values.\n\n**Hints** come one at a time. Use them. Revealing the solution is fine too, but if you do, redo the exercise from a blank editor the next day. The Progress page keeps a list.\n\n## The R boxes\n\nBlue boxes headed *If you know dplyr* map SQL to what you already know. They show up when the comparison helps, and they also flag the places where the analogy breaks. Those are worth reading twice.\n\n## Before your recruiter call\n\nThings worth asking, because they change how you prepare:\n\n- **Which SQL dialect?** (PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, BigQuery, SQL Server…) The [dialect sheet](#/ref/03-dialects) covers what changes, which is mostly dates.\n- **Live with an interviewer, or timed and unattended?** Which platform (CoderPad, HackerRank, CodeSignal)?\n- **How long, and how many questions?**\n- **Can you look up syntax?** Is the schema provided?\n- **Pure SQL, or SQL plus interpreting the result?**"}]}, {"id": "01-the-data", "title": "The data", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "A synthetic buy-now-pay-later lender. Customers apply for credit at checkout with a merchant. If approved, they take a loan: either **Pay in 4** (four interest-free payments every two weeks, the first at checkout) or a **monthly** installment loan of 3, 6, 12 or 24 months. Every loan has a payment schedule (`installments`) and a log of payment attempts (`payments`).\n\nThe snapshot date is **2025-12-31**. Nothing in the data happens after it, but schedules extend into the future.\n\n## How the tables connect\n\n```text\nmerchants (merchant_id)\n    │ 1\n    │ many\napplications (application_id) ── borrower_id ──▶ borrowers (borrower_id)\n    │ 1\n    │ 0 or 1\nloans (loan_id) ── borrower_id, merchant_id\n    │ 1\n    │ many\ninstallments (loan_id, installment_number)\n    │ 1\n    │ many\npayments (payment_id) ── loan_id, installment_number\n```\n\n## Tables\n\n**merchants**: one row per merchant.\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `merchant_id` | key |\n| `merchant_name` | |\n| `category` | Electronics, Fashion, Travel… **inconsistently cased, some trailing spaces, two NULLs** |\n| `onboarded_date` | |\n| `zero_apr_eligible` | merchant can offer 0% APR promotions |\n\n**borrowers**: one row per *account*.\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `borrower_id` | key |\n| `email` | **about 120 people opened a second account with the same email typed differently** (case, trailing space) |\n| `created_at` | timestamp |\n| `state` | two-letter code |\n| `annual_income` | self-reported, **NULL for ~9%** |\n\n**applications**: one row per credit application.\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `application_id` | key |\n| `borrower_id`, `merchant_id` | |\n| `applied_at` | timestamp |\n| `requested_amount` | |\n| `credit_score` | at time of application, **NULL when no credit file was found** |\n| `decision` | `approved` / `declined`. **Declined applicants often retry within minutes for a smaller amount.** |\n\nCredit policy tightened on **2024-07-01** (higher score cutoff).\n\n**loans**: one row per loan. About 88% of approved applications become loans.\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `loan_id` | key |\n| `application_id`, `borrower_id`, `merchant_id` | |\n| `originated_date` | date |\n| `product` | `pay_in_4` or `monthly` |\n| `term_months` | **NULL for pay_in_4** |\n| `n_installments` | 4 for pay_in_4, otherwise the term |\n| `principal`, `apr` | `apr` is 0 for pay_in_4 and promo loans |\n| `status` | as of the snapshot: `current`, `late`, `paid_off`, `charged_off` |\n| `charged_off_date` | set when charged off: 120 days after the first missed installment |\n\n**installments**: one row per scheduled payment. Key: (`loan_id`, `installment_number`).\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `due_date` | **includes future due dates** (after 2025-12-31) |\n| `amount_due` | |\n\n**payments**: one row per payment attempt. *Supposedly.*\n\n| column | notes |\n|---|---|\n| `payment_id` | **not unique: a pipeline replay loaded ~700 rows twice** |\n| `loan_id`, `installment_number` | which installment the payment was applied to |\n| `paid_at` | timestamp |\n| `amount` | **partial payments exist**: one installment can take two payments |\n| `status` | `succeeded` or `failed`. **Failed attempts are not cash.** |\n| `payment_method` | `ach`, `debit_card`, **or NULL** |\n\n## Things a careful analyst notices"}, {"type": "interview", "title": "", "text": "Interviewers often hand you a schema like this and watch whether you ask about these before writing SQL. Saying \"I'd check whether payment_id is unique first\" earns credit even if you don't have time to do it."}, {"type": "md", "text": "1. `payments` has exact duplicate rows. Sum it naively and you overstate collections.\n2. `payments` includes failed attempts. Filter `status = 'succeeded'` for cash.\n3. Joining `loans` to `payments` or `installments` multiplies loan rows. Aggregate first, then join.\n4. One person can have two `borrower_id`s. \"Unique customers\" is not `COUNT(DISTINCT borrower_id)` without thought.\n5. `credit_score`, `annual_income`, `term_months`, `category`, `payment_method` all have NULLs, for different reasons.\n6. `paid_at`, `applied_at` and `created_at` are timestamps. `<= DATE '2025-01-31'` misses most of January 31st.\n7. Recent loans haven't had time to go bad. Compare loans of the same age.\n\n```sql\n-- Check row counts against distinct keys\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS payment_rows, COUNT(DISTINCT payment_id) AS distinct_payment_ids\nFROM payments\n```"}]}, {"id": "02-dplyr-to-sql", "title": "dplyr → SQL phrasebook", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "A lookup table for when you know what you'd write in R. The SQL column is portable unless marked.\n\n## Verbs\n\n| dplyr | SQL |\n|---|---|\n| `filter(x > 1, y == \"a\")` | `WHERE x > 1 AND y = 'a'` |\n| `filter(is.na(x))` | `WHERE x IS NULL` |\n| `filter(x %in% c(\"a\", \"b\"))` | `WHERE x IN ('a', 'b')` |\n| `filter(between(x, 1, 5))` | `WHERE x BETWEEN 1 AND 5` |\n| `filter(str_detect(x, \"^Blue\"))` | `WHERE x LIKE 'Blue%'` |\n| `select(a, b)` | `SELECT a, b` |\n| `select(new = old)` / `rename()` | `SELECT old AS new` |\n| `mutate(z = x * y)` | `SELECT *, x * y AS z` |\n| `arrange(desc(x), y)` | `ORDER BY x DESC, y` |\n| `head(10)` / `slice_head(n = 10)` | `LIMIT 10` (after `ORDER BY`) |\n| `distinct(a, b)` | `SELECT DISTINCT a, b` |\n| `count(a)` | `SELECT a, COUNT(*) FROM t GROUP BY a` |\n| `group_by(a) \\|> summarise(n = n(), s = sum(x))` | `SELECT a, COUNT(*) AS n, SUM(x) AS s FROM t GROUP BY a` |\n| `summarise(...) \\|> filter(n > 5)` | `... GROUP BY a HAVING COUNT(*) > 5` |\n| `group_by(a) \\|> mutate(s = sum(x))` | `SUM(x) OVER (PARTITION BY a)` |\n| `bind_rows(x, y)` | `SELECT ... UNION ALL SELECT ...` (matched by position) |\n\n## Functions\n\n| R | SQL | Watch out |\n|---|---|---|\n| `n()` | `COUNT(*)` | |\n| `sum(!is.na(x))` | `COUNT(x)` | |\n| `n_distinct(x, na.rm = TRUE)` | `COUNT(DISTINCT x)` | plain `n_distinct()` counts NA |\n| `mean(x, na.rm = TRUE)` | `AVG(x)` | SQL always skips NULLs |\n| `mean(x == \"a\")` | `AVG(CASE WHEN x = 'a' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)` | |\n| `sum(x == \"a\")` | `SUM(CASE WHEN x = 'a' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)` | |\n| `if_else(c, a, b)` | `CASE WHEN c THEN a ELSE b END` | |\n| `case_when(c1 ~ a, c2 ~ b, .default = d)` | `CASE WHEN c1 THEN a WHEN c2 THEN b ELSE d END` | |\n| `coalesce(x, 0)` / `replace_na()` | `COALESCE(x, 0)` | |\n| `na_if(x, 0)` | `NULLIF(x, 0)` | |\n| `round(x, 2)` | `ROUND(x, 2)` | |\n| `x %/% y` | `FLOOR(x / y)` | `/` on integers truncates in Postgres/SQL Server |\n| `paste0(a, b)` | `a \\|\\| b` or `CONCAT(a, b)` | `\\|\\|` isn't string concat in MySQL or SQL Server |\n| `str_to_lower()`, `str_trim()` | `LOWER()`, `TRIM()` | |\n| `row_number()` | `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...)` | SQL needs an explicit `ORDER BY` |\n| `min_rank()` / `dense_rank()` | `RANK()` / `DENSE_RANK()` | |\n| `lag(x)` / `lead(x)` | `LAG(x) OVER (ORDER BY ...)` | |\n| `cumsum(x)` | `SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY ...)` | ties share a value (RANGE frame) |\n| `floor_date(d, \"month\")` | `DATE_TRUNC('month', d)` | dialect-specific |\n| `year(d)` | `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)` | |\n| `d + days(30)` | `d + INTERVAL '30 days'` | dialect-specific |\n| `as.numeric(d2 - d1)` | `d2 - d1` | Postgres/DuckDB; `DATEDIFF` elsewhere |\n\n## Joins\n\n| dplyr | SQL |\n|---|---|\n| `inner_join(x, y, by = join_by(id))` | `FROM x JOIN y ON y.id = x.id` |\n| `left_join(x, y, by = join_by(id))` | `FROM x LEFT JOIN y ON y.id = x.id` |\n| `full_join()` | `FULL OUTER JOIN` |\n| `semi_join(x, y, by = ...)` | `WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM y WHERE y.id = x.id)` |\n| `anti_join(x, y, by = ...)` | `WHERE NOT EXISTS (...)` |\n| `join_by(a, b)` (two keys) | `ON y.a = x.a AND y.b = x.b` |\n| `join_by(id, date >= start)` | `ON y.id = x.id AND x.date >= y.start` |\n\n## Where the analogy breaks\n\n- **Order:** dplyr runs in written order; SQL runs `FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY → LIMIT`.\n- **Row order:** data frames have it; tables don't. Window functions and `LIMIT` need explicit `ORDER BY`.\n- **NA in joins:** dplyr matches NA to NA by default; SQL never matches NULL keys.\n- **na.rm:** SQL aggregates always skip NULLs, silently.\n- **Many-to-many joins:** dplyr warns; SQL multiplies rows without a word.\n- **Grouping:** SQL's `GROUP BY` collapses rows and leaves no grouping behind; `group_by() |> mutate()` is a window function."}]}, {"id": "03-dialects", "title": "Dialect sheet", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "This course runs on **DuckDB**, which follows **PostgreSQL** closely (plus `QUALIFY`), so the PostgreSQL column is what you've been practising. Once you know your interview dialect, read down its column and rewrite a few of the lesson queries in it.\n\n## Dates and times\n\n| Task | PostgreSQL | MySQL | Snowflake | BigQuery | SQL Server |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Date literal | `DATE '2025-01-31'` | `DATE '2025-01-31'` | `DATE '2025-01-31'` | `DATE '2025-01-31'` | `CAST('2025-01-31' AS DATE)` |\n| Today | `CURRENT_DATE` | `CURRENT_DATE` | `CURRENT_DATE` | `CURRENT_DATE()` | `CAST(GETDATE() AS DATE)` |\n| Timestamp → date | `CAST(ts AS DATE)` | `DATE(ts)` | `CAST(ts AS DATE)` | `DATE(ts)` | `CAST(ts AS DATE)` |\n| First of month | `DATE_TRUNC('month', d)` ¹ | `DATE_FORMAT(d, '%Y-%m-01')` ² | `DATE_TRUNC('month', d)` | `DATE_TRUNC(d, MONTH)` | `DATETRUNC(month, d)` ³ |\n| Year | `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)` | `YEAR(d)` | `YEAR(d)` | `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)` | `YEAR(d)` |\n| Day of week | `EXTRACT(DOW FROM d)`, 0 = Sun | `DAYOFWEEK(d)`, **1** = Sun | `DAYOFWEEK(d)`, 0 = Sun ⁴ | `EXTRACT(DAYOFWEEK FROM d)`, **1** = Sun | `DATEPART(weekday, d)`, **1** = Sun ⁴ |\n| Add 30 days | `d + INTERVAL '30 days'` | `DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 30 DAY)` | `DATEADD(day, 30, d)` | `DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 30 DAY)` | `DATEADD(day, 30, d)` |\n| Add 1 month | `d + INTERVAL '1 month'` | `DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 1 MONTH)` | `DATEADD(month, 1, d)` | `DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 1 MONTH)` | `DATEADD(month, 1, d)` |\n| Days between | `d2 - d1` | `DATEDIFF(d2, d1)` | `DATEDIFF(day, d1, d2)` | `DATE_DIFF(d2, d1, DAY)` | `DATEDIFF(day, d1, d2)` |\n| Months between | year×12 + month formula ⁵ | `TIMESTAMPDIFF(MONTH, d1, d2)` ⁶ | `DATEDIFF(month, d1, d2)` | `DATE_DIFF(d2, d1, MONTH)` | `DATEDIFF(month, d1, d2)` |\n| Format as text | `TO_CHAR(d, 'YYYY-MM')` | `DATE_FORMAT(d, '%Y-%m')` | `TO_CHAR(d, 'YYYY-MM')` | `FORMAT_DATE('%Y-%m', d)` | `FORMAT(d, 'yyyy-MM')` |\n| Calendar of months | `generate_series(a, b, INTERVAL '1 month')` | recursive CTE | `GENERATOR` + `DATEADD` | `UNNEST(GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY(a, b, INTERVAL 1 MONTH))` | recursive CTE |\n\n1. Returns a **timestamp** in PostgreSQL; wrap in `CAST(... AS DATE)`.\n2. Returns text. For a real date: `DATE_SUB(d, INTERVAL DAY(d) - 1 DAY)`, or `CAST(DATE_FORMAT(d, '%Y-%m-01') AS DATE)`.\n3. SQL Server 2022+. Older: `DATEFROMPARTS(YEAR(d), MONTH(d), 1)`.\n4. Depends on a session setting (`WEEK_START` in Snowflake, `DATEFIRST` in SQL Server); these are the defaults.\n5. `(EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d2) - EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d1)) * 12 + EXTRACT(MONTH FROM d2) - EXTRACT(MONTH FROM d1)` counts month boundaries, like `DATEDIFF(month, ...)` elsewhere.\n6. Counts **complete** months: Jan 31 → Feb 1 is 0, where `DATEDIFF(month, ...)` in Snowflake/SQL Server gives 1.\n\n**Argument order is the trap.** `DATEDIFF(d2, d1)` in MySQL vs `DATEDIFF(day, d1, d2)` in Snowflake and SQL Server vs `DATE_DIFF(d2, d1, DAY)` in BigQuery. Say it out loud as you type: \"unit, from, to\".\n\n## Everything else\n\n| Task | PostgreSQL | MySQL | Snowflake | BigQuery | SQL Server |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| First n rows | `LIMIT n` | `LIMIT n` | `LIMIT n` | `LIMIT n` | `SELECT TOP n ...` |\n| `7 / 2` (integers) | **3** | 3.5000 | 3.5 | 3.5 | **3** |\n| String concat | `a \\|\\| b` | `CONCAT(a, b)` | `a \\|\\| b` | `CONCAT(a, b)` or `\\|\\|` | `a + b` or `CONCAT(a, b)` |\n| Case-insensitive match | `ILIKE` | `LIKE` (case-insensitive by default) | `ILIKE` | `LOWER(x) LIKE ...` | `LIKE` (case-insensitive by default) |\n| Filter on a window | CTE | CTE | `QUALIFY` | `QUALIFY` | CTE |\n| NULLs in `ORDER BY x` | last | first | last | first | first |\n| `GROUP BY 1` | yes | yes | yes | yes | **no** |\n| `FULL OUTER JOIN` | yes | **no** (UNION a LEFT and a RIGHT) | yes | yes | yes |\n| Cast that returns NULL on failure | none built in | none built in | `TRY_CAST` | `SAFE_CAST` | `TRY_CAST` |\n| Quote an identifier | `\"col\"` | `` `col` `` | `\"col\"` | `` `col` `` | `[col]` |\n| Boolean in `SELECT` | `x > 1 AS flag` | returns 1/0 | `x > 1 AS flag` | `x > 1 AS flag` | **not allowed**: use `CASE` |\n\n## The portable subset\n\nIf you don't know the dialect, this works nearly everywhere:\n\n- `CASE WHEN`, `COALESCE`, `NULLIF`, `CAST(x AS ...)`\n- `COUNT`/`SUM`/`AVG` with `CASE` inside, rather than `FILTER` or boolean sums\n- `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...)` in a CTE, then `WHERE rn = 1`, rather than `QUALIFY`\n- `JOIN ... ON`, rather than `USING`\n- `1.0 * a / b` for rates\n- `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)`, `EXTRACT(MONTH FROM d)`\n- Half-open date ranges with `>=` and `<`"}]}, {"id": "04-pitfalls", "title": "Pitfalls checklist", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "Read this the night before. Each item links back to the lesson that covers it.\n\n## Before you write\n\n- [ ] **What's one row of the answer?** Say the grain out loud. ([lesson 0](#/lesson/00-how-sql-thinks))\n- [ ] **Which rows count?** Status filters, successful payments only, open loans only, and the date range: originated, applied or paid?\n- [ ] **Count or dollars? Loans, accounts or people?**\n- [ ] **Ties?** Exactly N rows, or all tied rows?\n- [ ] **Is the key really unique?** `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(DISTINCT id)`.\n\n## While you write\n\n**NULL** ([lesson 1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null), [lesson 2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain))\n\n- [ ] `= NULL` never matches. Use `IS NULL`.\n- [ ] `<>`, `NOT` and `NOT IN` silently drop NULL rows.\n- [ ] `AVG`, `SUM` and `COUNT(col)` skip NULLs. Is that what you want?\n- [ ] `NOT IN (subquery)` returns nothing if the subquery has a NULL. Use `NOT EXISTS`.\n- [ ] NULL keys never join (unlike dplyr).\n\n**Joins** ([lesson 3](#/lesson/03-joins), [lesson 4](#/lesson/04-fan-out-and-anti-joins))\n\n- [ ] Joining to a \"many\" table repeats the \"one\" side. Sums of the one side are inflated: **aggregate first, then join**.\n- [ ] Composite keys need **every** column in `ON`.\n- [ ] A `WHERE` on a LEFT-joined table's column turns it into an INNER JOIN. Put it in `ON`.\n- [ ] After a LEFT JOIN, `COUNT(right.id)`, not `COUNT(*)`.\n- [ ] Reaching for `DISTINCT` to fix a join? You probably wanted `EXISTS`.\n\n**Aggregation** ([lesson 2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain))\n\n- [ ] Every selected column is grouped or aggregated.\n- [ ] Rates: `1.0 *` to avoid integer division, `NULLIF(denominator, 0)` to avoid divide-by-zero.\n- [ ] `WHERE` filters rows before grouping; `HAVING` filters groups after.\n\n**Windows** ([lesson 6](#/lesson/06-window-functions-ranking), [lesson 7](#/lesson/07-window-functions-sequences))\n\n- [ ] Can't filter a window in `WHERE`: use a CTE (or `QUALIFY` where supported).\n- [ ] Windows see only rows that passed `WHERE`. Filter *after* the window when the window needs all rows.\n- [ ] Add a tie-breaker to every window `ORDER BY`.\n- [ ] Running totals with tied order values jump together (default `RANGE` frame).\n- [ ] `LAST_VALUE` needs an explicit full frame.\n\n**Dates** ([lesson 8](#/lesson/08-dates-and-time))\n\n- [ ] Timestamps vs dates: use `>= start AND < next_start`, never `BETWEEN` on a timestamp.\n- [ ] `EXTRACT(MONTH ...)` merges years; use `DATE_TRUNC` for time series.\n- [ ] Months with no data are missing rows, not zeros: join to a calendar.\n- [ ] Future dates (schedules) aren't \"missed\" yet.\n- [ ] Recent cohorts haven't had time to go bad: compare at equal age. ([lesson 9](#/lesson/09-lending-metrics))\n\n**Data quality in this dataset**\n\n- [ ] `payments`: duplicate rows, failed attempts, partial payments.\n- [ ] `borrowers`: one person can have two accounts.\n- [ ] `merchants.category`: inconsistent case and spaces, some NULLs.\n- [ ] `term_months`: NULL for Pay in 4.\n\n## Before you answer\n\n- [ ] Does a total reconcile with the base table?\n- [ ] Spot-check one ID by hand.\n- [ ] Rates between 0 and 1? Groups add up to the whole?\n- [ ] Columns, names, sort order and rounding exactly as asked.\n- [ ] State your assumptions and one caveat."}]}, {"id": "05-coverage", "title": "Coverage checklist", "blocks": [{"type": "md", "text": "Everything an analyst SQL screen is likely to test, and where this course teaches (**L**) and drills (**D**) it. Problem-bank questions (**P**) combine several skills at once. Use it to spot gaps: if a row feels shaky, do its drills again from a blank editor.\n\n| Skill | Taught | Practised |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Query basics** | | |\n| Clause order vs. run order; grain | [L0](#/lesson/00-how-sql-thinks) | throughout |\n| Calculated columns, aliases, `ROUND`, `CAST` | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: SELECT & functions](#/drill/00-select-and-functions) |\n| String functions: `LOWER`, `TRIM`, `SUBSTRING`, `POSITION`, `LENGTH`, `\\|\\|` / `CONCAT` | [D: SELECT & functions](#/drill/00-select-and-functions) | P: t2-01, t2-07 |\n| `ORDER BY` (expressions, `CASE`, tie-breakers, NULL placement), `LIMIT`/`OFFSET` | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: SELECT & functions](#/drill/00-select-and-functions), P: t1-02 |\n| `DISTINCT` | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: SELECT & functions](#/drill/00-select-and-functions) |\n| **Filtering** | | |\n| `=`, `<>`, `IN`, `BETWEEN`, `LIKE`; `AND`/`OR` precedence | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: WHERE & NULL](#/drill/01-filtering-and-null) |\n| NULL: three-valued logic, `IS NULL`, `<>`/`NOT`/`NOT IN` traps | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: WHERE & NULL](#/drill/01-filtering-and-null) |\n| `CASE WHEN` (including NULL branches) | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null) | [D: WHERE & NULL](#/drill/01-filtering-and-null), P: t1-06 |\n| `COALESCE`, `NULLIF` | [L1](#/lesson/01-filtering-and-null), [L2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain) | [D: WHERE & NULL](#/drill/01-filtering-and-null), [D: aggregation](#/drill/02-aggregation) |\n| **Aggregation** | | |\n| `COUNT(*)` / `COUNT(col)` / `COUNT(DISTINCT)`; aggregates skip NULLs | [L2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain) | [D: aggregation](#/drill/02-aggregation) |\n| `GROUP BY` (several columns, expressions), `HAVING` | [L2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain) | [D: aggregation](#/drill/02-aggregation), P: t1-07 |\n| Conditional aggregation, pivots, rates; integer division | [L2](#/lesson/02-aggregation-and-grain) | [D: aggregation](#/drill/02-aggregation), [D: patterns](#/drill/07-classic-patterns), P: t1-08, t2-02 |\n| Median / percentiles | [D: aggregation](#/drill/02-aggregation) | |\n| **Joins** | | |\n| `INNER` / `LEFT` join syntax, aliases, multi-table | [L3](#/lesson/03-joins) | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins), P: t2-03 |\n| `ON` vs `WHERE` with LEFT JOIN; counting after LEFT JOIN | [L3](#/lesson/03-joins) | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins) |\n| Composite keys; NULL keys | [L3](#/lesson/03-joins) | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins), P: t2-10 |\n| Fan-out; aggregate-then-join | [L4](#/lesson/04-fan-out-and-anti-joins) | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins), P: t2-06 |\n| Semi / anti joins (`EXISTS`, `NOT EXISTS`, `LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL`) | [L4](#/lesson/04-fan-out-and-anti-joins) | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins), P: t2-04 |\n| Self joins, range (non-equi) joins, `CROSS JOIN`, `FULL OUTER JOIN` | [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins) | |\n| **Query structure** | | |\n| CTEs; subqueries (scalar, `IN`, correlated, in `FROM`) | [L5](#/lesson/05-ctes-and-subqueries) | [D: subqueries](#/drill/04-subqueries-and-sets), P: t2-05, t2-08 |\n| `UNION ALL` / `UNION` / `INTERSECT` / `EXCEPT` | [L5](#/lesson/05-ctes-and-subqueries) | [D: subqueries](#/drill/04-subqueries-and-sets) |\n| **Window functions** | | |\n| `PARTITION BY` / `ORDER BY`; windows vs `GROUP BY`; run order | [L6](#/lesson/06-window-functions-ranking) | [D: windows](#/drill/05-window-functions) |\n| `ROW_NUMBER` / `RANK` / `DENSE_RANK`; top-N; dedup; first/latest | [L6](#/lesson/06-window-functions-ranking) | [D: windows](#/drill/05-window-functions), P: t3-01, t3-05 |\n| `LAG` / `LEAD`; running totals; moving averages; frames | [L7](#/lesson/07-window-functions-sequences) | [D: windows](#/drill/05-window-functions), P: t3-02, t3-03 |\n| `NTILE`, `FIRST_VALUE`, `LAST_VALUE`, share of group | [D: windows](#/drill/05-window-functions) | |\n| **Dates** | | |\n| DATE vs TIMESTAMP; half-open ranges | [L8](#/lesson/08-dates-and-time) | [D: dates](#/drill/06-dates) |\n| `EXTRACT`, `DATE_TRUNC` (month/quarter/week), intervals, date differences | [L8](#/lesson/08-dates-and-time) | [D: dates](#/drill/06-dates), P: t3-06, t3-08 |\n| Month ends, months-between, calendars for missing periods | [L8](#/lesson/08-dates-and-time) | [D: dates](#/drill/06-dates), [D: joins](#/drill/03-joins) |\n| **Patterns** | | |\n| Nth event, A-then-B, retention cohorts, streaks (gaps & islands), consecutive events, YoY, histograms, Pareto | [D: patterns](#/drill/07-classic-patterns) | P: t3-04, t3-09, t4-06 |\n| Lending metrics: approval, DPD, vintages, FPD, loss rates | [L9](#/lesson/09-lending-metrics) | P: Tier 4 |\n| **Interview craft** | | |\n| Clarify, plan the grain, build in steps, verify, explain | [L10](#/lesson/10-interview-craft) | [Mock screens](#/mock) |\n| Dialect differences | [Dialect sheet](#/ref/03-dialects) | |"}]}]};
